setting up Macaulay2
********************

Macaulay2 is available from our web page Macaulay2.com (see
https://Macaulay2.com ). There you will find installation instructions for
MacOSX and several Linux distributions, as well as precompiled versions for
various other systems.

For developers, detailed information about building Macaulay2 from source is
available on our GitHub repository at https://github.com/Macaulay2/M2/wiki.

Once Macaulay2 is installed on your system, use the information here to set up
the Emacs interface (the recommended way to run Macaulay2), bookmark the html
documentation, and start your first Macaulay2 session.

Checking your installation
==========================

At a command prompt in a terminal window, type "M2". If Macaulay2 has been
installed correctly on your machine, you should see a prompt such as this:

+--------------------------+
|$ M2                      |
|Macaulay2, version 1.26.05|
|with packages: ...        |
|                          |
|i1 :                      |
+--------------------------+

If this is the first time Macaulay2 runs on this machine, it creates an *note
application directory: applicationDirectory, under .Macaulay2 in your home
directory on Unix-based systems, or under 'Library/Application
Support/Macaulay2' under MacOS-based systems. Inside this directory there are
several files of interest, including:

  * an index of your local documentation in index.html
  * your *note initialization file: initialization file, init.m2
  * your history file history.m2

If instead you see an error, or if the version printed is too older than the
version above, then see the following pages for help and come back to this
page.

Debugging issues with your installation

  * *note finding the Macaulay2 files: finding the Macaulay2 files,
  * *note teaching your shell how to find M2: teaching your shell how to find
    M2,
  * *note teaching M2 how to find its shared libraries: teaching M2 how to find
    its shared libraries,
  * *note getting help or reporting bugs: getting help or reporting bugs,

Bookmarking the documentation
=============================

Macaulay2 stores links to a local copy of the Macaulay2 documentation, as well
as any packages you have installed locally, in the file index.html. At the
Macaulay2 prompt type "viewHelp" and press "Enter" to open index.html in your
default web browser. This is a good time to bookmark this page in your browser.
Note that there are several different ways of *note reading the documentation:
reading the documentation,.

Interacting with Macaulay2
==========================

At this point you should try something simple in Macaulay2, such as

+---------------+
|printWidth = 60|
+---------------+
|R = QQ[a..d]   |
+---------------+
|(a+b+c+d)^4    |
+---------------+

Macaulay2 can be run in this way from the command-line, but it is generally
much more convenient to run Macaulay2 inside a program such as Emacs or
TeXmacs. This is because you can more easily view larger objects, use
tab-completion, cut and paste, search, save your session, and so on. There is a
nice major mode for running Macaulay2 inside Emacs.

Different ways of using Macaulay2

  * *note invoking the Macaulay2 program: invoking the Macaulay2 program,
  * *note using Macaulay2 with Emacs: using Macaulay2 with Emacs,
  * *note using Macaulay2 with TeXmacs: using Macaulay2 with TeXmacs,
  * *note using Macaulay2 in a terminal: using Macaulay2 in a terminal,

Regardless of how you use Macaulay2, you can start a new session by entering
*note restart: restart,, or terminate it by entering *note exit: exit,, *note
quit: quit,, *note end: end,, or your end of file character (which is "Ctrl-D"
under most systems). If Macaulay2 is reading and running a script, the end of
file will terminate the program.

See the Macaulay2 wiki (see https://github.com/Macaulay2/M2/wiki ) for links to
extensions for other popular editors that at least support syntax highlighting
for Macaulay2 files.

Once you are set up, it is time for *note a first Macaulay2 session: a first
Macaulay2 session,.



