reading the documentation
*************************

The documentation for Macaulay2 is available in several formats and can be
searched and viewed using the following functions:



Finding documentation nodes

  * *note about: about, -- search the documentation
  * *note apropos: apropos, -- symbols matching a pattern
  * *note headlines: headlines, -- display a table of documentation headlines

Accessing documentation nodes

  * *note help: help, -- view documentation nodes
  * *note viewHelp: viewHelp, -- view online documentation in a web browser
  * *note infoHelp: infoHelp, -- view documentation in Info format
  * *note ? Symbol: ? Symbol, -- view brief documentation of a symbol

Getting other information from documentation

  * *note examples: examples, -- list the examples in documentation

To begin, the *note about: about, method allows for searching all documentation
nodes whose title or content contains a given string:

+-----------------------------+
|i1 : about "Horrocks-Mumford"|
|                             |
|o1 = {}                      |
|                             |
|o1 : NumberedVerticalList    |
+-----------------------------+

The *note apropos: apropos, method is useful for finding all exported objects
whose symbol matches the given regular expression, and can be combined with the
*note headlines: headlines, method to display a table with each symbol and its
documentation headline:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : headlines apropos "(H|h)ilbert"                                         |
|                                                                             |
|o2 =  0.  "Macaulay2Doc :: Hilbert"                       --                 |
|      1.  "Polyhedra :: hilbertBasis"                     --                 |
|      2.  "Macaulay2Doc :: hilbertFunction"               --                 |
|      3.  "Macaulay2Doc :: hilbertPolynomial"             --                 |
|      4.  "Macaulay2Doc :: hilbertSeries"                 --                 |
|      5.  "Macaulay2Doc :: installHilbertFunction"        --                 |
|      6.  "Macaulay2Doc :: ProjectiveHilbertPolynomial"   --                 |
|      7.  "Macaulay2Doc :: projectiveHilbertPolynomial"   --                 |
|      8.  "Macaulay2Doc :: reduceHilbert"                 --                 |
|      9.  "Macaulay2Doc :: UseHilbertFunction"            --                 |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|an optional argument                                                         |
|computes the Hilbert basis of a Cone                                         |
|the Hilbert function                                                         |
|compute the Hilbert polynomial                                               |
|compute the Hilbert series                                                   |
|assemble degrees of a ring, module, or ideal into a polynomial               |
|the class of all Hilbert polynomials                                         |
|Hilbert polynomial of projective space                                       |
|reduce a Hilbert series expression                                           |
|an optional argument                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

While in Macaulay2, type *note help: help, to get the documentation on a topic
or function, or type *note viewHelp: viewHelp, to open ~/.Macaulay2/index.html,
which contains a list of all installed packages, in your default web browser.

For help on a specific topic, e.g., the Jacobian function, use "viewHelp
jacobian" or "viewHelp \"jacobian\"", or if you want the documentation for
Jacobian of an Ideal, use "viewHelp (jacobian, Ideal)" or "viewHelp
\"jacobian(Ideal)\"". Using *note help: help, instead of *note viewHelp:
viewHelp, results in the help text appearing in your Macaulay2 session.

A useful tip: within Emacs, if you place your cursor on one of the resulting
output lines that starts with a *, and press the return key, Macaulay2 will
display that documentation node.

The documentation for most functions comes with example code. You can obtain
the text of this example code using *note examples: examples,.

See also
========

  * *note writing documentation: writing documentation,
* Menu:

* initial help::                Welcome to Macaulay2
