infoHelp -- view documentation in Info format
*********************************************

  * Usage: 
        infoHelp
        infoHelp X
  * Inputs:
      * X, a *note thing: Thing,, a descriptor for a documentation node (see
        below for examples)
  * Consequences:
      * The given documentation page is displayed within a terminal window
        using the command info.
      * If you are running Macaulay2 in Emacs, then the page is opened in
        another window using Info mode.
      * If no argument is given to infoHelp, then the top node of the Macaulay2
        documentation is displayed.

Description
===========

Some example uses:

+-------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|infoHelp                       | -- open the top of the online documentation tree                          |
+-------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|infoHelp "Macaulay2"           | -- open the top of the online documentation tree                          |
+-------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|infoHelp "matrices"            | -- open an overview of matrices in Macaulay2                              |
+-------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|infoHelp X                     | -- open the documentation node whose key is X                             |
+-------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|infoHelp ideal                 | -- documentation for the function *note ideal: ideal, and ways to use it  |
+-------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|infoHelp(ideal, List)          | -- documentation for the method *note ideal(List): ideal_lpList_rp,       |
+-------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|infoHelp[gb, DegreeLimit]      | -- documentation for the optional argument *note gb(...,DegreeLimit=>...):|
|                               |gb,                                                                        |
+-------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|infoHelp(symbol **, Ring, Ring)| -- documentation for the method *note Ring ** Ring:                       |
|                               |tensor_lpMonoid_cmMonoid_rp,                                               |
+-------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+

While in the info program, there are many ways to navigate and search. Besides
the arrow keys to move around on the page, here is a list of the most useful
key strokes:

+-+----------------------------------------------------+
|?| -- information about all of the possible keystrokes|
+-+----------------------------------------------------+
|q| -- quit info, return to Macaulay2                  |
+-+----------------------------------------------------+
|n| -- go to the next documentation node               |
+-+----------------------------------------------------+
|p| -- go to the previous node                         |
+-+----------------------------------------------------+
|m| -- follow the menu link                            |
+-+----------------------------------------------------+
|r| -- follow a cross-reference                        |
+-+----------------------------------------------------+
|l| -- go to the last node visited                     |
+-+----------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

The *note help: help, command allows other possible arguments, such as help
methods ideal, but infoHelp requires that the argument refer to only one
documentation page.

Viewing Info files in Emacs
===========================

If you read the info form of the documentation in Emacs, we recommend
configuring the value of the Emacs variable Info-hide-note-references to hide
in order to prevent Emacs from inserting a superfluous See or see in front of
the hyperlinks. This is done automatically for you by running *note setup:
setup, or *note setupEmacs: setupEmacs,.

