The Macaulay2 language
**********************

The Macaulay2 language is a simple yet powerful interpreted language.  Every
object has a type, such as *note Type: Type,, *note ZZ: ZZ, (integer), *note
String: String,, *note HashTable: HashTable,, *note Ring: Ring,, *note Matrix:
Matrix,, *note Function: Function,, etc.  User defined variables may hold
values of any type.

The Macaulay2 language is based on expressions, which, generally speaking, are
used to create new objects from old ones. Other computer languages have things
called statements, but Macaulay2 does not. Other computer languages use newline
characters and even indentation to indicate separate and nesting of statements,
but Macaulay2 does not. Expressions in Macaulay2 include function calls,
control structures (e.g., *note for: for, and *note while: while, loops),
function definitions, and operator expressions.  Every expression returns an
object, although the basic object *note null: null, is often returned if the
expression is being used only to produce some side effect.

There is special syntax for creating and accessing strings, lists, sequences,
and hash tables.  These are the key data types underlying many new types.  The
Macaulay2 engine implements rings, ring elements, and matrices, as instances of
low-level types, and various high-level types, visible to the user, are based
on them.  Examples include *note Ring: Ring,, *note RingElement: RingElement,,
*note Matrix: Matrix,, *note Ideal: Ideal,, *note RingMap: RingMap,, *note
Module: Module,, and *note Complex: (Complexes)Complex,.

The user can create new types of higher level mathematical objects, whose types
form a hierarchy that facilitates the installation and use of appropriate
methods for computing with them.

