substring -- extract part of a string
*************************************

  * Usage: 
        substring(i, s)
        substring(i, n, s)
        substring(s, i, n)
        substring(s, i)
        substring((i, n), s)
  * Inputs:
      * i, an *note integer: ZZ,, starting index of substring
      * n, an *note integer: ZZ,, length of substring
      * s, a *note string: String,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note string: String,, 

Description
===========

The expressions substring(i, n, s), substring(s, i, n), and substring((i, n),
s) all return the substring that starts at index i and has n characters.

In other words, if $s=s_0s_1s_2\ldots s_k$, then the substring returned is $s_i
s_{i+1}\ldots s_{i+n-1}$.

Indices begin at zero, and a negative index is counted from the end of the
string. Requests for out-of-bound character positions are silently ignored.

+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : s = "I love computing Gröbner bases in Macaulay2.";|
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : substring(0, 5, s)                                 |
|                                                        |
|o2 = I lov                                              |
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : substring(s, -10, 5)                               |
|                                                        |
|o3 = Macau                                              |
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : substring(s, 100, 5)                               |
|                                                        |
|o4 =                                                    |
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : substring((3, 10), s)                              |
|                                                        |
|o5 = ove comput                                         |
+--------------------------------------------------------+

The expressions substring(i, s) and substring(s, i) return the substring that
starts at index i and continues to the end of the string.

In other words, if $s=s_0s_1s_2\ldots s_k$, then the substring returned is $s_i
s_{i+1}\ldots s_k$.



+----------------------------------------------+
|i6 : substring(3, s)                          |
|                                              |
|o6 = ove computing Gröbner bases in Macaulay2.|
+----------------------------------------------+
|i7 : substring(s, -10)                        |
|                                              |
|o7 = Macaulay2.                               |
+----------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note lines: lines, -- split a string into lines
  * *note separate: separate, -- split a string into substrings using a regular
    expression
  * *note strings and nets: strings and nets, -- an overview of strings and
    nets in Macaulay2
  * *note regular expressions: regular expressions, -- syntax for regular
    expressions
  * *note utf8substring: utf8substring, -- extract part of a utf8 string

Ways to use substring:
======================

