Net -- the class of all nets and strings
****************************************

Description
===========

A net is a generalization of a string that is designed to facilitate
two-dimensional printing on ascii terminals.  It consists of a rectangular
array of characters subdivided horizontally by an imaginary baseline.


Operations on nets also accept strings by interpreting a string as a rectangle
of height one with the baseline just below it.  In fact, the parent of *note
String: String, is *note Net: Net,.


Multiple nets per line can be sent to an output file with *note <<: <<, but
care must be taken to use *note endl: endl, to end lines, for nets with new
line characters embedded in them will be displayed in an unexpected way.


Warning: if so many characters are written to a file that an internal buffer is
filled before the line ends or first net is seen, then the buffer will be
flushed, and writing a net subsequently will produce an unexpected result.
* Menu:

* width::                       width of an object
* width(Net): width_lpNet_rp.   width of a net
* height(Net): height_lpNet_rp.  height of a net
* depth(Net): depth_lpNet_rp.   depth of a net
* length(Net): length_lpString_rp.  length of a string
* Net ^ ZZ::                    raise a net or string
* Net || Net::                  join nets or strings vertically
* Net | Net::                   join strings or nets

Types of net:
=============

  * *note String: String, -- the class of all strings

Functions and methods returning a net:
======================================

  * *note generateAssertions: generateAssertions, -- generate assert statements
    from experimental input
  * "horizontalJoin(BasicList)" -- see *note horizontalJoin: horizontalJoin, --
    join nets or strings horizontally
  * *note net: net, -- format for printing, as a net
  * *note Net ^ ZZ: Net ^ ZZ, -- raise a net or string
  * "String ^ ZZ" -- see *note Net ^ ZZ: Net ^ ZZ, -- raise a net or string
  * *note Net | Net: Net | Net, -- join strings or nets
  * *note Net || Net: Net || Net, -- join nets or strings vertically
  * *note peek: peek, -- examine contents of an object
  * *note peek': peek', -- examine contents of an object
  * "stack(BasicList)" -- see *note stack: stack, -- join nets or string
    vertically
  * *note String ^ Sequence: String ^ Sequence, -- vertically stacked copies of
    a string

Methods that use a net:
=======================

  * "# Net" -- see *note # List: # List, -- length or cardinality of a list,
    hash table, dictionary, set, or string
  * "Net #? ZZ" -- see *note #?: #?, -- check existence of value in a list,
    hash table, database, or string
  * "capture(Net)" -- see *note capture: capture, -- evaluate Macaulay2 code
    and capture the output
  * *note depth(Net): depth_lpNet_rp, -- depth of a net
  * *note height(Net): height_lpNet_rp, -- height of a net
  * "length(Net)" -- see *note length(String): length_lpString_rp, -- length of
    a string
  * "Net # ZZ" -- see *note List # ZZ: List # ZZ, -- get value from list, hash
    table, database, dictionary, or string
  * "NetFile << Net" -- see *note NetFile: NetFile, -- the class of all net
    files
  * "pad(Net,ZZ)" -- see *note pad: pad, -- pad a string or net with spaces
  * "pad(ZZ,Net)" -- see *note pad: pad, -- pad a string or net with spaces
  * "File << Net" -- see *note printing to a file: printing to a file, -- print
    to a file
  * "unstack(Net)" -- see *note unstack: unstack, -- list the rows of a net
  * *note width(Net): width_lpNet_rp, -- width of a net

For the programmer
==================

The object *note Net: Net, is a *note type: Type,, with ancestor class *note
Thing: Thing,.

