RingElement / RingElement -- fraction
*************************************

  * Operator: *note /: /,
  * Usage: 
        f/g
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring element: RingElement,
      * g, a *note ring element: RingElement,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring element: RingElement,, the fraction f/g

Description
===========

If either f or g is in a base ring of the other, then that one is promoted so
that both are elements in the same ring R.


The fraction will be an element of the fraction field, frac R, of R. If R is
already a field, then this means that the fraction will be an element of R.
+--------+
|i1 : 4/2|
|        |
|o1 = 2  |
|        |
|o1 : QQ |
+--------+
+---------------------------+
|i2 : R = GF(9,Variable=>a);|
+---------------------------+
|i3 : (a/a^3) * a^2 == 1    |
|                           |
|o3 = true                  |
+---------------------------+
+------------------------+
|i4 : S = ZZ[a,b]        |
|                        |
|o4 = S                  |
|                        |
|o4 : PolynomialRing     |
+------------------------+
|i5 : (a^6-b^6)/(a^9-b^9)|
|                        |
|          3    3        |
|         a  + b         |
|o5 = --------------     |
|      6    3 3    6     |
|     a  + a b  + b      |
|                        |
|o5 : frac S             |
+------------------------+
If the ring contains zero divisors, the fraction field is not defined.
Macaulay2 will not inform you of this right away.  However, if computation
finds a zero-divisor, an error message is generated.
+--------------------------+
|i6 : A = ZZ/101[a,b]/(a*b)|
|                          |
|o6 = A                    |
|                          |
|o6 : QuotientRing         |
+--------------------------+
|i7 : (a+b)/(a-b)          |
|                          |
|       b                  |
|o7 = - -                  |
|       b                  |
|                          |
|o7 : frac A               |
+--------------------------+
At this point, if one types a/b, then Macaulay2 would give an error saying that
a zero divisor was found in the denominator.

See also
========

  * *note //: //, -- a binary operator, usually used for quotient

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note RingElement / RingElement: RingElement / RingElement, -- fraction
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/operators/division.m2:201:0.

