List # ZZ -- get value from list, hash table, database, dictionary, or string
*****************************************************************************

  * Operator: *note #: #,
  * Usage: 
        x#i
  * Inputs:
      * x, a *note list: List,, a *note hash table: HashTable,, a *note
        database: Database,, or a *note string: String,,
      * i, an *note integer: ZZ,, an index or key

Description
===========

If x is a list, x#i returns the ith element of x. The entries of the list are
numbered starting with 0. If i is negative, then the entries are numbered
ending with -1. If i is out of range, an error is signaled.

+-------------------------+
|i1 : L = {a, b, c, b, a};|
+-------------------------+
|i2 : L#2                 |
|                         |
|o2 = c                   |
|                         |
|o2 : Symbol              |
+-------------------------+
|i3 : L#-2                |
|                         |
|o3 = b                   |
|                         |
|o3 : Symbol              |
+-------------------------+

If x is a hash table or database, x#i provides the value associated with the
key i.

+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : T = new HashTable from {a => 103, b => 89.4, c => 92};|
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : T#a                                                   |
|                                                           |
|o5 = 103                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : T#b                                                   |
|                                                           |
|o6 = 89.40000000000001                                     |
|                                                           |
|o6 : RR (of precision 53)                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+

If x is a string, x#i provides the ith character of x, if there is one.
Negative indices are counted backward from the end, as with lists. If i is out
of range, an error is thrown.

+----------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : s = "a perfectly adequate example of a string";|
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : s#2                                            |
|                                                    |
|o8 = p                                              |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : s#-2                                           |
|                                                    |
|o9 = n                                              |
+----------------------------------------------------+

Assignment to x#i can change x if x is mutable.

+--------------------------------------+
|i10 : V = new MutableHashTable from T;|
+--------------------------------------+
|i11 : V#a = 5;                        |
+--------------------------------------+
|i12 : V#d = 22.3;                     |
+--------------------------------------+
|i13 : peek V                          |
|                                      |
|o13 = MutableHashTable{a => 5   }     |
|                       b => 89.4      |
|                       c => 92        |
|                       d => 22.3      |
+--------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note #: #, -- length or access to elements
  * *note #?: #?, -- check existence of value in a list, hash table, database,
    or string
  * *note # BasicList: # List, -- length or cardinality of a list, hash table,
    dictionary, set, or string
  * *note VisibleList _ ZZ: VisibleList _us ZZ, -- get element from list
  * *note hash tables: hash tables,
  * *note lists and sequences: lists and sequences, -- a detailed overview of
    lists and sequences in Macaulay2

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * "BasicList # ZZ"
  * "Database # String"
  * "Dictionary # String"
  * "HashTable # Thing"
  * *note List # ZZ: List # ZZ, -- get value from list, hash table, database,
    dictionary, or string
  * "Net # ZZ"
  * "Sequence # ZZ"
  * "String # ZZ"
