scan -- apply a function to each element in a list or sequence
**************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        scan(L, f)
        scan(L, L', f)
        scan(n, f)
  * Inputs:
      * L, a *note basic list: BasicList,, or instance of a class with the
        *note iterator: iterator, method installed
      * n, an *note integer: ZZ,, 
      * f, a *note function: Function,, 

Description
===========

scan(L, f) applies the function f to each element of the list L. The function
values are discarded.

+-----------------------------------------+
|i1 : scan({a, 4, "George", 2^100}, print)|
|a                                        |
|4                                        |
|George                                   |
|1267650600228229401496703205376          |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i2 : scan("foo", print)                  |
|f                                        |
|o                                        |
|o                                        |
+-----------------------------------------+

scan(n, f) applies the function f to each element of the list 0, 1, ..., n-1

+---------------------------------------------+
|i3 : scan(4, print)                          |
|0                                            |
|1                                            |
|2                                            |
|3                                            |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i4 : v = {a,b,c}; scan(#v, i -> print(i,v#i))|
|(0, a)                                       |
|(1, b)                                       |
|(2, c)                                       |
+---------------------------------------------+

The keyword *note break: break, can be used to terminate the scan prematurely,
and optionally to specify a return value for the expression. Here we use it to
locate the first even number in a list.

+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : scan({3,5,7,11,44,55,77}, i -> if even i then break i)|
|                                                           |
|o6 = 44                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+

If L is an instance of a class with the *note iterator: iterator, method
installed (e.g., a string), then f is applied to the values obtained by
repeatedly calling *note next: next, on the output of iterator L until *note
StopIteration: StopIteration, is returned.

+-----------------------+
|i7 : scan("foo", print)|
|f                      |
|o                      |
|o                      |
+-----------------------+

See also
========

  * *note apply: apply, -- apply a function to each element
  * *note accumulate: accumulate, -- apply a binary operator repeatedly
  * *note fold: fold, -- apply a binary operator repeatedly
  * *note lists and sequences: lists and sequences, -- a detailed overview of
    lists and sequences in Macaulay2

Ways to use scan:
=================

  * "scan(BasicList,BasicList,Function)"
  * "scan(BasicList,Function)"
  * "scan(Thing,Function)"
  * "scan(ZZ,Function)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note scan: scan, is a *note compiled function: CompiledFunction,.

