Iterator -- class for iterators
*******************************

Description
===========

This is a class designed to simplify writing *note iterator: iterator, methods.
Each instance is a nullary *note FunctionClosure: FunctionClosure, that serves
as the *note next: next, method for the iterator.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : iter = iterator {1, 2, 3}                                               |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = iter                                                                    |
|                                                                             |
|o1 : Iterator                                                                |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : code iter                                                               |
|                                                                             |
|o2 = ../../../../../Macaulay2/m2/iterators.m2:22:4-27:15: --source code:     |
|         () -> (                                                             |
|             if i >= #x then StopIteration                                   |
|             else (                                                          |
|                 r := x#i;                                                   |
|                 i += 1;                                                     |
|                 r)))                                                        |
|     | symbol  class  value      location of symbol                          |
|     | ------  -----  -----      ------------------                          |
|     | i       ZZ     0          ../../../../../Macaulay2/m2/iterators.m2:21:|
|     | x       List   {1, 2, 3}  ../../../../../Macaulay2/m2/iterators.m2:20:|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|                                                                             |
|4-21:5                                                                       |
|24-20:25                                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Each call of next iter is equivalent to iter().

+------------------+
|i3 : next iter    |
|                  |
|o3 = 1            |
+------------------+
|i4 : iter()       |
|                  |
|o4 = 2            |
+------------------+
|i5 : next iter    |
|                  |
|o5 = 3            |
+------------------+
|i6 : iter()       |
|                  |
|o6 = StopIteration|
|                  |
|o6 : Symbol       |
+------------------+

Every Iterator object is an iterator for itself.

+----------------------------------+
|i7 : primes = Iterator (          |
|         p := 2;                  |
|         () -> (                  |
|             r := p;              |
|             p = nextPrime(p + 1);|
|             r));                 |
+----------------------------------+
|i8 : iterator primes === primes   |
|                                  |
|o8 = true                         |
+----------------------------------+

However, we cannot "rewind" an Iterator object.  Every time that it is iterated
upon using *note for: for,, *note scan: scan,, etc., iteration will resume
where it left off previously.

+------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : for p in primes list if p > 20 then break else p |
|                                                      |
|o9 = {2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19}                     |
|                                                      |
|o9 : List                                             |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : for p in primes list if p > 20 then break else p|
|                                                      |
|o10 = {}                                              |
|                                                      |
|o10 : List                                            |
+------------------------------------------------------+

Contrast this with most other classes with the *note iterator: iterator, method
installed, like, strings, for which a new Iterator object is created every time
it is iterated upon, and so iteration starts over from the beginning

+---------------------------------------------+
|i11 : s = "Hello, world!"                    |
|                                             |
|o11 = Hello, world!                          |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i12 : for c in s list c                      |
|                                             |
|o12 = {H, e, l, l, o, ,,  , w, o, r, l, d, !}|
|                                             |
|o12 : List                                   |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i13 : for c in s list c                      |
|                                             |
|o13 = {H, e, l, l, o, ,,  , w, o, r, l, d, !}|
|                                             |
|o13 : List                                   |
+---------------------------------------------+
* Menu:

* iterator::                    get an iterator
* next::                        get the next object from an iterator
* StopIteration::               stop iteration

Functions and methods returning an iterator:
============================================

  * "accumulate(Function,Thing)" -- see *note accumulate: accumulate, -- apply
    a binary operator repeatedly
  * "accumulate(Function,Thing,Thing)" -- see *note accumulate: accumulate, --
    apply a binary operator repeatedly
  * *note apply(Thing,Function): apply_lpThing_cmFunction_rp, -- apply a
    function to an object with an iterator
  * "applyPairs(Thing,Function)" -- see *note applyPairs: applyPairs, -- apply
    a function to each pair in a hash table
  * "pairs(Thing)" -- see *note pairs: pairs, -- list the pairs in a hash
    table, dictionary, or basic list
  * *note select(Thing,Function): select_lpThing_cmFunction_rp, -- select
    elements from an object with an iterator

Methods that use an iterator:
=============================

  * "iterator(Iterator)" -- see *note iterator: iterator, -- get an iterator
  * Iterator | Iterator (missing documentation)
  * "next(Iterator)" -- see *note next: next, -- get the next object from an
    iterator

For the programmer
==================

The object *note Iterator: Iterator, is a *note self initializing type:
SelfInitializingType,, with ancestor classes *note FunctionClosure:
FunctionClosure, < *note Function: Function, < *note Thing: Thing,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

