same -- whether everything in a list is the same
************************************************

  * Usage: 
        same L
  * Inputs:
      * L, a list
  * Outputs:
      * b, a Boolean

Description
===========

+----------------------+
|i1 : same {1, 1, 1, 1}|
|                      |
|o1 = true             |
+----------------------+
|i2 : same {1, 2, 1, 1}|
|                      |
|o2 = false            |
+----------------------+

The comparison is done with "===", which is quick, but not always intuitive.
Here is a simple example of what can go wrong:

+---------------------------+
|i3 : R = QQ[x,y,z];        |
+---------------------------+
|i4 : L = {gcd{x,y}, x/x, 1}|
|                           |
|o4 = {1, 1, 1}             |
|                           |
|o4 : List                  |
+---------------------------+
|i5 : same L                |
|                           |
|o5 = false                 |
+---------------------------+

We can see the problem by asking Macaulay2 to display the class of each element
of L. (Or use the function *note uniform: uniform,, which returns whether or
not the elements of a list are all of the same class.)

+--------------------+
|i6 : apply(L, class)|
|                    |
|o6 = {R, frac R, ZZ}|
|                    |
|o6 : List           |
+--------------------+
|i7 : uniform L      |
|                    |
|o7 = false          |
+--------------------+

The first 1 is an element of the ring R, the second 1 is an element of the
fraction field of R, and the third 1 is an integer. Thus Macaulay2 thinks of
these three elements as being pairwise unequal, with respect to the operator
"===".

