position -- the first element of a list satisfying a condition
**************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        position(A, f)
        position(A, B, f)
        position(A, f, Reverse => true)
        position(n, f)
  * Inputs:
      * A, a *note visible list: VisibleList,, 
      * B, a *note visible list: VisibleList,, 
      * n, an *note integer: ZZ,, 
      * f, a *note function: Function,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Reverse => ..., default value false
  * Outputs:
      * p, an *note integer: ZZ,, the first index to satisfy the boolean
        function f

Description
===========

position(A, f) returns the smallest index i such that f(A#i) is true. If no
element satisfies the condition, *note null: null, is returned.

+--------------------------------------------+
|i1 : position((10,20,43,105,6,93), odd)     |
|                                            |
|o1 = 2                                      |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i2 : position((10,20,43,105,6,93), i -> i<0)|
+--------------------------------------------+

Use position(A, B, f) to return the smallest index i such that f(A#i, B#i) is
true.

+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : position((10,20,43,105,6,93),(18,82,12,7,35,92), (a,b) -> a>b)|
|                                                                   |
|o3 = 2                                                             |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+

The Reverse option will return the largest index instead.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : position((10,20,43,105,6,93), odd, Reverse => true)                     |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = 5                                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : position((10,20,43,105,6,93),(18,82,12,7,35,92), (a,b) -> a>b, Reverse  |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = 5                                                                       |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|=> true)                                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Use position(n, f) to return the smallest index i such that f(i) is true.

+--------------------------------------+
|i6 : position(17, i -> i^2 % 17 == 13)|
|                                      |
|o6 = 8                                |
+--------------------------------------+

To find all indices of elements satisfying the condition, see *note positions:
positions,. To return the elements, rather than their indices, see *note
select: select,. The function *note number: number, counts the number of
elements satisfying the condition.

+----------------------------------------+
|i7 : positions((10,20,43,105,6,93), odd)|
|                                        |
|o7 = {2, 3, 5}                          |
|                                        |
|o7 : List                               |
+----------------------------------------+
|i8 : select((10,20,43,105,6,93), odd)   |
|                                        |
|o8 = (43, 105, 93)                      |
|                                        |
|o8 : Sequence                           |
+----------------------------------------+
|i9 : number((10,20,43,105,6,93), odd)   |
|                                        |
|o9 = 3                                  |
+----------------------------------------+

