File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: sublists, Next: table, Prev: partition, Up: lists and sequences

sublists -- process interspersed subsequences of a visible list
***************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        sublists(L, f, g, h)
  * Inputs:
      * L, a *note visible list: VisibleList,, 
      * f, a *note function: Function,, returning Boolean values
      * g, a *note function: Function,, which acts on VisibleLists, applied to
        sublists of L with f(i) true for each i in the sublist
      * h, a *note function: Function,, which acts on elements of L, applied to
        the elements i of L with f(i) false
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: List,, the result obtained by applying g to each of the
        maximal nonempty sequences of consecutive elements with f true, and
        applying h to the other elements

Description
===========

In the first example, consecutive odd elements are grouped into sublists, while
each even element is negated.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : L = {1,2,3,5,7,8,10,12,13,17,18,20,21};                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : sublists(L, odd, toList, minus)                             |
|                                                                 |
|o2 = {{1}, -2, {3, 5, 7}, -8, -10, -12, {13, 17}, -18, -20, {21}}|
|                                                                 |
|o2 : List                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+

If g or h is omitted, the *note identity: identity, function is used in its
place.

+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : sublists(L, odd, toList)                              |
|                                                           |
|o3 = {{1}, 2, {3, 5, 7}, 8, 10, 12, {13, 17}, 18, 20, {21}}|
|                                                           |
|o3 : List                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : sublists(L, odd)                                      |
|                                                           |
|o4 = {{1}, 2, {3, 5, 7}, 8, 10, 12, {13, 17}, 18, 20, {21}}|
|                                                           |
|o4 : List                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+

The sublists will belong to the same class as L.

+--------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : L = (1,2,3,5,7,8,10,12,13,17,18,20,21);      |
+--------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : sublists(L, isPrime, , e -> 0)               |
|                                                  |
|o6 = {0, (2, 3, 5, 7), 0, 0, 0, (13, 17), 0, 0, 0}|
|                                                  |
|o6 : List                                         |
+--------------------------------------------------+

Note that g acts on the sublists, not their elements.

+---------------------------------------+
|i7 : sublists(L, isPrime, plus, e -> 0)|
|                                       |
|o7 = {0, 17, 0, 0, 0, 30, 0, 0, 0}     |
|                                       |
|o7 : List                              |
+---------------------------------------+

Because of the grouping of consecutive elements that return true when input to
f, sublists(L, f, g, h) is NOT the same as applying g to elements returning
true, and applying h to elements returning false. This could be achieved with a
simple *note if: if,-then-else loop, or with *note apply: apply,.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : a = for l in L list if isPrime l then l else -10*l          |
|                                                                 |
|o8 = {-10, 2, 3, 5, 7, -80, -100, -120, 13, 17, -180, -200, -210}|
|                                                                 |
|o8 : List                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : b = apply(L, l -> if isPrime l then l else -10*l)           |
|                                                                 |
|o9 = (-10, 2, 3, 5, 7, -80, -100, -120, 13, 17, -180, -200, -210)|
|                                                                 |
|o9 : Sequence                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+

On the other hand, if we want to group both "true" and "false" elements into
sublists, we can achieve this with a second call to sublists, selecting those
elements not already grouped by the first sublists command, as long as the
original list was not nested.

+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : X = {1, 3, 5, 2, 4, 7, 1, 3, 4, 4, 5, 4, 7, 9, 13};         |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : sublists(sublists(X, odd), i -> not instance(i, List))      |
|                                                                  |
|o11 = {{1, 3, 5}, {2, 4}, {7, 1, 3}, {4, 4}, {5}, {4}, {7, 9, 13}}|
|                                                                  |
|o11 : List                                                        |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note apply: apply, -- apply a function to each element
  * *note partition: partition, -- partition a set or list by values of a
    function
  * *note positions: positions, -- which elements of a list satisfy a condition
  * *note select: select, -- select from a list, hash table, or string
  * *note lists and sequences: lists and sequences, -- a detailed overview of
    lists and sequences in Macaulay2

Ways to use sublists:
=====================

  * "sublists(VisibleList,Function)"
  * "sublists(VisibleList,Function,Function)"
  * "sublists(VisibleList,Function,Function,Function)"
  * "sublists(VisibleList,Function,Function,Nothing)"
  * "sublists(VisibleList,Function,Nothing)"
  * "sublists(VisibleList,Function,Nothing,Function)"
