sort(List) -- sort a list
*************************

  * Function: *note sort: sort,
  * Usage: 
        sort v
  * Inputs:
      * v, a *note list: List,
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note DegreeOrder: sort_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeOrder=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value null, specify Ascending, Descending, or null
      * *note MonomialOrder: sort_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmMonomialOrder=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value Ascending, specify Ascending or Descending monomial
        order
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: List,,  of the same length as v sorted in ascending order

Description
===========

The sort function uses *note <=: <=, to compare elements of the list, which in
turn calls upon the comparison operator *note ?: ?,, so, to determine how the
elements are sorted, refer to its documentation.  Methods for new user defined
types of objects can be installed for it, too, to obtain the desired sorting
behavior.

+-----------------------+
|i1 : sort {c,e,a,f,b,f}|
|                       |
|o1 = {a, b, c, e, f, f}|
|                       |
|o1 : List              |
+-----------------------+
|i2 : sort {4,2,6,3,8,2}|
|                       |
|o2 = {2, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8}|
|                       |
|o2 : List              |
+-----------------------+

Comparison of strings is implemented so that symbols come before alphanumeric
characters, upper and lower case characters are sorted together, and strings of
digits are sorted by size of the implied number.

+------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : sort {"a11","a1","a2","A11","A1","A2","B2"}|
|                                                |
|o3 = {A1, a1, A2, a2, A11, a11, B2}             |
|                                                |
|o3 : List                                       |
+------------------------------------------------+

Visible lists (lists, sequences, and arrays) are compared lexicographically.

+---------------------------------------------+
|i4 : sort {(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,2),(3,2)}    |
|                                             |
|o4 = {(1, 1), (1, 2), (2, 1), (3, 1), (3, 2)}|
|                                             |
|o4 : List                                    |
+---------------------------------------------+

If the elements of the list are polynomials, then the monomial order is used to
sort the elements according to their lead terms.

+-------------------------------------+
|i5 : R = ZZ[a..d,MonomialOrder=>Lex];|
+-------------------------------------+
|i6 : sort{a*d, b^100, c^3*d}         |
|                                     |
|       3    100                      |
|o6 = {c d, b   , a*d}                |
|                                     |
|o6 : List                            |
+-------------------------------------+

The optional arguments are not used in this version of sort.

Caveat
======

If a list contains elements of different types, the sorting still occurs, but
might not be particularly intuitive.

See also
========

  * *note rsort: rsort, -- sort a list or matrix in reverse order
  * *note <=: <=, -- less than or equal
  * *note ?: ?, -- comparison operator

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note sort(List): sort_lpList_rp, -- sort a list
  * "sort(List,Function)"
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/functions/sort-doc.m2:101:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: sort_lpMatrix_rp, Next: sortColumns, Prev: sort_lpList_rp, Up: sort

sort(Matrix) -- sort the columns of a matrix
********************************************

  * Function: *note sort: sort,
  * Usage: 
        sort m
  * Inputs:
      * m, a *note matrix: Matrix,, between free modules
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note DegreeOrder: sort_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeOrder=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value null, specify Ascending, Descending, or null
      * *note MonomialOrder: sort_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmMonomialOrder=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value Ascending, specify Ascending or Descending monomial
        order
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: Matrix,, with the same shape as m whose columns are
        sorted in increasing order

Description
===========


The default order is to sort the columns in ascending degree first, and within
each degree, first compare ring elements in the highest non-zero component,
breaking ties by going to the next lower component. Zero is considered earlier
in the order than other elements
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/32003[a..d,MonomialOrder=>Lex];         |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : m = matrix{{a*d, b^2, b^100, b^50*d^50, c^2*d}}|
|                                                    |
|o2 = | ad b2 b100 b50d50 c2d |                      |
|                                                    |
|             1      5                               |
|o2 : Matrix R  <-- R                                |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : sort m                                         |
|                                                    |
|o3 = | c2d b2 b50d50 b100 ad |                      |
|                                                    |
|             1      5                               |
|o3 : Matrix R  <-- R                                |
+----------------------------------------------------+


The two optional arguments can modify this default order. The value of *note
DegreeOrder: DegreeOrder, is considered first (Ascending, Descending, or null),
and after that the monomial (or ring) order is used to break ties, either
ascending or descending, depending on the value of *note MonomialOrder:
MonomialOrder,.


To sort the columns of m in descending monomial order:
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : options sort                                         |
|                                                          |
|o4 = OptionTable{DegreeOrder => null       }              |
|                 MonomialOrder => Ascending               |
|                                                          |
|o4 : OptionTable                                          |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : sort(m, DegreeOrder=>null, MonomialOrder=>Descending)|
|                                                          |
|o5 = | ad b100 b50d50 b2 c2d |                            |
|                                                          |
|             1      5                                     |
|o5 : Matrix R  <-- R                                      |
+----------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note sortColumns: sortColumns, -- permutation giving sort order
* Menu:

* sort(...,MonomialOrder=>...): sort_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmMonomialOrder=>_pd_pd_pd_rp.  specify Ascending or Descending monomial order
* sort(...,DegreeOrder=>...): sort_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeOrder=>_pd_pd_pd_rp.  specify Ascending, Descending, or null
* Ascending::                   specify ascending order
* Descending::                  specify descending order

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note sort(Matrix): sort_lpMatrix_rp, -- sort the columns of a matrix
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/functions/sort-doc.m2:56:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: sort_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmMonomialOrder=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, Next: sort_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeOrder=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, Up: sort_lpMatrix_rp

sort(...,MonomialOrder=>...) -- specify Ascending or Descending monomial order
******************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        sort(...,MonomialOrder=>x)
  * Inputs:
      * x, a *note symbol: Symbol,, either *note Ascending: Ascending, or *note
        Descending: Descending,
