append -- add an element to the end of a list
*********************************************

  * Usage: 
        append(L, x)
  * Inputs:
      * L, a *note basic list: BasicList,, 
      * x, a *note thing: Thing,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note basic list: BasicList,, the list obtained by adding the element
        x to the end of list L

Description
===========

+-------------------------------+
|i1 : append({1, 7, 8, 3}, 3)   |
|                               |
|o1 = {1, 7, 8, 3, 3}           |
|                               |
|o1 : List                      |
+-------------------------------+
|i2 : L = {"old", "old", "old"};|
+-------------------------------+
|i3 : append(L, "new")          |
|                               |
|o3 = {old, old, old, new}      |
|                               |
|o3 : List                      |
+-------------------------------+

The new list will be of the same *note class: class, as L.

+-------------------+
|i4 : K = (a, b, c);|
+-------------------+
|i5 : append(K, z)  |
|                   |
|o5 = (a, b, c, z)  |
|                   |
|o5 : Sequence      |
+-------------------+

Only a single element can be appended with this function. To append the
elements of a list, use *note join: join,. To add the new element to the
beginning of the list, or at a particular index, use *note prepend: prepend, or
*note insert: insert,, respectively.

+-----------------------+
|i6 : join(K, (x, y, z))|
|                       |
|o6 = (a, b, c, x, y, z)|
|                       |
|o6 : Sequence          |
+-----------------------+
|i7 : prepend(z, K)     |
|                       |
|o7 = (z, a, b, c)      |
|                       |
|o7 : Sequence          |
+-----------------------+
|i8 : insert(1, z, K)   |
|                       |
|o8 = (a, z, b, c)      |
|                       |
|o8 : Sequence          |
+-----------------------+

Append always returns a new list, rather than modifying the input list, even if
L is a *note MutableList: MutableList,.

+--------------------------------------+
|i9 : L = new MutableList from {2,3,5};|
+--------------------------------------+
|i10 : peek append(L, 7)               |
|                                      |
|o10 = MutableList{2, 3, 5, 7}         |
+--------------------------------------+
|i11 : peek L                          |
|                                      |
|o11 = MutableList{2, 3, 5}            |
+--------------------------------------+

Notice that the order of the arguments is switched in prepend versus append: we
write prepend(x, L) and append(L, x). A good way to remember this is that the
new element is visually placed before or after the list, depending on where we
want it to appear in the output.

See also
========

  * *note insert: insert, -- copy a list, inserting an element
  * *note join: join, -- join lists, sequences, and iterable objects
  * *note prepend: prepend, -- add an element to the beginning of a list
  * *note lists and sequences: lists and sequences, -- a detailed overview of
    lists and sequences in Macaulay2

Ways to use append:
===================

  * "append(BasicList,Thing)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note append: append, is a *note compiled function:
CompiledFunction,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

