prepend -- add an element to the beginning of a list
****************************************************

  * Usage: 
        prepend(x, L)
  * Inputs:
      * L, a *note basic list: BasicList,, 
      * x, a *note thing: Thing,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note basic list: BasicList,, the list obtained by adding the element
        x to the beginning of list L

Description
===========

+-------------------------------+
|i1 : prepend(3, {1, 7, 8, 3})  |
|                               |
|o1 = {3, 1, 7, 8, 3}           |
|                               |
|o1 : List                      |
+-------------------------------+
|i2 : L = {"old", "old", "old"};|
+-------------------------------+
|i3 : prepend("new", L)         |
|                               |
|o3 = {new, old, old, old}      |
|                               |
|o3 : List                      |
+-------------------------------+

The new list will be of the same *note class: class, as L.

+-------------------+
|i4 : K = (a, b, c);|
+-------------------+
|i5 : prepend(z, K) |
|                   |
|o5 = (z, a, b, c)  |
|                   |
|o5 : Sequence      |
+-------------------+

Only a single element can be prepended with this function. To prepend the
elements of a list, use *note join: join,. To add the new element to the end of
the list, or at a particular index, use *note append: append, or *note insert:
insert,, respectively.

+-----------------------+
|i6 : join((x, y, z), K)|
|                       |
|o6 = (x, y, z, a, b, c)|
|                       |
|o6 : Sequence          |
+-----------------------+
|i7 : append(K, z)      |
|                       |
|o7 = (a, b, c, z)      |
|                       |
|o7 : Sequence          |
+-----------------------+
|i8 : insert(1, z, K)   |
|                       |
|o8 = (a, z, b, c)      |
|                       |
|o8 : Sequence          |
+-----------------------+

Prepend always returns a new list, rather than modifying the input list, even
if L is a *note MutableList: MutableList,.

+--------------------------------------+
|i9 : L = new MutableList from {2,3,5};|
+--------------------------------------+
|i10 : peek prepend(7, L)              |
|                                      |
|o10 = MutableList{7, 2, 3, 5}         |
+--------------------------------------+
|i11 : peek L                          |
|                                      |
|o11 = MutableList{2, 3, 5}            |
+--------------------------------------+

Notice that the order of the arguments is switched in prepend versus append: we
write prepend(x, L) and append(L, x). A good way to remember this is that the
new element is visually placed before or after the list, depending on where we
want it to appear in the output.

