toList -- create a list
***********************

  * Usage: 
        toList A
  * Inputs:
      * A, a *note set: Set,, a *note string: String,, a *note basic list:
        BasicList,, or a *note tally: Tally,, or an instance of a class with
        the *note iterator: iterator, method installed
  * Outputs:
      * L, a *note list: List,, a list whose elements are the elements of A

Description
===========

+---------------------+
|i1 : toList "foo"    |
|                     |
|o1 = {f, o, o}       |
|                     |
|o1 : List            |
+---------------------+
|i2 : A = set(3,7,9,6)|
|                     |
|o2 = set {9, 3, 6, 7}|
|                     |
|o2 : Set             |
+---------------------+
|i3 : toList A        |
|                     |
|o3 = {9, 3, 6, 7}    |
|                     |
|o3 : List            |
+---------------------+

The command toList 1..9 will throw an error, because toList comes before *note
..: _pd_pd, in Macaulay2's order of operations. To create a list from a range,
use toList (1..9) instead.

+--------------------------------+
|i4 : toList (1..9)              |
|                                |
|o4 = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9}|
|                                |
|o4 : List                       |
+--------------------------------+

Converting between list types may change the order of the elements in
unexpected ways.

+------------------------+
|i5 : toList set {4,5,13}|
|                        |
|o5 = {4, 13, 5}         |
|                        |
|o5 : List               |
+------------------------+

