flatten -- flatten a nested list or a matrix
********************************************

  * Usage: 
        flatten x
  * Inputs:
      * x, a *note list: List,, or *note Matrix: Matrix,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: List,, or matrix obtained by removing braces one level in

Description
===========

If x is a list, then flatten x effectively removes the braces surrounding any
elements of x that happen to be lists.

+-----------------------------------+
|i1 : flatten {{2,3,4}, {{5}, 6}, 7}|
|                                   |
|o1 = {2, 3, 4, {5}, 6, 7}          |
|                                   |
|o1 : List                          |
+-----------------------------------+

If x is a matrix, then flatten x puts the elements of x in a single row,
ordered by column. If x is an $m\times n$ matrix, then flatten x is a $1\times
mn$ matrix.

+-------------------------------------------+
|i2 : R = ZZ/101[a,b,c];                    |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i3 : m = matrix {{2, a},{b^2, 23},{c, c^3}}|
|                                           |
|o3 = | 2  a  |                             |
|     | b2 23 |                             |
|     | c  c3 |                             |
|                                           |
|             3      2                      |
|o3 : Matrix R  <-- R                       |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i4 : flatten m                             |
|                                           |
|o4 = | 2 b2 c a 23 c3 |                    |
|                                           |
|             1      6                      |
|o4 : Matrix R  <-- R                       |
+-------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

The matrix produced by flatten m is not the same as the matrix given by
unnesting the list used to define the matrix.

See also
========

  * *note deepSplice: deepSplice, -- remove all levels of subsequences from a
    sequence or list
  * *note splice: splice, -- remove subsequences from a sequence or list
  * *note lists and sequences: lists and sequences, -- a detailed overview of
    lists and sequences in Macaulay2

Ways to use flatten:
====================

  * "flatten(Matrix)"
  * "flatten(VisibleList)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note flatten: flatten, is a *note method function with a single
argument: MethodFunctionSingle,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

