File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: hash tables, Next: operators, Prev: lists and sequences, Up: The Macaulay2 language

hash tables
***********

A hash table is a data structure that can implement a function whose domain is
a finite set.  An element of the domain is called a key.  The hash table stores
the key-value pairs in such a way that when presented with a key, the
corresponding value can be quickly recovered.


A dictionary could be implemented as a hash table: the keys would be the words
in the language, and the values could be the definitions of the words.


A phone book could also be implemented as a hash table: the keys would be the
names of the subscribers, and the values could be the corresponding phone
numbers.  (We exclude the possibility of two subscribers with the same name.)


As an example we implement a phone book.
+-----------------------------------+
|i1 : book = new HashTable from {   |
|          "Joe" => "344-5567",     |
|          "Sarah" => "567-4223",   |
|          "John" => "322-1456"}    |
|                                   |
|o1 = HashTable{"Joe" => 344-5567  }|
|               "John" => 322-1456  |
|               "Sarah" => 567-4223 |
|                                   |
|o1 : HashTable                     |
+-----------------------------------+
We use the operator *note #: #, to obtain values from the phone book.
+-----------------+
|i2 : book#"Sarah"|
|                 |
|o2 = 567-4223    |
+-----------------+
The operator *note #?: #?, can be used to tell us whether a given key has an
entry in the hash table.
+-----------------+
|i3 : book#?"Mary"|
|                 |
|o3 = false       |
+-----------------+
We have implemented the notion of set via hash tables in which every value is
the number 1.
+------------------------+
|i4 : x = set {a,b,c,r,t}|
|                        |
|o4 = set {c, t, a, r, b}|
|                        |
|o4 : Set                |
+------------------------+
|i5 : peek x             |
|                        |
|o5 = Set{a => 1}        |
|         b => 1         |
|         c => 1         |
|         r => 1         |
|         t => 1         |
+------------------------+
|i6 : x#?a               |
|                        |
|o6 = true               |
+------------------------+
|i7 : x#?4               |
|                        |
|o7 = false              |
+------------------------+
There is a type of hash table that is mutable, i.e., a hash table whose entries
can be changed.  They are changed with assignment statements of the form
x#key=value.
+------------------------------+
|i8 : x = new MutableHashTable;|
+------------------------------+
|i9 : x#"Joe" = "344-5567";    |
+------------------------------+
|i10 : x#3 = {a,b,c};          |
+------------------------------+
|i11 : x#{1,2} = 44;           |
+------------------------------+
|i12 : x#3                     |
|                              |
|o12 = {a, b, c}               |
|                              |
|o12 : List                    |
+------------------------------+
|i13 : x#?4                    |
|                              |
|o13 = false                   |
+------------------------------+
When a mutable hash table is printed, its contents are not displayed. This
prevents infinite loops in printing routines.
+-------------------------------+
|i14 : x                        |
|                               |
|o14 = MutableHashTable{...3...}|
|                               |
|o14 : MutableHashTable         |
+-------------------------------+
Use *note peek: peek, to see the contents of a mutable hash table.
+---------------------------------------+
|i15 : peek x                           |
|                                       |
|o15 = MutableHashTable{{1, 2} => 44   }|
|                       3 => {a, b, c}  |
|                       Joe => 344-5567 |
+---------------------------------------+
A variant of *note #: #, is *note .: _pd,.  It takes only global symbols as
keys, and ignores their values.
+----------------+
|i16 : p=4;      |
+----------------+
|i17 : x.p = 444;|
+----------------+
|i18 : x.p       |
|                |
|o18 = 444       |
+----------------+
|i19 : x#?4      |
|                |
|o19 = false     |
+----------------+
* Menu:

* HashTable::                   the class of all hash tables
* MutableHashTable::            the class of all mutable hash tables
* hashing::
* hash::                        hash code of an object
* youngest::                    the youngest member of a sequence

constructing hash tables
* hashTable::                   make a hash table
* new HashTable from List::     make a hash table from a list

functions for manipulating hash tables
* isMutable::                   whether something may be modified
* keys::                        keys used in a hash table, dictionary, or database
* values::                      values in a hash table
* pairs::                       list the pairs in a hash table, dictionary, or basic list
* copy::                        copy an object
* remove::                      remove an entry from a mutable hash table, list, or database
* merge::                       merge two hash tables
* combine::                     combine hash tables
* override::                    override default values for optional arguments
* mapping over hash tables::    apply a function to each element of a hash table
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

