CacheTable -- hash tables for caching
*************************************

Description
===========

A cache table is a type of mutable hash table designed for caching computed
values that may be recalled later. Cache tables are designed so their contents
will not participate in any comparisons by the strict comparison operator ===.

+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : A = { 1, 2, new CacheTable from { 1 => 10,  2 => 20 }} |
|                                                            |
|o1 = {1, 2, CacheTable{...2...}}                            |
|                                                            |
|o1 : List                                                   |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : B = { 1, 2, new CacheTable from { 1 => 100, 2 => 200 }}|
|                                                            |
|o2 = {1, 2, CacheTable{...2...}}                            |
|                                                            |
|o2 : List                                                   |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : A === B                                                |
|                                                            |
|o3 = true                                                   |
+------------------------------------------------------------+

To that end, any two cache tables with the same class and parent are considered
equal to each other and have hash code equal to 0.

+-------------+
|i4 : hash A#2|
|             |
|o4 = 0       |
+-------------+
|i5 : hash B#2|
|             |
|o5 = 0       |
+-------------+

For the programmer
==================

The object *note CacheTable: CacheTable, is a *note type: Type,, with ancestor
classes *note MutableHashTable: MutableHashTable, < *note HashTable: HashTable,
< *note Thing: Thing,.

