remove -- remove an entry from a mutable hash table, list, or database
**********************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        remove(T, k)
  * Inputs:
      * T, a *note hash table: HashTable,, a *note mutable list: MutableList,,
        or a *note database: Database,,
      * k, the key to remove (must be an *note integer: ZZ, if T is a mutable
        list or a *note string: String, if it is a database)
  * Outputs:
      * a *note thing: Thing,, the removed value; 

Description
===========

remove(T, k) removes and returns the entry of T stored under the key k.

+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : T = new MutableHashTable from {a => 1, b => 2, c => 3}; peek T|
|                                                                   |
|o2 = MutableHashTable{a => 1}                                      |
|                      b => 2                                       |
|                      c => 3                                       |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : remove(T, a)                                                  |
|                                                                   |
|o3 = 1                                                             |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : peek T                                                        |
|                                                                   |
|o4 = MutableHashTable{b => 2}                                      |
|                      c => 3                                       |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+

If T is not a mutable hash table, an error is thrown. One way to remove an
entry from an immutable hash table is with the function *note applyPairs:
applyPairs,:

+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : T = new HashTable from {a => 1, b => 2, c => 3}    |
|                                                        |
|o5 = HashTable{a => 1}                                  |
|               b => 2                                   |
|               c => 3                                   |
|                                                        |
|o5 : HashTable                                          |
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : T = applyPairs(T, (k, v) -> if k =!= a then (k, v))|
|                                                        |
|o6 = HashTable{b => 2}                                  |
|               c => 3                                   |
|                                                        |
|o6 : HashTable                                          |
+--------------------------------------------------------+

remove works similarly when T is a database.  See *note Database: Database, for
more information.

If T is a mutable list, then k gives the index of the element to be removed.

+--------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : T = new MutableList from {1, 2, 3, 4}; peek T|
|                                                  |
|o8 = MutableList{1, 2, 3, 4}                      |
+--------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : remove(T, 0)                                 |
|                                                  |
|o9 = 1                                            |
+--------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : peek T                                      |
|                                                  |
|o10 = MutableList{2, 3, 4}                        |
+--------------------------------------------------+

If k is negative, then the index is determined by counting backwards from the
end of T.

+-----------------------+
|i11 : remove(T, -1)    |
|                       |
|o11 = 4                |
+-----------------------+
|i12 : peek T           |
|                       |
|o12 = MutableList{2, 3}|
+-----------------------+

