merge -- merge two hash tables
******************************

  * Usage: 
        z = merge(x, y, f)
  * Inputs:
      * x, a *note hash table: HashTable,, 
      * y, a *note hash table: HashTable,, 
      * f, a *note function: Function,, of two variables specifying how to
        handle duplicate keys
  * Outputs:
      * z, a *note hash table: HashTable,, a new hash table whose keys are the
        union of the keys of x and the keys of y

Description
===========

Key-value pairs whose keys appear in only one of the input tables appear
unchanged in the output table. If the key k appears in both x and y, then
f(x#k, y#k) is the value associated to k in the output hash table.

For example, we could take the max of the two values, their average, or make a
list containing both.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : x = new HashTable from {1 => 203, 2 => 21, 3 => 5, 4 => 130}|
|                                                                 |
|o1 = HashTable{1 => 203}                                         |
|               2 => 21                                           |
|               3 => 5                                            |
|               4 => 130                                          |
|                                                                 |
|o1 : HashTable                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : y = new HashTable from {2 => 37, 3 => 5, 4 => 56, 5 => 1}   |
|                                                                 |
|o2 = HashTable{2 => 37}                                          |
|               3 => 5                                            |
|               4 => 56                                           |
|               5 => 1                                            |
|                                                                 |
|o2 : HashTable                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : merge(x, y, max)                                            |
|                                                                 |
|o3 = HashTable{1 => 203}                                         |
|               2 => 37                                           |
|               3 => 5                                            |
|               4 => 130                                          |
|               5 => 1                                            |
|                                                                 |
|o3 : HashTable                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : merge(x, y, (i,j) -> (i+j)/2)                               |
|                                                                 |
|o4 = HashTable{1 => 203}                                         |
|               2 => 29                                           |
|               3 => 5                                            |
|               4 => 93                                           |
|               5 => 1                                            |
|                                                                 |
|o4 : HashTable                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : merge(x, y, (i,j) -> {i,j})                                 |
|                                                                 |
|o5 = HashTable{1 => 203      }                                   |
|               2 => {21, 37}                                     |
|               3 => {5, 5}                                       |
|               4 => {130, 56}                                    |
|               5 => 1                                            |
|                                                                 |
|o5 : HashTable                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+

If the function f(x#k, y#k) returns *note continue: continue,, then the key k
is omitted from the merged table.

+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : merge(x, y, (i,j) -> if i==j then i else continue)|
|                                                       |
|o6 = HashTable{1 => 203}                               |
|               3 => 5                                  |
|               5 => 1                                  |
|                                                       |
|o6 : HashTable                                         |
+-------------------------------------------------------+

Here is a simple implementation of the free abelian group on four letters,
where each element is represented as a hash table that associates coefficients
to strings.

+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : Free = new Type of HashTable                          |
|                                                           |
|o7 = Free                                                  |
|                                                           |
|o7 : Type                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : p = new Free from { "x" => 2, "y" => 3, "z" => 5 }    |
|                                                           |
|o8 = Free{"x" => 2}                                        |
|          "y" => 3                                         |
|          "z" => 5                                         |
|                                                           |
|o8 : Free                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : q = new Free from { "x" => 100, "y" => 200, "w" => 7 }|
|                                                           |
|o9 = Free{"w" => 7  }                                      |
|          "x" => 100                                       |
|          "y" => 200                                       |
|                                                           |
|o9 : Free                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : Free + Free := (p,q) -> merge(p, q, plus);           |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : p+q                                                  |
|                                                           |
|o11 = Free{"w" => 7  }                                     |
|           "x" => 102                                      |
|           "y" => 203                                      |
|           "z" => 5                                        |
|                                                           |
|o11 : Free                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+

If x and y have the same *note class: class, and have the same *note parent:
parent,, as in the previous example, then so will z.

+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : x = new MutableHashTable from {"alice" => 53709, "bob" => 6549};|
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : y = new MutableHashTable from {"bob" => 86, "charlie" => 23};   |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : mutable merge(x, y, plus)                                       |
|                                                                      |
|o14 = true                                                            |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+

The function *note combine: combine, allows much greater control when combining
two hash tables: you can give functions for how to handle every key and value
of the input tables, not just the duplicates. The function *note mergePairs:
mergePairs, is similar to merge, but works on lists of pairs rather than hash
tables.

See also
========

  * *note applyKeys: applyKeys, -- apply a function to each key in a hash table
  * *note applyPairs: applyPairs, -- apply a function to each pair in a hash
    table
  * *note applyValues: applyValues, -- apply a function to each value in a hash
    table
  * *note combine: combine, -- combine hash tables
  * *note keys: keys, -- keys used in a hash table, dictionary, or database
  * *note mergePairs: mergePairs, -- merge sorted lists of pairs
  * *note pairs: pairs, -- list the pairs in a hash table, dictionary, or basic
    list
  * *note scanKeys: scanKeys, -- apply a function to each key in a hash table
    or database
  * *note scanPairs: scanPairs, -- apply a function to the pairs in a hash
    table
  * *note scanValues: scanValues, -- apply a function to each value in a hash
    table or database
  * *note values: values, -- values in a hash table
  * *note hash tables: hash tables,

Ways to use merge:
==================

  * "merge(HashTable,HashTable,Function)"

