# -- length or access to elements
*********************************

Description
===========

# is used as both a unary and a binary operator.

As a unary operator: #x returns the length or cardinality of a list, set, hash
table, or string x.

As a binary operator: x#i returns the ith element of a list, hash table,
database, or string x.

+------------------------------+
|i1 : L = {23, 42, 107, 2, 50};|
+------------------------------+
|i2 : #L                       |
|                              |
|o2 = 5                        |
+------------------------------+
|i3 : L#2                      |
|                              |
|o3 = 107                      |
+------------------------------+

Caveat
======

The precedence of # when used as a binary operator is high, as high as *note .:
_pd,, but when used as a unary operator the precedence is much lower.

See also
========

  * *note #?: #?, -- check existence of value in a list, hash table, database,
    or string
  * *note # BasicList: # List, -- length or cardinality of a list, hash table,
    dictionary, set, or string
  * *note BasicList # ZZ: List # ZZ, -- get value from list, hash table,
    database, dictionary, or string
  * *note hash tables: hash tables,
  * *note lists and sequences: lists and sequences, -- a detailed overview of
    lists and sequences in Macaulay2

