..< -- a binary operator, used for sequences of consecutive items, not including the endpoint
*********************************************************************************************

Description
===========

The most confusing thing about this operator, in all its guises, is that it is
not a syntactic construction, and so the resulting sequences do not splice
themselves into enclosing lists, as in each of the following examples.

+---------------------+
|i1 : {10..<11}       |
|                     |
|o1 = {1 : (10)}      |
|                     |
|o1 : List            |
+---------------------+
|i2 : {10..<8}        |
|                     |
|o2 = {()}            |
|                     |
|o2 : List            |
+---------------------+
|i3 : {3..<5,8..<10}  |
|                     |
|o3 = {(3, 4), (8, 9)}|
|                     |
|o3 : List            |
+---------------------+

  Use *note splice: splice, to fix that.

+--------------------------+
|i4 : splice {3..<5,8..<10}|
|                          |
|o4 = {3, 4, 8, 9}         |
|                          |
|o4 : List                 |
+--------------------------+

If a type of list, instead of a sequence, is desired, use *note toList: toList,
or the operator *note new: new,.

+----------------------------+
|i5 : 0..<5                  |
|                            |
|o5 = (0, 1, 2, 3, 4)        |
|                            |
|o5 : Sequence               |
+----------------------------+
|i6 : toList (0..<5)         |
|                            |
|o6 = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}        |
|                            |
|o6 : List                   |
+----------------------------+
|i7 : new Array from 0..<5   |
|                            |
|o7 = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]        |
|                            |
|o7 : Array                  |
+----------------------------+
|i8 : new Sum from 0..<5     |
|                            |
|o8 = 0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4      |
|                            |
|o8 : Expression of class Sum|
+----------------------------+

The operator can be used with sequences or lists, whose elements are of various
types, to produce rectangular intervals.

+------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : (0,0)..<(2,3)                                    |
|                                                      |
|o9 = ((0, 0), (0, 1), (0, 2), (1, 0), (1, 1), (1, 2)) |
|                                                      |
|o9 : Sequence                                         |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : p_(0,a) ..< r_(2,c)                             |
|                                                      |
|o10 = (p   , p   , p   , p   , q   , q   , q   , q   )|
|        0,a   0,b   1,a   1,b   0,a   0,b   1,a   1,b |
|                                                      |
|o10 : Sequence                                        |
+------------------------------------------------------+

Use *note ..: _pd_pd, instead to get a sequence that does not stop short of the
endpoint.

