?? -- null coalescing operator
******************************

  * Usage: 
        x ?? y
  * Inputs:
      * x, a *note thing: Thing,, 
      * y, a *note thing: Thing,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note thing: Thing,, either x or y; 

Description
===========

If x is null or a non-interrupting error, then y is returned.  When it is an
error, the error message is suppressed like it is with *note try: try,.

+--------------+
|i1 : null ?? 2|
|              |
|o1 = 2        |
+--------------+
|i2 : 1/0 ?? 3 |
|              |
|o2 = 3        |
+--------------+

In all other cases, x is returned.

+-----------+
|i3 : 5 ?? 6|
|           |
|o3 = 5     |
+-----------+

Note that y is lazily evaluated, that is, it is only evaluated if it is
necessary.

The null coalescing operator can be combined with *note augmented assignment:
augmented assignment, as a shortcut for if x === null then x = y and if not
x#?i then x#i = y.  Note that it behaves slightly differently than other
augmented assignment operators, as x ??= y is treated like x ?? (x = y) rather
than x = x ?? y.

+------------------------+
|i4 : x = null           |
+------------------------+
|i5 : x ??= 2            |
|                        |
|o5 = 2                  |
+------------------------+
|i6 : x ??= 3            |
|                        |
|o6 = 2                  |
+------------------------+
|i7 : x = new MutableList|
|                        |
|o7 = MutableList{}      |
|                        |
|o7 : MutableList        |
+------------------------+
|i8 : x#0 ??= 4          |
|                        |
|o8 = 4                  |
+------------------------+
|i9 : peek x             |
|                        |
|o9 = MutableList{4}     |
+------------------------+
|i10 : x#0 ??= 5         |
|                        |
|o10 = 4                 |
+------------------------+
|i11 : peek x            |
|                        |
|o11 = MutableList{4}    |
+------------------------+

It is also possible to install a method for a particular type to determine
whether an instance x of that type should be considered "null" or not.  Such a
method should return either x or *note null: null,

+------------------------------------+
|i12 : X = new Type of BasicList;    |
+------------------------------------+
|i13 : ?? X := x -> if #x > 0 then x;|
+------------------------------------+
|i14 : x = new X from {};            |
+------------------------------------+
|i15 : y = new X from {5};           |
+------------------------------------+
|i16 : x ?? y                        |
|                                    |
|o16 = X{5}                          |
|                                    |
|o16 : X                             |
+------------------------------------+
|i17 : y ?? x                        |
|                                    |
|o17 = X{5}                          |
|                                    |
|o17 : X                             |
+------------------------------------+

It is also possible to use ?? as a prefix operator to call this method.

+-------------+
|i18 : ?? 2   |
|             |
|o18 = 2      |
+-------------+
|i19 : ?? x   |
+-------------+
|i20 : ??(1/0)|
+-------------+

Caveat
======

Although this operator is "flexible" in the sense that it possible to install a
method to determine the behavior on the left-hand side, it is not truly
flexible like most other binary operators.  In particular, since it is only
lazily evaluated, it is not possible to install a method that uses the value of
the right-hand side.

For the programmer
==================

The object *note ??: ??, is a *note keyword: Keyword,.

This operator may be used as a binary operator in an expression like x??y. 

This operator may be used as a prefix unary operator in an expression like ??y.
The user may *note install a method: installing methods, for handling such
expressions with code such as

           ?? Y := (y) -> ...

where Y is the class of y.

