precedence of operators
***********************

One aspect of parsing precedence is associativity.  A left-associative operator
is one, such as *note *: _st,, with the property that x * y * z is parsed as (x
* y) * z. A right-associative operator is one, such as *note =: =,, with the
property that x = y = z is parsed as x = (y = z).  These operators are left
associative: *note #: #, *note #?: #?, *note %: %, *note &: &, *note *: _st,
*note **: _st_st, *note +: +, *note ++: ++, *note ,: _cm, *note -: -, *note .:
_pd, *note ..: _pd_pd, *note ..<: _pd_pd<, *note .?: _pd?, *note /: /, *note
//: //, *note <<: <<, *note @@: @@, *note @@?: @@?, *note ^: ^, *note ^**:
^_st_st, *note ^<: ^<, *note ^<=: ^<=, *note ^>: ^>, *note ^>=: ^>=, *note ^^:
^^, *note _: _us, *note _<: _us<, *note _<=: _us<=, *note _>: _us>, *note _>=:
_us>=, *note |: |, *note |_: |_us, *note ||: ||, *note ·: ·, *note ⊠: ⊠, *note
⧢: ⧢,, and these operators are right associative: *note !=: !=, *note %=: %=,
*note &=: &=, *note **=: _st_st=, *note *=: _st=, *note ++=: ++=, *note +=: +=,
*note -=: -=, *note ->: ->, *note ..<=: _pd_pd<=, *note ..=: _pd_pd=, *note
//=: //=, *note /=: /=, *note _colon_: _co, *note _colon_=: _co=, *note ;: ;,
*note <: <, *note <-: <-, *note <<=: <<=, *note <=: <=, *note <==: <==, *note
<===: <===, *note <==>: <==>, *note <==>=: <==>=, *note =: =, *note =!=: =!=,
*note ==: ==, *note ===: ===, *note ===>: ===>, *note ===>=: ===>=, *note ==>:
==>, *note ==>=: ==>=, *note =>: =>, *note >: >, *note >=: >=, *note >>: >>,
*note >>=: >>=, *note ?: ?, *note ??: ??, *note ??=: ??=, *note @: @, *note @=:
@=, *note @@=: @@=, *note @@?=: @@?=, *note \: \, *note \=: \=, *note \\: \\,
*note \\=: \\=, *note ^**=: ^_st_st=, *note ^=: ^=, *note ^^=: ^^=, *note _=:
_us=, *note |-: |-, *note |-=: |-=, *note |=: |=, *note |_=: |_us=, *note ||=:
||=, *note ~: ~, *note ~=: ~=, *note ·=: ·=, *note ⊠=: ⊠=, *note ⧢=: ⧢=, *note
and: and, *note or: or, *note SPACE: SPACE, *note xor: xor, .

Here are the operators arranged in order of increasing parsing precedence.  For
example, * has higher parsing precedence than +, and hence 2+3*5 is parsed as
though it had been written as 2+(3*5).  The symbol *note SPACE: SPACE,
represents the operator that is used when two things are adjacent in program
code.

