conditional execution -- if-then-else statements
************************************************

The basic way to control the execution of code is with the *note if: if,
expression.  Such an expression typically has the form
if X then Y else Z
 and is evaluated as follows.  First X is evaluated.  If the result is true,
then the value of Y is provided, and if the result is false, then the value of
Z is provided.  An error is signalled if the value of X is anything but true or
false.
+--------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : (-4 .. 4) /                                  |
|          (i -> if i < 0 then "neg"               |
|               else if i == 0 then "zer"          |
|               else "pos")                        |
|                                                  |
|o1 = (neg, neg, neg, neg, zer, pos, pos, pos, pos)|
|                                                  |
|o1 : Sequence                                     |
+--------------------------------------------------+
The else clause may be omitted from an if expression.  In that case, if value
of the predicate X is false, then *note null: null, is provided as the value of
the if expression.
+--------------------------------------+
|i2 : (-4 .. 4) /                      |
|          (i -> if i < 0 then "neg"   |
|          else if i == 0 then "zer")  |
|                                      |
|o2 = (neg, neg, neg, neg, zer, , , , )|
|                                      |
|o2 : Sequence                         |
+--------------------------------------+
There are a variety of predicate functions (such as <, used above) that yield
true or false and can be used as the predicate in an if expression.  For a
list, see *note Boolean: Boolean,.  Boolean results may be combined with *note
not: not,, *note and: and,, and *note or: or,.
* Menu:

* if::                          condition testing
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

