error handling -- signalling and trapping errors
************************************************

When an error occurs in your program, an error message will appear that gives
the name of the file, the line number, and the column number of the code that
provoked the error.


You may use the function *note error: error, in your programs to signal errors.
Your error message will appear on the screen and execution will be stopped.


The keywords *note try: try, and *note trap: trap, can be used to catch an
error before execution is stopped and to continue or to try something else.

See also
========

  * *note stopIfError: stopIfError, -- whether to stop the program when an
    error occurs
* Menu:

* error::                       deliver error message
* try::                         catch an error
* throw::                       throw and catch exceptions
* trap::                        trap an error
* Error::                       error information
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_language.m2:714:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: error, Next: try, Up: error handling

error -- deliver error message
******************************

  * Usage: 
        error s
  * Inputs:
      * s, a *note string: String,, a string or a sequence of things that can
        be converted to strings
  * Consequences:
      * an error message is displayed

Description
===========

error s causes an error message s to be displayed.


The error message s (which should be a string or a sequence of things that can
be converted to strings and concatenated) is printed. Execution of the code is
interrupted, and control is returned to top level. Here is an explicit example
showing how this command is used:
exampleCode = p -> (
     if not isPrime p
     then error "expected a prime integer";
     if p == 2
     then error "expected an odd prime";
     lift((p+1)/2, ZZ)
     );

Eventually we will have a means of ensuring that the line number printed out
with the error message will have more significance, but currently it is the
location in the code of the error expression itself.

If the error message begins with a hyphen, then the word "error" will not be
inserted into the error message.

For the programmer
==================

The object *note error: error, is a *note compiled function: CompiledFunction,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_debugging.m2:332:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: try, Next: throw, Prev: error, Up: error handling

try -- catch an error
*********************

  * Usage: 
        try x then y else z
        try x then y except err do z
  * Inputs:
      * x, code; 
      * y, code; 
      * err, a *note symbol: Symbol,, 
      * z, code; 
  * Consequences:
      * the code x is run; if no error or *note alarm: alarm, occurs occurs,
        then the code y is run; otherwise, the code z is run

Description
===========

The return value is the value returned by y or z, as the case may be.

+------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : apply(-3..3, i -> try 1/i then 1/i else infinity)|
|                                                      |
|        1    1                   1  1                 |
|o1 = (- -, - -, -1, infinity, 1, -, -)                |
|        3    2                   2  3                 |
|                                                      |
|o1 : Sequence                                         |
+------------------------------------------------------+

The clause then y may be omitted, in which case the return value is the value
returned by x if there is no error or alarm.

+---------------------------------------------+
|i2 : apply(-3..3, i -> try 1/i else infinity)|
|                                             |
|        1    1                   1  1        |
|o2 = (- -, - -, -1, infinity, 1, -, -)       |
|        3    2                   2  3        |
|                                             |
|o2 : Sequence                                |
+---------------------------------------------+

The clause else z may be omitted, in which case the return value is the value
returned by y, unless an error or alarm occurs, in which case *note null: null,
is returned.

+----------------------------------------+
|i3 : apply(-3..3, i -> try 1/i then 1/i)|
|                                        |
|        1    1           1  1           |
|o3 = (- -, - -, -1, , 1, -, -)          |
|        3    2           2  3           |
|                                        |
|o3 : Sequence                           |
+----------------------------------------+

The clauses then y else z may both be omitted, in which case the return value
is the value returned by x, unless an error or alarm occurs, in which case
*note null: null, is returned.

+-------------------------------+
|i4 : apply(-3..3, i -> try 1/i)|
|                               |
|        1    1           1  1  |
|o4 = (- -, - -, -1, , 1, -, -) |
|        3    2           2  3  |
|                               |
|o4 : Sequence                  |
+-------------------------------+

In the alternate form try x then y except err do z, x is first evaluated. If no
error occurs, then the value of y is returned, or the value of x if the then y
clause is omitted. If an error occurs, then the corresponding *note Error:
Error, object is assigned to the symbol specified by err. That symbol is then
available during the evaluation of z, whose value is returned.

+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : apply(-3..3, i -> try 1/i then 1/i except err do err)|
|                                                          |
|        1    1                           1  1             |
|o5 = (- -, - -, -1, division by zero, 1, -, -)            |
|        3    2                           2  3             |
|                                                          |
|o5 : Sequence                                             |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : apply(-3..3, i -> try 1/i except err do err)         |
|                                                          |
|        1    1                           1  1             |
|o6 = (- -, - -, -1, division by zero, 1, -, -)            |
|        3    2                           2  3             |
|                                                          |
|o6 : Sequence                                             |
+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : oo#3                                                 |
|                                                          |
|o7 = division by zero                                     |
|                                                          |
|o7 : Error                                                |
+----------------------------------------------------------+

The behavior of interrupts (other than alarms) is unaffected.

Caveat
======

We will change the behavior of this function soon so that it will be possible
to catch errors of a particular type.  Meanwhile, users are recommended to use
this function sparingly, if at all.

See also
========

  * *note trap: trap, -- trap an error

For the programmer
==================

The object *note try: try, is a *note keyword: Keyword,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_language.m2:778:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: throw, Next: trap, Prev: try, Up: error handling

throw -- throw and catch exceptions
***********************************

  * Usage: 
        catch c
        throw x
  * Outputs:
      * catch returns the value obtained by evaluating the code c, or, if a
        throw was executed during the evaluation of c, the argument given to
        throw.
  * Consequences:
      * throw passes the flow of control to the surrounding catch, and x is
        returned as its value

Description
===========

+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : catch scan(0..10, i -> if i == 5 then throw 18 else print i)|
|0                                                                |
|1                                                                |
|2                                                                |
|3                                                                |
|4                                                                |
|                                                                 |
|o1 = 18                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+

For the programmer
==================

The object *note throw: throw, is a *note keyword: Keyword,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_language.m2:599:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: trap, Next: Error, Prev: throw, Up: error handling

trap -- trap an error
*********************

  * Usage: 
        trap c

Description
===========

The code c is evaluated and a sequence containing two elements is returned.  If
the evaluation completes successfully, then the first element is the value and
the second element is null.

+-------------+
|i1 : trap 5  |
|             |
|o1 = (5, )   |
|             |
|o1 : Sequence|
+-------------+

If an error occurs, then the first element is null and the second element is an
*note Error: Error, object containing information about the error is returned.

+-------------------------+
|i2 : trap 1/0            |
|                         |
|o2 = (, division by zero)|
|                         |
|o2 : Sequence            |
+-------------------------+

Note that this is a *note Keyword: Keyword,, not a method, and so it is not
necessary to enclose c in parentheses.

+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : trap 5 == "foo"                                         |
|                                                             |
|o3 = (, no method for binary operator == applied to objects:)|
|                    5 (of class ZZ)                          |
|             ==     "foo" (of class String)                  |
|                                                             |
|o3 : Sequence                                                |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : {trap error "bar", 1/2}                                 |
|                                                             |
|               1                                             |
|o4 = {(, bar), -}                                            |
|               2                                             |
|                                                             |
|o4 : List                                                    |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : trap 1/0; 1/2                                           |
|                                                             |
|     1                                                       |
|o6 = -                                                       |
|     2                                                       |
|                                                             |
|o6 : QQ                                                      |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note try: try, -- catch an error

For the programmer
==================

The object *note trap: trap, is a *note keyword: Keyword,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_debugging.m2:399:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: Error, Prev: trap, Up: error handling

Error -- error information
**************************

Description
===========

An instance of this class may be caught when an error occurs inside a *note
trap: trap, or *note try: try, statement.  The error message may be recovered
using *note toString: toString, and the location using *note locate: locate,.

+--------------------------+
|i1 : (val, err) = trap 1/0|
|                          |
|o1 = (, division by zero) |
|                          |
|o1 : Sequence             |
+--------------------------+
|i2 : toString err         |
|                          |
|o2 = division by zero     |
+--------------------------+
|i3 : locate err           |
|                          |
|o3 = stdio:1:18-1:21      |
|                          |
|o3 : FilePosition         |
+--------------------------+

An Error object may be constructed from an error message.

+-------------------------------+
|i4 : err = new Error from "foo"|
|                               |
|o4 = foo                       |
|                               |
|o4 : Error                     |
+-------------------------------+

An error may be raised from an Error object using *note error: error,.

+-----------------------------+
|i5 : stopIfError = false     |
|                             |
|o5 = false                   |
+-----------------------------+
|i6 : error err               |
|stdio:6:5:(3):[1]: error: foo|
+-----------------------------+

It is possible to create subclasses of Error for finer error handling.

+------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : MyError = new SelfInitializingType of Error|
|                                                |
|o7 = MyError                                    |
|                                                |
|o7 : SelfInitializingType                       |
+------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : try error MyError "bar" except err do err  |
|                                                |
|o8 = bar                                        |
|                                                |
|o8 : MyError                                    |
+------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note try: try, -- catch an error
  * *note trap: trap, -- trap an error
  * *note error: error, -- deliver error message

Methods that use an error:
==========================

  * "new Error from Thing"
  * "locate(Error)" -- see *note locate: locate, -- locate source code

For the programmer
==================

The object *note Error: Error, is a *note type: Type,, with ancestor class
*note Thing: Thing,.

