File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: what a class is, Next: installing methods, Prev: using hooks, Up: The Macaulay2 language

what a class is
***************

In Macaulay2 the behavior of a function depends heavily on the types of the
arguments it's presented with.  For example, the expression x+y means the sum
if x and y are integers, but it means the union if x and y are sets.  To
implement this in a clean fashion, we store the code for doing things with sets
in something called *note Set: Set, and we store the code for doing things with
integers in something called *note ZZ: ZZ,.  We say that each integer is an
*note instance: instance, of *note ZZ: ZZ,, and *note ZZ: ZZ, is the *note
class: class, (or type) of each integer.  The function *note class: class,
provides the class of an object, and the function *note instance: instance,
tells whether a given object is an instance of a given class, or a subclass of
it, and so on.


+------------------------+
|i1 : class 33           |
|                        |
|o1 = ZZ                 |
|                        |
|o1 : Ring               |
+------------------------+
|i2 : instance(33,ZZ)    |
|                        |
|o2 = true               |
+------------------------+
|i3 : instance(33,String)|
|                        |
|o3 = false              |
+------------------------+
The corresponding mathematical idea is that *note ZZ: ZZ, is the set of all
integers.


The class of all classes or types is called *note Type: Type,.
+----------------------+
|i4 : instance(ZZ,Type)|
|                      |
|o4 = true             |
+----------------------+
|i5 : instance(33,Type)|
|                      |
|o5 = false            |
+----------------------+
The class of all objects in the system is *note Thing: Thing,.
+-----------------------+
|i6 : instance(33,Thing)|
|                       |
|o6 = true              |
+-----------------------+
Everything has a class, and every class has a *note parent: parent,.  The
parent class represents a broader class of objects, and is used to contain code
that applies to the broader class.  For example, *note ZZ: ZZ, is a ring, and
every ring is also a type.
+--------------------+
|i7 : class ZZ       |
|                    |
|o7 = Ring           |
|                    |
|o7 : Type           |
+--------------------+
|i8 : parent class ZZ|
|                    |
|o8 = Type           |
|                    |
|o8 : Type           |
+--------------------+
Types are implemented as hash tables -- it's a versatile way of storing bits of
code that are needed in various situations; the keys for the hash table are
constructed in a certain way from the function and the types of its arguments
whose details the user doesn't need to know.


See also
========

  * *note uniform: uniform, -- whether all elements of a list are the same
    class
  * *note Thing: Thing, -- the class of all things
  * *note Nothing: Nothing, -- the empty class
  * *note Type: Type, -- the class of all mutable types
  * *note MutableList: MutableList, -- the class of all mutable lists
  * *note MutableHashTable: MutableHashTable, -- the class of all mutable hash
    tables
  * *note SelfInitializingType: SelfInitializingType, -- the class of all self
    initializing types
