newClass -- set the class and parent of an object
*************************************************

Description
===========

setting the class and parent
============================

  * Usage: 
        newClass(A,B,x)
  * Inputs:
      * A, a *note hash table: HashTable,
      * B, a *note hash table: HashTable,
      * x
  * Outputs:
      * a copy (possibly) of x with A as class and B as parent

setting the class
=================

  * Usage: 
        newClass(A,x)
  * Inputs:
      * A, a *note hash table: HashTable,
      * x
  * Outputs:
      * a copy (possibly) of x with A as the new class

common remarks
==============

If x is a basic list or sequence, then *note BasicList: BasicList, should be an
ancestor of A and B should be *note Nothing: Nothing,. If x is a hash table,
then *note HashTable: HashTable, should be an ancestor of A.

If the class (and parent) of x are already equal to A (and B, respectively),
then copying of the elements of x is not required, and is not done.

If x is mutable, and instances of class A are also mutable, then copying of the
elements of x is not required, and is not done.

If x is not a hash table, basic list, or sequence, then its class will be set
to A internally, essentially by wrapping it in a special kind of object
designed solely to indicate the new class.  The new class A must be a
specialization of the class of x. The parent cannot be reset this way. Not all
of the internal code of Macaulay2 is ready to recognize such wrapped objects,
which are part of a new feature, except for the code that handles functions.

+----------------------------------------------+
|i1 : t = 1..4                                 |
|                                              |
|o1 = (1, 2, 3, 4)                             |
|                                              |
|o1 : Sequence                                 |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i2 : newClass(Array,t)                        |
|                                              |
|o2 = [1, 2, 3, 4]                             |
|                                              |
|o2 : Array                                    |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i3 : x = new HashTable from { a => 1, b => 2 }|
|                                              |
|o3 = HashTable{a => 1}                        |
|               b => 2                         |
|                                              |
|o3 : HashTable                                |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i4 : z = newClass(ImmutableType,Vector,x)     |
|                                              |
|o4 = ImmutableType{a => 1}                    |
|                   b => 2                     |
|                                              |
|o4 : ImmutableType                            |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i5 : parent z                                 |
|                                              |
|o5 = Vector                                   |
|                                              |
|o5 : Type                                     |
+----------------------------------------------+

The difference between new A of B from x and newClass(A,B,x) is that the
methods installed for *note new: new, are not used.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : new Thing of Thing from Thing := (A,B,c) -> (                     |
|            << "-- new " << A << " of " << B                           |
|            << " from " << toString c << endl;                         |
|            c);                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : new ImmutableType of Vector from x                                |
|-- new ImmutableType of Vector from new HashTable from {a => 1, b => 2}|
|                                                                       |
|o7 = ImmutableType{a => 1}                                             |
|                   b => 2                                              |
|                                                                       |
|o7 : ImmutableType                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : newClass(ImmutableType,Vector,x)                                  |
|                                                                       |
|o8 = ImmutableType{a => 1}                                             |
|                   b => 2                                              |
|                                                                       |
|o8 : ImmutableType                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note new: new, -- new objects and new types
  * *note copy: copy, -- copy an object
  * *note toList: toList, -- create a list

