File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: making a new method function, Next: debugging, Prev: making new classes, Up: The Macaulay2 language

making a new method function
****************************

The function *note method: method, can be used to make new functions which
execute different bits of code depending on the types of the arguments
presented.  Our system depends heavily on such functions.
+-------------------+
|i1 : f = method()  |
|                   |
|o1 = f             |
|                   |
|o1 : MethodFunction|
+-------------------+
We can install a method to be used when f is applied to a string as follows.
+--------------------------+
|i2 : f String := s -> s|s;|
+--------------------------+
|i3 : f ".abcd."           |
|                          |
|o3 = .abcd..abcd.         |
+--------------------------+
We can check for the types of up to three arguments, too.
+------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : f(ZZ,String) := (n,s) -> concatenate (n:s);|
+------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : f(5,".abcd.")                              |
|                                                |
|o5 = .abcd..abcd..abcd..abcd..abcd.             |
+------------------------------------------------+
* Menu:

* method::                      make a new method function
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_methods.m2:23:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: method, Up: making a new method function

method -- make a new method function
************************************

  * Usage: 
        f = method()
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * TypicalValue => a *note type: Type,, default value Thing, the type of
        the value returned by f, typically.  This information is used only to
        build documentation automatically, and is stored in the hash table
        *note typicalValues: typicalValues,.
      * Options => a *note list: List,, default value null, a list of options J
        => v, where J is the name of an optional argument for f, and v is its
        default value. The list of options could be also replaced by the
        corresponding *note OptionTable: OptionTable,. Specifying true here
        indicates that option handling is done by the individual methods, while
        still allowing dispatching to methods based on types of arguments to be
        done. The individual methods may have various sets of acceptable option
        names, possibly empty. See *note making new functions with optional
        arguments: making new functions with optional arguments,.
      * Binary => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value false, whether
        the method is to be binary: for three arguments or more the result will
        be computed by calling binary methods installed for f with two
        arguments at a time.
      * Dispatch => ..., default value {Thing, Thing, Thing, Thing}, the method
        for getting a list of types from the parameters; the value of this
        option should be *note Thing: Thing, or *note Type: Type, to indicate
        that a sequence should be regarded as a single argument, or, if the
        elements of a sequence are to be regarded as separate parameters, a
        list whose elements are *note Thing: Thing, or *note Type: Type,.
        Parameters corresponding to *note Thing: Thing, or to a position beyond
        the end of the list are dispatched according to their *note class:
        class,, whereas parameters corresponding to *note Type: Type, are
        expected to be types (actually, hash tables) and are used directly in
        the search for methods; see *note inheritance: inheritance,.
  * Outputs:
      * f, a new method function, a *note method function: MethodFunction,, a
        *note method function with a single argument: MethodFunctionSingle,, an
        *note associative binary method function: MethodFunctionBinary,, or a
        *note method function with options: MethodFunctionWithOptions,

Description
===========

The code above creates a method function that takes up to four arguments,
looking up the appropriate method according to the classes of the arguments,
with inheritance.  To install a method for two arguments, (x,y), of classes X
and Y, use code like this:
     f(X,Y) := (x,y) -> ...
where '...' represents the body of the function you wish to install. The syntax
for one or three arguments is analogous.  See *note _colon_=: _co=, for
details.
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : f = method()                                                            |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = f                                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o1 : MethodFunction                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : f ZZ := x -> -x;                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : f(ZZ,String) := (n,s) -> concatenate(n:s);                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : f(String,ZZ,String) := (s,n,t) -> concatenate(s," : ",toString n," :    |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|",t);                                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : f 44                                                                    |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = -44                                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : f(5,"abcd ")                                                            |
|                                                                             |
|o6 = abcd abcd abcd abcd abcd                                                |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : f("foo",88,"bar")                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o7 = foo : 88 : bar                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

In the following example we install a asymmetric method to illustrate the
left-associative order of evaluation for a binary method function.

+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : p = method(Binary => true, TypicalValue => List)|
|                                                     |
|o8 = p                                               |
|                                                     |
|o8 : MethodFunctionBinary                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : p(ZZ,ZZ) := p(List,ZZ) := (i,j) -> {i,j}        |
|                                                     |
|o9 = FunctionClosure[stdio:9:26-9:40]                |
|                                                     |
|o9 : FunctionClosure                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : p(1,2)                                         |
|                                                     |
|o10 = {1, 2}                                         |
|                                                     |
|o10 : List                                           |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : p(1,2,3,4,5,6)                                 |
|                                                     |
|o11 = {{{{{1, 2}, 3}, 4}, 5}, 6}                     |
|                                                     |
|o11 : List                                           |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
By default, at most four arguments (in a sequence) can be handled by a method
function, and the types have to be considered separately when installing
methods. In this example, we define a method function that treats a sequence as
a single argument, and we install a method for handling such arguments.
+------------------------------------+
|i12 : g = method(Dispatch => Thing);|
+------------------------------------+
|i13 : g ZZ := i -> -i;              |
+------------------------------------+
|i14 : g Sequence := S -> reverse S; |
+------------------------------------+
|i15 : g 44                          |
|                                    |
|o15 = -44                           |
+------------------------------------+
|i16 : g(3,4,5,6)                    |
|                                    |
|o16 = (6, 5, 4, 3)                  |
|                                    |
|o16 : Sequence                      |
+------------------------------------+
Here we define a method whose first argument is to be a type.  It will convert
its second argument to that type.
+------------------------------------+
|i17 : h = method(Dispatch => {Type})|
|                                    |
|o17 = h                             |
|                                    |
|o17 : MethodFunction                |
+------------------------------------+
|i18 : h(QQ,ZZ) := (QQ,n) -> n/1;    |
+------------------------------------+
|i19 : h(RR,ZZ) := (RR,n) -> n + 0.; |
+------------------------------------+
|i20 : h(ZZ,ZZ) := (ZZ,n) -> n;      |
+------------------------------------+
|i21 : h(ZZ,14)                      |
|                                    |
|o21 = 14                            |
+------------------------------------+
|i22 : h(QQ,14)                      |
|                                    |
|o22 = 14                            |
|                                    |
|o22 : QQ                            |
+------------------------------------+
|i23 : h(RR,14)                      |
|                                    |
|o23 = 14                            |
|                                    |
|o23 : RR (of precision 53)          |
+------------------------------------+

In the next example we make a linear function of a single real variable whose
coefficients are provided as optional arguments named Slope and Intercept, with
default value 1.

+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i24 : r = method(Options => {Slope => 1, Intercept => 1})|
|                                                         |
|o24 = r                                                  |
|                                                         |
|o24 : MethodFunctionWithOptions                          |
+---------------------------------------------------------+

The methods installed for this method function should be written in the form
opts -> args -> (...).  The argument opts will be assigned a hash table of type
*note OptionTable: OptionTable, containing the optional argument names and
their current values.  For example, in the body of the function, the current
value for the argument named b can be recovered with opts#b, or with opts.b, in
case b is known to be a global symbol.  Be careful not to change the value of
b, or the code will stop working; it would be a good idea to protect it.

+-------------------------------------------------+
|i25 : r RR := o -> x -> o.Slope * x + o.Intercept|
|                                                 |
|o25 = FunctionClosure[stdio:25:8-25:43]          |
|                                                 |
|o25 : FunctionClosure                            |
+-------------------------------------------------+
|i26 : r(5.)                                      |
|                                                 |
|o26 = 6                                          |
|                                                 |
|o26 : RR (of precision 53)                       |
+-------------------------------------------------+
|i27 : r(5.,Slope=>100)                           |
|                                                 |
|o27 = 501                                        |
|                                                 |
|o27 : RR (of precision 53)                       |
+-------------------------------------------------+

The default *note option table: OptionTable, for r can be recovered with the
function *note options: options,.  The options given to *note method: method,
can be recovered with *note methodOptions: methodOptions_lpFunction_rp,.

+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i28 : options r                                            |
|                                                           |
|o28 = OptionTable{Intercept => 1}                          |
|                  Slope => 1                               |
|                                                           |
|o28 : OptionTable                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i29 : methodOptions r                                      |
|                                                           |
|o29 = OptionTable{Binary => false                         }|
|                  Dispatch => {Thing, Thing, Thing, Thing} |
|                  Options => {Slope => 1, Intercept => 1}  |
|                  TypicalValue => Thing                    |
|                                                           |
|o29 : OptionTable                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+

In the next example we define a method function that leaves option processing
to the individual methods.

+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i30 : s = method(Options => true)                                          |
|                                                                           |
|o30 = s                                                                    |
|                                                                           |
|o30 : MethodFunctionWithOptions                                            |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i31 : s ZZ := { Slope => 17 } >> o -> x -> o.Slope * x                     |
|                                                                           |
|o31 = FunctionClosure[/usr/local/share/Macaulay2/Core/option.m2:15:14-18:8]|
|                                                                           |
|o31 : FunctionClosure                                                      |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i32 : s RR := { Intercept => 11 } >> o -> x -> x + o.Intercept             |
|                                                                           |
|o32 = FunctionClosure[/usr/local/share/Macaulay2/Core/option.m2:15:14-18:8]|
|                                                                           |
|o32 : FunctionClosure                                                      |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i33 : s 100                                                                |
|                                                                           |
|o33 = 1700                                                                 |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i34 : s 1000.                                                              |
|                                                                           |
|o34 = 1011                                                                 |
|                                                                           |
|o34 : RR (of precision 53)                                                 |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i35 : options s                                                            |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i36 : options(s,ZZ)                                                        |
|                                                                           |
|o36 = OptionTable{Slope => 17}                                             |
|                                                                           |
|o36 : OptionTable                                                          |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i37 : options(s,RR)                                                        |
|                                                                           |
|o37 = OptionTable{Intercept => 11}                                         |
|                                                                           |
|o37 : OptionTable                                                          |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+

For now, one installs a method function for s with no non-optional arguments
using *note installMethod: installMethod,:

+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i38 : installMethod(s,{ Slope => 1234 } >> o -> () -> o.Slope)             |
|                                                                           |
|o38 = FunctionClosure[/usr/local/share/Macaulay2/Core/option.m2:15:14-18:8]|
|                                                                           |
|o38 : FunctionClosure                                                      |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i39 : s()                                                                  |
|                                                                           |
|o39 = 1234                                                                 |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i40 : s(Slope => 4)                                                        |
|                                                                           |
|o40 = 4                                                                    |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
