File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: specifying typical values, Next: MethodFunction, Up: method

specifying typical values
*************************

For the purpose of constructing good documentation automatically, it is useful
to specify the type of value typically returned by a function or method.  For
example, the function *note isModule: isModule, returns a boolean value, and
this is specified when creating the method function with the option *note
TypicalValue: method, as follows.
isModule = method(TypicalValue => Boolean)


Other functions, such as *note prune: prune,, return values of various types,
depending on the type of the arguments provided.  To install a function f as
the handler for prune applied to a matrix, we would normally use the following
statement.
prune Matrix := f
To specify that the value typically returned is a matrix (of class Matrix), we
replace f by Matrix => f, as follows.
prune Matrix := Matrix => f
Here is the way our code looks.
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : code(prune, Matrix)                                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = -- code for method: prune(Matrix)                                       |
|     ../../../../../Macaulay2/m2/modules2.                                   |
|     minimalPresentation(Matrix) := prune(                                   |
|          M := source m;                                                     |
|          if not M.cache.?pruningMap then                                    |
|          N := target m;                                                     |
|          if not N.cache.?pruningMap then                                    |
|          m)                                                                 |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|m2:213:58-218:7: --source code:                                              |
|Matrix) := Matrix => opts -> (m) -> (                                        |
|                                                                             |
|m = m * (minimalPresentation M).cache.pruningMap;                            |
|                                                                             |
|m = (minimalPresentation N).cache.pruningMap^-1 * m;                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
The information is stored in the hash table *note typicalValues:
typicalValues,, and can be recovered like this.
+---------------------------------+
|i2 : typicalValues#(prune,Matrix)|
|                                 |
|o2 = Matrix                      |
|                                 |
|o2 : Type                        |
+---------------------------------+


Warning: don't imagine that a definition of the form 
f = t -> (...)
can be replaced with a declaration of the following form.
f = X => t -> (...)
The difference here is that here we are using simple assignment, rather than
installing a method.  To document the return type is X in this case, make an
entry in typicalValues directly.
f = t -> (...)
typicalValues#f = X
* Menu:

* typicalValues::               types of values returned by functions
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_methods.m2:57:0.

