export -- export symbols from a package
***************************************

  * Usage: 
        export {"symbol1", "symbol2", ...}
  * Inputs:
      * a *note list: List,, containing strings or options with string keys and
        values belonging to the package
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: List,, the list of exported symbols
  * Consequences:
      * the symbols whose names are in the list as strings, which should refer
        to functions or other symbols defined in the package, are made
        available to the user of the package, and are marked non-mutable. The
        strings are converted to symbols with those names in the dictionary of
        the package. An option of the form "name" => "symbol" creates a symbol
        with the name name that is a synonym of the symbol symbol.

Description
===========

A package can contain the code for many functions, only some of which should be
made visible to the user. The function export allows one to specify which
symbols are to be made visible. For an example see *note an example of a
package: an example of a package,.

No single-letter symbol should be exported, since such symbols are reserved as
variables for the user.

Use *note exportMutable: exportMutable, to export symbols whose values the user
is permitted to modify.

Use *note importFrom: importFrom, to import private symbols of a package.

See also
========

  * *note exportFrom: exportFrom, -- export symbols from a package's private
    dictionary
  * *note exportMutable: exportMutable, -- export mutable symbols from a
    package
  * *note importFrom: importFrom, -- import symbols to the current private
    dictionary

Ways to use export:
===================

  * "export(List)"
  * "export(String)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note export: export, is a *note method function with a single
argument: MethodFunctionSingle,.

