writing documentation
*********************

Documentation for user defined *note packages: packages, and Macaulay2 itself
is written using two main methods:



Macaulay2 documentation methods

  * the *note document(List): document, function, using a list-based *note
    hypertext: hypertext list format, markup format;
  * the *note doc(String): (SimpleDoc)doc, function, using a string-based *note
    SimpleDoc: (SimpleDoc)Top, format.

It is then formatted via *note installPackage: installPackage, as the
documentation built into Macaulay2, the online HTML documentation, and the info
pages. Much of the format and structure of the documentation is automatically
generated. Each documentation entry must be part of a package, and occur after
the *note beginDocumentation: beginDocumentation, section of the package.

Documentation templates
-----------------------

Each documentation entry is either an overview topic, or documentation of an
individual feature, such as a symbol, a function name, a function call (that
is, a function name, together with specific types of its arguments), an
optional argument to a function, or a package.

The easiest way to write documentation for an entry is to start with an example
as a template. See the documentation on either of the documentation methods
above for templates and examples.



The documentation writing cycle
-------------------------------

Start with the package that you wish to document, and select one or several of
the above examples or templates. Cycle through the following steps as you
refine your documentation.

  0. Edit your documentation entries as desired
  1. Generate the html pages for your package, using 
     installPackage("YourPackage")
  2. Review the generated HTML documentation using *note viewHelp: viewHelp,
     which displays this page in a browser.

Documentation style conventions
-------------------------------

There are a few stylistic conventions that should be noted. While not hard and
fast rules, keeping these stylistic conventions in mind makes for easier
reading by users.

  * Lowercase is used for all titles, unless a proper noun is being used.
  * Use *note TO: (Text)TO, to reference any Macaulay2 function, option, or
    variable occurring in the documentation as a hyperlink.
  * *note Inputs: Inputs,, *note Outputs: Outputs,, and *note Consequences:
    Consequences, should not end with periods.

See also
========

  * *note viewHelp: viewHelp, -- view online documentation in a web browser
  * *note installPackage: installPackage, -- load and install a package and its
    documentation
  * *note SimpleDoc: (SimpleDoc)Top, -- a simple documentation function
* Menu:

* hypertext list format::
* handling hypertext::
* document::                    create a documentation node
* tutorial::                    convert documentation from tutorial format
* undocumented::                declare that something need not be documented
* ofClass::                     English phrases for types
* EXAMPLE::                     construct a hypertext item from a Macaulay2 input string
* SYNOPSIS::                    a standardized synopsis for use in documentation nodes

For programmers
* documentation keys::
