File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: Key, Next: Headline, Prev: overview documentation template, Up: document

Key -- key of a documentation node
**********************************

  * Usage: 
        document { Key => key, ... }
  * Consequences:
      * specifies the name under which the documentation is stored

Description
===========

The document key is the name of the node, specifically the one that allows
users to find the documentation for the desired command. Some node names,
rather than being strings, are sequences of Macaulay2 objects that refer to
specific method functions. If one is documenting a function of Macaulay2, the
key will just be the function's name, as in the documentation for *note
resolution: (OldChainComplexes)resolution,.

+-------------------+
|Key => {resolution}|
+-------------------+

However, if one is documenting a method for a function, then the key will have
a different form, as in the documentation for *note resolution(Module):
(OldChainComplexes)resolution_lpModule_rp,.

+-----------------------------+
|Key => {(resolution, Module)}|
+-----------------------------+

If one is documenting an optional argument to a function, then the key has a
different form still, as in *note resolution(...,SyzygyLimit=>...):
(OldChainComplexes)resolution_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmSyzygyLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,.

+----------------------------------+
|Key => {[resolution, SyzygyLimit]}|
+----------------------------------+

Finally, if one is documenting an overview of a group of functions or a
package, as in *note ideals: ideals, then one would set up a document key of
the form:

+-----------------+
|Key => {"ideals"}|
+-----------------+

Caveat
======

No two documentation nodes may have the same key or even a key that is a
synonym for a key which is already used. However, sometimes there is a need for
several documentation nodes to have the same key. This happens when a command
that needs to be documented is a synonym for another already documented
command. In this case the synonym's key should be stated in quotation marks. As
an example, look at the documentation for *note SUBSECTION: (Text)SUBSECTION,
and *note HEADER2: (Text)HEADER2, in Text.m2 (see
common/share/Macaulay2/Text.m2 ).

See also
========

  * *note TO: (Text)TO, -- hypertext documentation link

Functions with optional argument named Key:
===========================================

  * "document(...,Key=>...)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note Key: Key, is a *note symbol: Symbol,.

