File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: parallel programming with threads and tasks, Next: system facilities, Prev: writing documentation, Up: The Macaulay2 language

parallel programming with threads and tasks
*******************************************

The simplest way to run computations in parallel is to use *note parallelApply:
parallelApply,. This works like *note apply(BasicList,Function):
apply_lpBasicList_cmFunction_rp,, except that it uses all your cores, and
always returns a *note List: List,.

+----------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : parallelApply(1..10, n -> n!)                        |
|                                                          |
|o1 = {1, 2, 6, 24, 120, 720, 5040, 40320, 362880, 3628800}|
|                                                          |
|o1 : List                                                 |
+----------------------------------------------------------+

There is some overhead to parallelism, so this will only speed things up for a
big computation. If the list is long, it will be split into chunks for each
core, reducing the overhead. But the speedup is still limited by the different
threads competing for memory, including cpu caches; it is like running
Macaulay2 on a computer that is running other big programs at the same time. We
can see this using *note elapsedTime: elapsedTime,.

+----------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : L = shuffle toList (1..10000);                 |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : elapsedTime         apply(1..100, n -> sort L);|
| -- .665354s elapsed                                |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : elapsedTime parallelApply(1..100, n -> sort L);|
| -- .261645s elapsed                                |
+----------------------------------------------------+

You will have to try it on your examples to see how much they speed up.

Warning: Threads computing in parallel can give wrong answers if their code is
not "thread safe", meaning they make modifications to memory without ensuring
the modifications get safely communicated to other threads. (Thread safety can
slow computations some.) Currently, modifications to Macaulay2 variables and
mutable hash tables are thread safe, but not changes inside mutable lists.
Also, access to external libraries such as singular, etc., may not currently be
thread safe.

The rest of this document describes how to control parallel tasks more
directly.

The task system schedules functions and inputs to run on a preset number of
threads. The number of threads to be used is given by the variable *note
allowableThreads: allowableThreads,, and may be examined and changed as
follows. (*note allowableThreads: allowableThreads, is temporarily increased if
necessary inside *note parallelApply: parallelApply,.)

+-------------------------------------------+
|i5 : allowableThreads                      |
|                                           |
|o5 = 5                                     |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i6 : allowableThreads = maxAllowableThreads|
|                                           |
|o6 = 5                                     |
+-------------------------------------------+

To run a function in another thread use *note schedule: schedule,, as in the
following example.

+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : R = QQ[x,y,z];                                                       |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : I = ideal(x^2 + 2*y^2 - y - 2*z, x^2 - 8*y^2 + 10*z - 1, x^2 - 7*y*z)|
|                                                                          |
|             2     2            2     2             2                     |
|o8 = ideal (x  + 2y  - y - 2z, x  - 8y  + 10z - 1, x  - 7y*z)             |
|                                                                          |
|o8 : Ideal of R                                                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : f = () -> gens gb I                                                  |
|                                                                          |
|o9 = f                                                                    |
|                                                                          |
|o9 : FunctionClosure                                                      |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : t = schedule f                                                      |
|                                                                          |
|o10 = <<task, created>>                                                   |
|                                                                          |
|o10 : Task                                                                |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Note that *note schedule: schedule, returns a task, not the result of the
computation, which will be accessible only after the task has completed the
computation.

+-----------------------+
|i11 : t                |
|                       |
|o11 = <<task, running>>|
|                       |
|o11 : Task             |
+-----------------------+

Use *note isReady: isReady_lpFile_rp, to check whether the result is available
yet.

+---------------+
|i12 : isReady t|
|               |
|o12 = false    |
+---------------+

To wait for the result and then retrieve it, use *note taskResult:
taskResult_lpTask_rp,.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : taskResult t                                               |
|                                                                 |
|o13 = | 980z2-18y-201z+13 35yz-4y+2z-1 10y2-y-12z+1 5x2-4y+2z-1 ||
|                                                                 |
|              1      4                                           |
|o13 : Matrix R  <-- R                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : assert instance(oo, Matrix)                                |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+

It is possible to make a task without starting it running, using *note
createTask: createTask,.

+-----------------------+
|i15 : t' = createTask f|
|                       |
|o15 = <<task, created>>|
|                       |
|o15 : Task             |
+-----------------------+
|i16 : t'               |
|                       |
|o16 = <<task, created>>|
|                       |
|o16 : Task             |
+-----------------------+

Start it running with *note schedule: schedule,.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i17 : schedule t';                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i18 : t'                                                         |
|                                                                 |
|o18 = <<task, created>>                                          |
|                                                                 |
|o18 : Task                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i19 : taskResult t'                                              |
|                                                                 |
|o19 = | 980z2-18y-201z+13 35yz-4y+2z-1 10y2-y-12z+1 5x2-4y+2z-1 ||
|                                                                 |
|              1      4                                           |
|o19 : Matrix R  <-- R                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+

One may use *note addStartTask: addStartTask_lpTask_cmTask_rp, to specify that
one task is to be started after another one finishes.  In the following
example, G will start after F finishes.

+-----------------------------------------+
|i20 : F = createTask(() -> "result of F")|
|                                         |
|o20 = <<task, created>>                  |
|                                         |
|o20 : Task                               |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i21 : G = createTask(() -> "result of G")|
|                                         |
|o21 = <<task, created>>                  |
|                                         |
|o21 : Task                               |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i22 : addStartTask(F,G)                  |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i23 : schedule F                         |
|                                         |
|o23 = <<task, created>>                  |
|                                         |
|o23 : Task                               |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i24 : taskResult F                       |
|                                         |
|o24 = result of F                        |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i25 : taskResult G                       |
|                                         |
|o25 = result of G                        |
+-----------------------------------------+

Use *note addCancelTask: addCancelTask_lpTask_cmTask_rp, to specify that the
completion of one task triggers the cancellation of another, by means of an
interrupt exception.

Use *note addDependencyTask: addDependencyTask_lpTask_cmTask_rp, to schedule a
task, but to ensure that it will not run until one or more other tasks finish
running.

Using the functions above, essentially any parallel functionality needed can be
created.

Low level C API functionality using the same scheduler also exists in the
Macaulay2/system directory. It works essentially the same way as the Macaulay2
interface.

See also
========

  * *note parallelism in engine computations: parallelism in engine
    computations, -- parallelism in engine computations
  * *note elapsedTime: elapsedTime, -- time a computation including time
    elapsed
* Menu:

* parallelApply::               apply a function to each element in parallel
* createTask::                  create a task
* addCancelTask: addCancelTask_lpTask_cmTask_rp.  specify that the completion of one task triggers the cancellation of another
* addDependencyTask: addDependencyTask_lpTask_cmTask_rp.  schedule a task, but ensure that it will not run until another task finishes
* addStartTask: addStartTask_lpTask_cmTask_rp.  schedule a task upon completion of another
* schedule::                    schedule a task for execution
* isReady(Task): isReady_lpTask_rp.  whether a task is finished
* taskResult: taskResult_lpTask_rp.  retrieve the value returned by a task
* cancelTask: cancelTask_lpTask_rp.  stop a task
* isCanceled: isCanceled_lpTask_rp.  whether a task has been canceled
* setIOExclusive::              exclusive I/O for the current thread
* setIOSynchronized::           synchronized I/O for threads
* setIOUnSynchronized::         unsynchronized I/O for threads
* getIOThreadMode::             get I/O thread mode
* threadLocal::                 create a symbol whose value in one thread is not shared with others
* allowableThreads::            the current maximum number of simultaneously running tasks
* maxAllowableThreads::         the maximum possible number of simultaneously running tasks
* Task::                        the class of all tasks
