realpath -- convert a filename to one passing through no symbolic links
***********************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        realpath fn
  * Inputs:
      * fn, a *note string: String,, a filename, or path to a file
  * Outputs:
      * a *note string: String,, a pathname to fn passing through no symbolic
        links, and ending with a slash if fn refers to a directory

Description
===========

+-------------------------------+
|i1 : realpath "."              |
|                               |
|o1 = /tmp/M2-31115-0/93-rundir/|
+-------------------------------+
|i2 : p = temporaryFileName()   |
|                               |
|o2 = /tmp/M2-32983-0/0         |
+-------------------------------+
|i3 : q = temporaryFileName()   |
|                               |
|o3 = /tmp/M2-32983-0/1         |
+-------------------------------+
|i4 : symlinkFile(p,q)          |
+-------------------------------+
|i5 : p << close                |
|                               |
|o5 = /tmp/M2-32983-0/0         |
|                               |
|o5 : File                      |
+-------------------------------+
|i6 : readlink q                |
|                               |
|o6 = /tmp/M2-32983-0/0         |
+-------------------------------+
|i7 : realpath q                |
|                               |
|o7 = /tmp/M2-32983-0/0         |
+-------------------------------+
|i8 : removeFile p              |
+-------------------------------+
|i9 : removeFile q              |
+-------------------------------+

The empty string is interpreted as a reference to the current directory.

+--------------------------------+
|i10 : realpath ""               |
|                                |
|o10 = /tmp/M2-31115-0/93-rundir/|
+--------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Every component of the path must exist in the file system and be accessible to
the user. Terminal slashes will be dropped.  Warning: under most operating
systems, the value returned is an absolute path (one starting at the root of
the file system), but under Solaris, this system call may, in certain
circumstances, return a relative path when given a relative path.

