run -- run an external command
******************************

  * Usage: 
        run s
  * Inputs:
      * s, a *note string: String,, a command understandable by the operating
        system
  * Outputs:
      * an *note integer: ZZ,, the return code

Description
===========

The process is run in the same process group, so signals invoked by control
characters at the terminal will go both to it and to Macaulay2.

Under Linux and Mac OS, the return code is 256 times the exit status code of
the command, if the command exited normally; by convention, an exit status code
of 0 indicates error free execution or the Boolean value true, an exit status
code of 1 indicates an error or the Boolean value false; if the command
terminated in response to a signal or fault, the signal number (in the range
1-126) is returned, added to 128 if a core dump was created; if the shell
(command interpreter) could not be executed, then 127 is returned.  Signal
numbers typically include 2 for interrupt, 3 for quit, 6 for abort, 9 for kill,
11 for segmentation fault, and 15 for termination.  For details, see the man
page of the libc routine system().

For the programmer
==================

The object *note run: run, is a *note compiled function: CompiledFunction,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

