prefixPath -- absolute locations of Macaulay2 files
***************************************************

Description
===========

The absolute location of a Macaulay2 file can be obtained by concatenating
three components: (a) the prefix, which is one of the members of the list *note
prefixPath: prefixPath,; (b) the relative location of the directory containing
the file, as recorded in the hash table *note Layout: currentLayout,; and (c)
the base name of the file. The value of *note prefixPath: prefixPath, is used
by *note installPackage: installPackage, when determining how to direct
documentation hyperlinks from one package to another.

The initial value of *note prefixPath: prefixPath, contains just the following
two optional items. If the variable *note prefixDirectory: prefixDirectory, was
given a non-null value initially or by a -e command line argument, then it will
be the last element of *note prefixPath: prefixPath,.  If the -q option was not
given on the command line used to invoke Macaulay2, then the value of
applicationDirectory()|"local/" will be the first element of *note prefixPath:
prefixPath,.  No attempt is made to synchronize the value of *note prefixPath:
prefixPath, with the values of *note prefixDirectory: prefixDirectory, and of
applicationDirectory(), which may change.

When running a newly compiled version of Macaulay2, adding something like -E
'prefixDirectory="/usr/"' to the command line is a good way to direct
hyperlinks created by *note installPackage: installPackage, to the
documentation provided by an older copy of Macaulay2 installed with the prefix
/usr/, and that, in turn, is easily done within Emacs by the keystroke sequence
"C-u f12", which offers you a chance to edit the command line.

The initial value of *note prefixPath: prefixPath, described above can be
overridden by the user's *note initialization file: initialization file,).

The list *note prefixPath: prefixPath, should be distinguished from the list
*note path: path,, which is used to locate files to be loaded, by functions
such as *note searchPath: searchPath_lpList_cmString_rp,, *note load: load,,
*note loadPackage: loadPackage,, and *note needsPackage: needsPackage,.

The following example shows the list of places where we might find the source
code of a package called Foo after it has been installed by *note
installPackage: installPackage,.

+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : stack apply(prefixPath, p -> p | Layout#1#"packages" | "Foo.m2")|
|                                                                     |
|o1 = /usr/local/share/Macaulay2/Foo.m2                               |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+

This example shows the list of places where we might reasonably find the html
file documenting a function named bar in a package called Foo.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : stack apply(prefixPath, p -> p | replace("PKG","Foo",Layout#1#"packageht|
|                                                                             |
|o2 = /usr/local/share/doc/Macaulay2/Foo/                                     |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     html/bar.html                                                           |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|ml") | "bar.html")                                                           |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

This example shows the list of places where we might reasonably find the info
file documenting a package called Foo.

+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : stack apply(prefixPath, p -> p | Layout#1#"info" | "Foo.info")|
|                                                                   |
|o3 = /usr/local/share/info/Foo.info                                |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+

