null -- the unique member of the empty class
********************************************

Description
===========

When it is the value of an expression entered into the interpreter, the output
line doesn't appear.  Empty spots in a list are represented by it.


It is the only member of the class *note Nothing: Nothing,, which strictly
speaking, ought to have no members at all.


An *note if: if, expression with no *note else: if, clause returns *note null:
null, when the predicate is false.


Various routines that prepare data for printing convert *note null: null, to an
empty string.
+--------------------+
|i1 : x = {2,3,,4}   |
|                    |
|o1 = {2, 3, , 4}    |
|                    |
|o1 : List           |
+--------------------+
|i2 : net x          |
|                    |
|o2 = {2, 3, , 4}    |
+--------------------+
|i3 : toString x#2   |
|                    |
|o3 = null           |
+--------------------+
|i4 : peek x         |
|                    |
|o4 = {2, 3, null, 4}|
+--------------------+

For the programmer
==================

The object *note null: null, is *note null: null,.

