norm -- l^p norm
****************

  * Usage: 
        norm M
        norm(p, M)
  * Inputs:
      * p, a *note number: Number,, specifying which norm to compute; 
      * M, a *note list: List,, a *note matrix: Matrix,, a *note mutable
        matrix: MutableMatrix,, a *note number: Number,, a *note ring element:
        RingElement,, or a *note vector: Vector,,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note number: Number,, The $\ell^p$-norm of M, computed to the
        minimum of the precisions of M and p.

Description
===========

By default, the $\ell^\infty$ norm giving the maximum absolute entry is
returned.

+------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : printingPrecision = 2                      |
|                                                |
|o1 = 2                                          |
+------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : R = RR_100                                 |
|                                                |
|o2 = RR                                         |
|       100                                      |
|                                                |
|o2 : RealField                                  |
+------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : M = 10*random(R^3,R^10)                    |
|                                                |
|o3 = | 2   5.6 1.9 4.1 3.5 2.6 6.1 5.3 .83 .44 ||
|     | 2   3.7 4.1 6.1 4.7 1.2 9   7.4 .95 1.5 ||
|     | 7.4 1.5 4.3 3.4 6.9 6   9.5 10  4.8 8   ||
|                                                |
|                 3          10                  |
|o3 : Matrix RR     <-- RR                       |
|              100        100                    |
+------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : norm M                                     |
|                                                |
|o4 = 9.952219758672894287555632834552           |
|                                                |
|o4 : RR (of precision 100)                      |
+------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : norm_(numeric_20 infinity) M               |
|                                                |
|o5 = 9.952225                                   |
|                                                |
|o5 : RR (of precision 20)                       |
+------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : norm {3/2,4,-5}                            |
|                                                |
|o6 = 5                                          |
|                                                |
|o6 : QQ                                         |
+------------------------------------------------+

For finite $p$, the $\ell^p$ norm $$\|M\|_p = \left(\sum_{i,j}
|M_{ij}|^p\right)^{1/p}$$ is returned.  For example, when $p = 2$, this is the
Frobenius norm.

+----------------------------------+
|i7 : M = matrix {{3, 4}, {12, 84}}|
|                                  |
|o7 = | 3  4  |                    |
|     | 12 84 |                    |
|                                  |
|              2       2           |
|o7 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ            |
+----------------------------------+
|i8 : norm_2 M                     |
|                                  |
|o8 = 85                           |
|                                  |
|o8 : RR (of precision 53)         |
+----------------------------------+

The norm of a polynomial is the norm of the vector of its coefficients.

+-------------------------------------+
|i9 : RR[x]                           |
|                                     |
|o9 = RR  [x]                         |
|       53                            |
|                                     |
|o9 : PolynomialRing                  |
+-------------------------------------+
|i10 : (1+x)^5                        |
|                                     |
|       5     4      3      2         |
|o10 = x  + 5x  + 10x  + 10x  + 5x + 1|
|                                     |
|o10 : RR  [x]                        |
|        53                           |
+-------------------------------------+
|i11 : norm oo                        |
|                                     |
|o11 = 10                             |
|                                     |
|o11 : RR (of precision 53)           |
+-------------------------------------+

Ways to use norm:
