Pseudocode -- the class of pseudocodes
**************************************

Description
===========

The Macaulay2 interpreter compiles its language into pseudocode, which is
evaluated later, step by step.  At each step, the evaluator is considering a
pseudocode item.  These pseudocode items are normally not available to the
user, but the internal function *note pseudocode: pseudocode, can convert a
function closure to pseudocode and display their contents, the function *note
value: value, can evaluate it (bindings of values to local symbols are enclosed
with the pseudocode), the operator *note ===: ===, can be used for equality
testing, and when the debugger is activated after an error, the variable *note
current: current, contains the pseudocode step whose execution produced the
error.

See also
========

  * *note disassemble: disassemble, -- disassemble a pseudocode or function
* Menu:

* value(Pseudocode): value_lpPseudocode_rp.  execute pseudocode

Types of Pseudocode:
====================

  * "PseudocodeClosure"

Methods that use an object of class Pseudocode:
===============================================

  * "code(Pseudocode)" -- see *note code: code, -- display source code
  * "disassemble(Pseudocode)" -- see *note disassemble: disassemble, --
    disassemble a pseudocode or function
  * "locate(Pseudocode)" -- see *note locate: locate, -- locate source code
  * toList(Pseudocode) (missing documentation)
  * *note value(Pseudocode): value_lpPseudocode_rp, -- execute pseudocode

For the programmer
==================

The object *note Pseudocode: Pseudocode, is a *note type: Type,, with ancestor
class *note Thing: Thing,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_repl.m2:992:0.

