tensor(Monoid,Monoid) -- tensor product of monoids
**************************************************

  * Function: *note tensor: tensor,
  * Usage: 
        C = A ** B
        C = tensor(A, B, ...)
  * Inputs:
      * A, a *note ring: Ring, or a *note monoid: Monoid,, 
      * B, a *note ring: Ring, or a *note monoid: Monoid,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * DegreeRank => an *note integer: ZZ,, default value null, see *note
        monoid(...,DegreeRank=>...):
        monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegrees=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * Degrees => a *note list: List,, default value null, see *note
        monoid(...,Degrees=>...): monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegrees=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * Inverses => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value null, see
        *note monoid(...,Inverses=>...):
        monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmInverses=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * Global => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value true, see
        *note monoid(...,Global=>...):
        monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmLocal=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * Local => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value false, see
        *note monoid(...,Local=>...):
        monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmLocal=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * MonomialOrder => a *note list: List,, default value null, see *note
        monoid(...,MonomialOrder=>...):
        monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmMonomialOrder=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * MonomialSize => an *note integer: ZZ,, default value 32, see *note
        monoid(...,MonomialSize=>...):
        monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmMonomialSize=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * Variables => a *note list: List,, default value null, see *note
        monoid(...,Variables=>...):
        monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVariables=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * VariableBaseName => a *note symbol: Symbol,, default value null, see
        *note monoid(...,VariableBaseName=>...):
        monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVariables=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * Heft => a *note list: List,, default value null, see *note
        monoid(...,Heft=>...): monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmHeft=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * Join => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value null, overrides
        the corresponding option in A; see *note monoid(...,Join=>...):
        monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmJoin=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * DegreeMap => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value null,
        overrides the corresponding option in A; see *note
        monoid(...,DegreeMap=>...):
        monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeLift=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * DegreeLift => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value null,
        overrides the corresponding option in A; see *note
        monoid(...,DegreeLift=>...):
        monoid_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeLift=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,;
      * Constants => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value false,
        ignored by this routine;
      * SkewCommutative => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value {},
        ignored by this routine;
      * WeylAlgebra => a *note list: List,, default value {}, ignored by this
        routine;
      * Weights => a *note list: List,, default value {}, ignored by this
        routine;
      * DegreeGroup => ..., default value null
  * Outputs:
      * C, a *note ring: Ring, or a *note monoid: Monoid,, tensor product of
        the monoids or rings

Description
===========

This is the same as A ** B except that options are allowed, see *note Monoid **
Monoid: tensor_lpMonoid_cmMonoid_rp, and *note Ring ** Ring:
tensor_lpMonoid_cmMonoid_rp,. This method allows many of the options available
for monoids, see *note monoid: monoid, for details. This method essentially
combines the variables of A and B into one monoid or ring.

+-------------------+
|i1 : kk = ZZ/101   |
|                   |
|o1 = kk            |
|                   |
|o1 : QuotientRing  |
+-------------------+
|i2 : A = kk[a,b]   |
|                   |
|o2 = A             |
|                   |
|o2 : PolynomialRing|
+-------------------+
|i3 : B = kk[c,d,e] |
|                   |
|o3 = B             |
|                   |
|o3 : PolynomialRing|
+-------------------+

The simplest version is to simply use *note **: _st_st,:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : describe(A**B)                                                          |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = kk[a..e, Degrees => {2:{1}, 3:{0}}, Heft => {2:1}, MonomialOrder =>     |
|                            {0}    {1}                                       |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|{MonomialSize => 32}]                                                        |
|{GRevLex => {2:1}  }                                                         |
|{Position => Up    }                                                         |
|{GRevLex => {3:1}  }                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

If you wish to change the variable names:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : describe tensor(A, B, VariableBaseName => p)                            |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = kk[p ..p , Degrees => {2:{1}, 3:{0}}, Heft => {2:1}, MonomialOrder =>   |
|         0   4                {0}    {1}                                     |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|{MonomialSize => 32}]                                                        |
|{GRevLex => {2:1}  }                                                         |
|{Position => Up    }                                                         |
|{GRevLex => {3:1}  }                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : describe tensor(A, B, Variables => {a1,a2,b1,b2,b3})                    |
|                                                                             |
|o6 = kk[a1, a2, b1, b2, b3, Degrees => {2:{1}, 3:{0}}, Heft => {2:1},        |
|                                          {0}    {1}                         |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|MonomialOrder => {MonomialSize => 32}]                                       |
|                 {GRevLex => {2:1}  }                                        |
|                 {Position => Up    }                                        |
|                 {GRevLex => {3:1}  }                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The tensor product of two singly graded rings is bigraded. Sometimes you want a
singly graded ring. Here is one way to get it:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : describe (C = tensor(A, B,                                              |
|                                                                             |
|o7 = kk[a..e, Degrees => {5:1},                                              |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|DegreeRank => 1, Degrees => {5:1}))                                          |
|                                                                             |
|Heft => {1}, MonomialOrder => {MonomialSize => 32}]                          |
|                              {GRevLex => {2:1}  }                           |
|                              {Position => Up    }                           |
|                              {GRevLex => {3:1}  }                           |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : degreeLength C                                                          |
|                                                                             |
|o8 = 1                                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : degreesRing C                                                           |
|                                                                             |
|o9 = ZZ[T]                                                                   |
|                                                                             |
|o9 : PolynomialRing                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Packing monomials into smaller space is more efficient, but less flexible. The
default is 32 bits, so if you want to pack them into 8 bit exponents, use:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : describe tensor(A, B, MonomialSize => 8)                               |
|                                                                             |
|o10 = kk[a..e, Degrees => {2:{1}, 3:{0}}, Heft => {2:1}, MonomialOrder =>    |
|                             {0}    {1}                                      |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|{MonomialSize => 8 }]                                                        |
|{MonomialSize => 32}                                                         |
|{GRevLex => {2:1}  }                                                         |
|{Position => Up    }                                                         |
|{GRevLex => {3:1}  }                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The default monomial order for tensor products is a product order. Sometimes
other orders are more desirable, e.g. GRevLex, or an elimination order:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : describe (C = tensor(A, B, MonomialOrder => Eliminate numgens A))      |
|                                                                             |
|o11 = kk[a..e, Degrees => {2:{1}, 3:{0}}, Heft => {2:1}, MonomialOrder =>    |
|                             {0}    {1}                                      |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|{MonomialSize => 32}]                                                        |
|{Weights => {2:1}  }                                                         |
|{GRevLex => {5:1}  }                                                         |
|{Position => Up    }                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : describe (C = tensor(A, B, MonomialOrder => GRevLex))                  |
|                                                                             |
|o12 = kk[a..e, Degrees => {2:{1}, 3:{0}}, Heft => {2:1}]                     |
|                             {0}    {1}                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

If you tensor two skew-commutative rings, (or one skew commutative ring with a
commutative polynomial ring), then all of the skew-commuting variables skew
commute with each other:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : As = kk[a, b, SkewCommutative => true]                                 |
|                                                                             |
|o13 = As                                                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o13 : PolynomialRing, 2 skew commutative variable(s)                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : D  = kk[c, d, e, SkewCommutative => true]                              |
|                                                                             |
|o14 = D                                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|o14 : PolynomialRing, 3 skew commutative variable(s)                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i15 : E = tensor(As, D)                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|o15 = E                                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|o15 : PolynomialRing, 5 skew commutative variable(s)                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i16 : describe E                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|o16 = kk[a..e, Degrees => {2:{1}, 3:{0}}, Heft => {2:1}, MonomialOrder =>    |
|                             {0}    {1}                                      |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|{MonomialSize => 32}, SkewCommutative => {0..4}]                             |
|{GRevLex => {2:1}  }                                                         |
|{Position => Up    }                                                         |
|{GRevLex => {3:1}  }                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i17 : c * a                                                                  |
|                                                                             |
|o17 = -a*c                                                                   |
|                                                                             |
|o17 : E                                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Similarly, tensoring two Weyl algebras (or one and a polynomial ring) produces
a Weyl algebra with both sets of non-commuting pairs.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i18 : E = kk[x, Dx, WeylAlgebra => {x => Dx}]                                |
|                                                                             |
|o18 = E                                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|o18 : PolynomialRing, 1 differential variable(s)                             |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i19 : tensor(E, E, Variables => {x, Dx, y, Dy})                              |
|                                                                             |
|o19 = kk[x, Dx, y, Dy]                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o19 : PolynomialRing, 2 differential variable(s)                             |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i20 : describe oo                                                            |
|                                                                             |
|o20 = kk[x, Dx, y, Dy, Degrees => {2:{1}, 2:{0}}, Heft => {2:1},             |
|                                     {0}    {1}                              |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|MonomialOrder => {MonomialSize => 32}, WeylAlgebra => {{x, Dx}, {y, Dy}}]    |
|                 {GRevLex => {2:1}  }                                        |
|                 {Position => Up    }                                        |
|                 {GRevLex => {2:1}  }                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Two polynomial rings must have the same coefficient ring, otherwise an error is
issued. Currently, there is no way to specify other rings over which to define
the tensor product.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i21 : A = ZZ/101[a, b]                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o21 = A                                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|o21 : PolynomialRing                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i22 : B = A[x, y]                                                            |
|                                                                             |
|o22 = B                                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|o22 : PolynomialRing                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i23 : C = tensor(B, B, Variables => {x1, y1, x2, y2})                        |
|                                                                             |
|o23 = C                                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|o23 : PolynomialRing                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i24 : describe C                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|o24 = A[x1, y1, x2, y2, Degrees => {2:{1}, 2:{0}}, Heft => {2:1},            |
|                                      {0}    {1}                             |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|MonomialOrder => {MonomialSize => 32}]                                       |
|                 {GRevLex => {2:1}  }                                        |
|                 {Position => Up    }                                        |
|                 {GRevLex => {2:1}  }                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The flat monoid with the all variables visible, including those from the base
ring, can be obtained as follows.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i25 : C.FlatMonoid                                                           |
|                                                                             |
|o25 = monoid[x1, y1, x2, y2, a..b, Degrees => {2:{1}, 2:{0}, 2:{0}}, Heft => |
|                                                 {0}    {1}    {0}           |
|                                                 {0}    {0}    {1}           |
|                                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|o25 : GeneralOrderedMonoid                                                   |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|{3:1}, MonomialOrder => {MonomialSize => 32   }]                             |
|                        {GRevLex => {2:1}     }                              |
|                        {Position => Up       }                              |
|                        {2:(GRevLex => {1, 1})}                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Not all of the options for monoid are useful here. Some are silently ignored.

See also
========

  * *note describe: describe, -- real description
  * *note degreesRing: degreesRing, -- the ring or monoid of degrees
  * *note degreeLength: degreeLength, -- the length of the degree vector
  * *note **: _st_st, -- a binary operator, usually used for tensor product or
    Cartesian product
  * *note FlatMonoid: FlatMonoid,

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note tensor(Monoid,Monoid): tensor_lpMonoid_cmMonoid_rp, -- tensor product
    of monoids
  * "tensor(Ring,Ring)"
