EngineRing -- the class of rings handled by the engine
******************************************************

Description
===========

Typically, *note the engine of Macaulay2: the engine of Macaulay2, handles the
rings in the system.


The command new EngineRing from x is not meant for general users, and provides
the developers with a way to create top-level rings corresponding to rings
implemented in the engine.  Here x may be:
  * commands for the engine, as a string, or a sequence or list of strings,
    which cause a ring to be placed on the top of the engine's stack.
  * a ring, in which case another top-level ring is formed as an interface to
    the same underlying engine ring.
  * the handle of on engine ring
* Menu:

* Engine::                      specify whether a ring is handled by the engine
* InexactField::                the class of inexact fields
* GaloisField::                 the class of all Galois fields
* FractionField::               the class of all fraction fields
* PolynomialRing::              the class of all ordered monoid rings
* QuotientRing::                the class of all quotient rings

Types of engine ring:
=====================

  * *note FractionField: FractionField, -- the class of all fraction fields
  * *note GaloisField: GaloisField, -- the class of all Galois fields
  * *note InexactField: InexactField, -- the class of inexact fields
  * *note PolynomialRing: PolynomialRing, -- the class of all ordered monoid
    rings
  * *note QuotientRing: QuotientRing, -- the class of all quotient rings

Methods that use an engine ring:
================================

  * "frac(EngineRing)" -- see *note frac: frac, -- construct a fraction field
  * "isField(EngineRing)" -- see *note isField: isField, -- whether something
    is a field
  * "numgens(EngineRing)" -- see *note numgens(Ring): numgens_lpRing_rp, --
    number of generators of a polynomial ring

