File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: finite fields, Next: polynomial rings, Prev: integers modulo a prime, Up: rings

finite fields
*************

Two basic finite fields are:
  * *note ZZ/p: integers modulo a prime,
  * *note GF(p^n): GF,
Create a finite field with $q = p^n$ elements using
+---------------------------+
|i1 : F = GF(81,Variable=>a)|
|                           |
|o1 = F                     |
|                           |
|o1 : GaloisField           |
+---------------------------+
This creates the ring of characteristic 3, having 3^4 = 81 elements.  The
elements of this ring are 0, a, a^2, a^3, ..., a^80.
+---------+
|i2 : a^80|
|         |
|o2 = 1   |
|         |
|o2 : F   |
+---------+
|i3 : a^40|
|         |
|o3 = -1  |
|         |
|o3 : F   |
+---------+
Use *note ambient: ambient, to see the quotient ring the field is made from.
+-----------------+
|i4 : ambient F   |
|                 |
|        ZZ       |
|        --[a]    |
|         3       |
|o4 = ----------- |
|      4    3     |
|     a  - a  - 1 |
|                 |
|o4 : QuotientRing|
+-----------------+
Now check that a satisfies this equation.
+----------------+
|i5 : a^4 + a - 1|
|                |
|      3         |
|o5 = a  + a     |
|                |
|o5 : F          |
+----------------+
It is often preferable to view elements of F as polynomials in a rather than as
powers of a.  This can be accomplished by lifting the elements back to this
ambient ring.
+-------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : lift(a^20, ambient F)                       |
|                                                 |
|        3    2                                   |
|o6 = - a  - a  - 1                               |
|                                                 |
|        ZZ                                       |
|        --[a]                                    |
|         3                                       |
|o6 : -----------                                 |
|      4    3                                     |
|     a  - a  - 1                                 |
+-------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : apply({20,40,80}, i -> lift(a^i, ambient F))|
|                                                 |
|         3    2                                  |
|o7 = {- a  - a  - 1, -1, 1}                      |
|                                                 |
|o7 : List                                        |
+-------------------------------------------------+
(for more details on lift, see *note working with multiple rings: working with
multiple rings,).


Finite fields can be used as base rings for polynomial rings.
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : R = F[x,y,z]                                                            |
|                                                                             |
|o8 = R                                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o8 : PolynomialRing                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : f = random(2,R)                                                         |
|                                                                             |
|         3      2    2          3    2          2     3                 3    |
|o9 = (- a  + a)x  + a x*y + (- a  - a  - a - 1)y  + (a  + a + 1)x*z + (a  -  |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|      2               3    2          2                                      |
|     a  - a)y*z + (- a  - a  + a - 1)z                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o9 : R                                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : f = (leadCoefficient f)^(-1) * f                                       |
|                                                                             |
|       2       2                 3    2      2       3             2         |
|o10 = x  + (- a  + a - 1)x*y + (a  + a  + 1)y  + (- a  - a)x*z + (a  - a)y*z |
|      -----------------------------------------------------------------------|
|            3      2                                                         |
|      + (- a  + a)z                                                          |
|                                                                             |
|o10 : R                                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
Gröbner bases, and all related computations work in these rings.


The prime finite fields can be made easily as quotient rings of *note ZZ: ZZ,.
+------------------+
|i11 : ZZ/101      |
|                  |
|       ZZ         |
|o11 = ---         |
|      101         |
|                  |
|o11 : QuotientRing|
+------------------+
In general, to make a finite field with q elements, we use *note GF: GF,.
+-----------------+
|i12 : k = GF 81  |
|                 |
|o12 = k          |
|                 |
|o12 : GaloisField|
+-----------------+
The generator of the field is available as the variable a or it can be obtained
as usual.
+---------+
|i13 : a  |
|         |
|o13 = a  |
|         |
|o13 : k  |
+---------+
|i14 : k_0|
|         |
|o14 = a  |
|         |
|o14 : k  |
+---------+
You may use *note ambient: ambient, to see the quotient ring the field is made
from.
+------------------+
|i15 : ambient k   |
|                  |
|         ZZ       |
|         --[a]    |
|          3       |
|o15 = ----------- |
|       4    3     |
|      a  - a  - 1 |
|                  |
|o15 : QuotientRing|
+------------------+
Use *note ideal: ideal, to see the ideal that defined that quotient ring.
+------------------------+
|i16 : ideal oo          |
|                        |
|             4    3     |
|o16 = ideal(a  - a  - 1)|
|                        |
|               ZZ       |
|o16 : Ideal of --[a]    |
|                3       |
+------------------------+
Finally, you may use *note _: _us, to recover the generator of the ideal.
+-----------------+
|i17 : oo_0       |
|                 |
|       4    3    |
|o17 = a  - a  - 1|
|                 |
|      ZZ         |
|o17 : --[a]      |
|       3         |
+-----------------+
To specify a different name for the generator when the field is created, use
the *note Variable: Variable, option.
+-------------------------------+
|i18 : F = GF(16, Variable => b)|
|                               |
|o18 = F                        |
|                               |
|o18 : GaloisField              |
+-------------------------------+
|i19 : b^20 + 1                 |
|                               |
|       2                       |
|o19 = b  + b + 1               |
|                               |
|o19 : F                        |
+-------------------------------+
|i20 : random F                 |
|                               |
|o20 = 0                        |
|                               |
|o20 : F                        |
+-------------------------------+
Finite fields can be used as base rings for polynomial rings.
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i21 : R = F[x,y,z]                                                           |
|                                                                             |
|o21 = R                                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|o21 : PolynomialRing                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i22 : random(2,R)                                                            |
|                                                                             |
|        3          2     3    2             2          2     2               |
|o22 = (b  + b + 1)x  + (b  + b  + 1)x*y + (b  + b + 1)y  + (b  + b + 1)x*z + |
|      -----------------------------------------------------------------------|
|                3    2      2                                                |
|      b*y*z + (b  + b  + b)z                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|o22 : R                                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
If you have a quotient ring that you know is a finite field, then you can
convert it to ring that is known by the system to be a finite field.
+--------------------------------------------+
|i23 : GF (ZZ/2[T]/(T^9+T+1), Variable => T) |
|                                            |
|o23 = GF 512                                |
|                                            |
|o23 : GaloisField                           |
+--------------------------------------------+
You may also provide your own choice of primitive element.  Internally,
elements of the finite field are stored as powers of the primitive element.
First we assign our quotient ring to a global variable to ensure that T gets
set to a value in the quotient ring, and then we call GF.
+-------------------------------------------+
|i24 : A = ZZ/2[T]/(T^9+T+1)                |
|                                           |
|o24 = A                                    |
|                                           |
|o24 : QuotientRing                         |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i25 : k = GF (A, PrimitiveElement => T^3+1)|
|                                           |
|o25 = k                                    |
|                                           |
|o25 : GaloisField                          |
+-------------------------------------------+
Notice that T is now recorded as an element of this finite field.
+-------+
|i26 : T|
|       |
|o26 = T|
|       |
|o26 : k|
+-------+
The generator of A can be obtained this way:
+---------+
|i27 : A_0|
|         |
|o27 = T  |
|         |
|o27 : A  |
+---------+
Use *note substitute: substitute, to map it to an element of the finite field.
+-----------------------+
|i28 : substitute(A_0,k)|
|                       |
|o28 = T                |
|                       |
|o28 : k                |
+-----------------------+
Conversely, a given element of the finite field can be transferred back to the
quotient ring with *note lift: lift,.
+-----------------------+
|i29 : lift(k_0, ring T)|
|                       |
|o29 = T                |
|                       |
|o29 : k                |
+-----------------------+
We can even lift it back to the polynomial ring.
+-------------------------------+
|i30 : lift(k_0, ambient ring T)|
|                               |
|o30 = T                        |
|                               |
|o30 : A                        |
+-------------------------------+
For more information see *note GaloisField: GaloisField,.
* Menu:

* isFinitePrimeField::          whether a ring is a finite prime field
