File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: degree, Next: homogenize, Up: manipulating polynomials

degree
******

Description
===========

Degree is a common name, meaning different things for different kinds of
mathematical objects. In Macaulay2, there are currently three related, yet
different notions of degree:

Degree of polynomials or vectors of such
----------------------------------------

  * *note degree(RingElement): degree_lpRingElement_rp,
  * *note degree(Vector): degree_lpRingElement_rp,
  * *note degree(RingElement,RingElement):
    degree_lpRingElement_cmRingElement_rp, -- degree with respect to a variable
  * *note degree(ProjectiveHilbertPolynomial):
    degree_lpProjectiveHilbertPolynomial_rp,

Degree of ideals, varieties and modules
---------------------------------------

  * *note degree(Ideal): degree_lpIdeal_rp,
  * *note degree(Ring): degree_lpRing_rp,
  * *note degree(Module): degree_lpModule_rp, -- get the degree of a module
  * *note degree(ProjectiveVariety): (Varieties)degree_lpProjectiveVariety_rp,

Degree of homomorphisms
-----------------------

  * degree(Matrix) (missing documentation)

See also
========

  * *note degreeLength: degreeLength, -- the length of the degree vector
  * *note degreesRing: degreesRing, -- the ring or monoid of degrees

Ways to use degree:
===================

  * "degree(BettiTally)" -- see *note BettiTally: BettiTally, -- the class of
    all Betti tallies
  * *note degree(Ideal): degree_lpIdeal_rp,
  * degree(Matrix) (missing documentation)
  * *note degree(Module): degree_lpModule_rp, -- get the degree of a module
  * degree(MonoidElement) (missing documentation)
  * *note degree(ProjectiveHilbertPolynomial):
    degree_lpProjectiveHilbertPolynomial_rp,
  * *note degree(Ring): degree_lpRing_rp,
  * *note degree(RingElement): degree_lpRingElement_rp,
  * "degree(Vector)" -- see *note degree(RingElement): degree_lpRingElement_rp,
  * *note degree(RingElement,RingElement):
    degree_lpRingElement_cmRingElement_rp, -- degree with respect to a variable

For the programmer
==================

The object *note degree: degree, is a *note method function: MethodFunction,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/functions/degree-doc.m2:46:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: homogenize, Next: weightRange, Prev: degree, Up: manipulating polynomials

homogenize -- homogenize with respect to a variable
***************************************************

Description
===========

homogenize(m,v) -- homogenize the ring element, vector, matrix, or module m
using the variable v in the ring of m.
homogenize(m,v,w) -- homogenize m using the variable v, so that the result is
homogeneous with respect to the given list w of integers provided as weights
for the variables.


+-----------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/101[x,y,z,Degrees => {1,2,3}]|
|                                         |
|o1 = R                                   |
|                                         |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                      |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i2 : f = 1 + y + z^2                     |
|                                         |
|      2                                  |
|o2 = z  + y + 1                          |
|                                         |
|o2 : R                                   |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i3 : homogenize(f,x)                     |
|                                         |
|      6    4     2                       |
|o3 = x  + x y + z                        |
|                                         |
|o3 : R                                   |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i4 : homogenize(f,x,{1,0,-1})            |
|                                         |
|      2 2                                |
|o4 = x z  + y + 1                        |
|                                         |
|o4 : R                                   |
+-----------------------------------------+


The weights that may be used are limited (roughly) to the range -2^30 .. 2^30.


Caveat
======

If the homogenization overflows the monomial, this is not reported as an error.

Ways to use homogenize:
=======================

  * "homogenize(Ideal,RingElement)"
  * "homogenize(Matrix,RingElement)"
  * "homogenize(Matrix,RingElement,List)"
  * "homogenize(Module,RingElement)"
  * "homogenize(Module,RingElement,List)"
  * "homogenize(RingElement,RingElement)"
  * "homogenize(RingElement,RingElement,List)"
  * "homogenize(Vector,RingElement)"
  * "homogenize(Vector,RingElement,List)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note homogenize: homogenize, is a *note method function:
MethodFunction,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

