Macaulay2Doc/functions/homogenize-doc.m2:37:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: weightRange, Next: monomials, Prev: homogenize, Up: manipulating polynomials

weightRange -- the pair of lowest and highest weights of the monomials
**********************************************************************

Description
===========

Synopsis
========

  * Usage: 
        (lo,hi) = weightRange(w,f)
  * Inputs:
      * w, a *note list: List,, of integers, the weight of each variable
      * f, a *note ring element: RingElement,, in a polynomial ring
  * Outputs:
      * lo, an *note integer: ZZ,, the least weight of the monomials of f
      * hi, an *note integer: ZZ,, the greatest weight of the monomials of f
The weight of a monomial is the dot product of w and the exponent vector. If
the weight vector has length smaller than the number of variables, the other
variables are assumed to have weight zero.  If there are too many weights
given, the extras are silently ignored.
+-----------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..g]                  |
|                                   |
|o1 = R                             |
|                                   |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                |
+-----------------------------------+
|i2 : f = a^3+b^2*c+3*f^10*d-1+e-e  |
|                                   |
|         10    3    2              |
|o2 = 3d*f   + a  + b c - 1         |
|                                   |
|o2 : R                             |
+-----------------------------------+
|i3 : weightRange({1,1,0,0,0,0,0},f)|
|                                   |
|o3 = (0, 3)                        |
|                                   |
|o3 : Sequence                      |
+-----------------------------------+
Use  *note terms: terms, and *note weightRange: weightRange, together to select
the terms which have a given weight.
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : f = a^2*b+3*a^2*c+b*c+1                                |
|                                                            |
|      2      2                                              |
|o4 = a b + 3a c + b*c + 1                                   |
|                                                            |
|o4 : R                                                      |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : sum select(terms f, t -> (weightRange({1,0},t))#0 == 2)|
|                                                            |
|      2      2                                              |
|o5 = a b + 3a c                                             |
|                                                            |
|o5 : R                                                      |
+------------------------------------------------------------+
If the coefficient ring is a polynomial ring, one can use the weights of these
variables too.  The order of weights is the same as the order of variables (see
*note index: index,)
+---------------------------------------+
|i6 : S = R[x,y];                       |
+---------------------------------------+
|i7 : weightRange({0,0,3,7},a*x^2+b*x*y)|
|                                       |
|o7 = (3, 7)                            |
|                                       |
|o7 : Sequence                          |
+---------------------------------------+

Synopsis
========

  * Usage: 
        (lo,hi) = weightRange f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring element: RingElement,
  * Outputs:
      * lo, an *note integer: ZZ,, the minimum value for (the first component
        of) the degrees of the monomials occurring in f
      * hi, an *note integer: ZZ,, the corresponding maximum value
+----------------------------+
|i8 : R = QQ[x,y];           |
+----------------------------+
|i9 : weightRange (x^3+y^2)^5|
|                            |
|o9 = (10, 15)               |
|                            |
|o9 : Sequence               |
+----------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note degree: degree,
  * *note homogenize: homogenize, -- homogenize with respect to a variable
  * *note part: part, -- select terms of a polynomial by degree(s) or weight(s)
  * *note index: index, -- numeric index of a ring variable

Ways to use weightRange:
========================

  * "weightRange(List,RingElement)"
  * "weightRange(RingElement)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note weightRange: weightRange, is a *note method function:
MethodFunction,.

