index -- numeric index of a ring variable
*****************************************

  * Usage: 
        index v
  * Inputs:
      * v, a *note ring element: RingElement,
  * Outputs:
      * an *note integer: ZZ,, the integer index, starting at 0, of v, if it is
        a variable. If v is not a variable, then *note null: null, is returned

Description
===========

Variables are indexed in the following way: the first variable has index 0, the
second index 1, and so on, until n-1, where n is the number of generators of R,
the ring of v.  Then, if the coefficient ring is a polynomial ring, those
variables are numbered starting at n, and so on.
+-----------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/101[a..d,t]|
|                       |
|o1 = R                 |
|                       |
|o1 : PolynomialRing    |
+-----------------------+
|i2 : index a           |
|                       |
|o2 = 0                 |
+-----------------------+
|i3 : index t           |
|                       |
|o3 = 4                 |
+-----------------------+
+---------------------------------------------+
|i4 : A = ZZ[a..d]; B = A[r,s,t]; C = B[x,y,z]|
|                                             |
|o6 = C                                       |
|                                             |
|o6 : PolynomialRing                          |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i7 : index x                                 |
|                                             |
|o7 = 0                                       |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i8 : index z                                 |
|                                             |
|o8 = 2                                       |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i9 : index r                                 |
|                                             |
|o9 = 0                                       |
+---------------------------------------------+
Notice that r is an element of B, and so indices are taken from that ring. If
we consider r as an element of C, we get a different answer.  Variables of
coefficient rings of coefficient rings have an index too.
+------------------+
|i10 : index(r*1_C)|
|                  |
|o10 = 3           |
+------------------+
|i11 : index(b*1_C)|
|                  |
|o11 = 7           |
+------------------+


The symbol index is also as a key used in *note Monoids: Monoid, to store a
table that is used to map generator names to the position of the generator in
the list of generators.

See also
========

  * *note indices: indices, -- indices of a polynomial; also components for a
    direct sum
  * *note support: support, -- list of variables occurring in a polynomial or
    matrix
  * *note Ring _ ZZ: get a ring variable by index, -- get a ring variable by
    index

Ways to use index:
==================

  * "index(RingElement)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note index: index, is a *note method function: MethodFunction,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/functions/index-doc.m2:44:0.

