File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: use_lpRing_rp, Next: lift, Up: working with multiple rings

use(Ring) -- install ring variables and ring operations
*******************************************************

  * Function: *note use: use,
  * Usage: 
        use R
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: Ring, or a *note monoid: Monoid,
  * Consequences:
      * All variables of R are set to global variables.  Additionally, certain
        operations creating elements of R are installed globally.

Description
===========

When a ring (or a monoid) is assigned to a global variable, this function is
automatically called for it.


It is possible to have several polynomial rings defined, perhaps with a
variable belonging to several rings.
+--------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..d]   |
|                    |
|o1 = R              |
|                    |
|o1 : PolynomialRing |
+--------------------+
|i2 : S = QQ[b,c,d,e]|
|                    |
|o2 = S              |
|                    |
|o2 : PolynomialRing |
+--------------------+
|i3 : b              |
|                    |
|o3 = b              |
|                    |
|o3 : S              |
+--------------------+
At this point, b is thought to be a variable of S.  If one typed a+b, an error
would occur, since Macaulay2 doesn't know how to add elements of R and S
together.  This is fixed via:
+-------------------+
|i4 : use R         |
|                   |
|o4 = R             |
|                   |
|o4 : PolynomialRing|
+-------------------+
|i5 : b             |
|                   |
|o5 = b             |
|                   |
|o5 : R             |
+-------------------+
|i6 : a+b           |
|                   |
|o6 = a + b         |
|                   |
|o6 : R             |
+-------------------+


There are several functions that create rings for you.  Generally, their
variables are not globally visible.  However, once you 'use' the ring, the
variables are available.For example, the numerator of the Hilbert function is a
polynomial in a ring with a variable T.
+-------------------------------+
|i7 : T                         |
|                               |
|o7 = T                         |
|                               |
|o7 : Symbol                    |
+-------------------------------+
|i8 : hf = poincare ideal vars S|
|                               |
|                2     3    4   |
|o8 = 1 - 4T + 6T  - 4T  + T    |
|                               |
|o8 : ZZ[T]                     |
+-------------------------------+
|i9 : T                         |
|                               |
|o9 = T                         |
|                               |
|o9 : Symbol                    |
+-------------------------------+
|i10 : use ring hf              |
|                               |
|o10 = ZZ[T]                    |
|                               |
|o10 : PolynomialRing           |
+-------------------------------+
|i11 : T                        |
|                               |
|o11 = T                        |
|                               |
|o11 : Symbol                   |
+-------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Any values stored in the variables that have been assigned to are lost, hence
this operation should not be used by code in a package.

See also
========

  * *note GlobalAssignHook: GlobalAssignHook, -- hook for assignment to global
    variables

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * "use(Monoid)"
  * *note use(Ring): use_lpRing_rp, -- install ring variables and ring
    operations
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/functions/use-doc.m2:79:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: lift, Next: promote, Prev: use_lpRing_rp, Up: working with multiple rings

lift -- lift to another ring
****************************

  * Usage: 
        lift(f,R)
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring element: RingElement,, *note ideal: Ideal,, or *note
        matrix: Matrix,
      * R, a *note ring: Ring,
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Verify => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value true, whether
        to give an error message if lifting is not possible, or, alternatively,
        to return *note null: null,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring element: RingElement,, *note ideal: Ideal,, or *note
        matrix: Matrix,, over the ring R

Description
===========

(Disambiguation: for division of matrices, which is thought of as lifting one
homomorphism over another, see instead *note Matrix // Matrix:
quotient_lpMatrix_cmMatrix_rp,.  For lifting a map between modules to a map
between their free resolutions, see *note extend: extend,.)

The ring R should be one of the base rings associated with the ring of f.  An
error is raised if f cannot be lifted to R.

The first example is lifting from the fraction field of R to R.

+--------------------------------------+
|i1 : lift(4/2,ZZ)                     |
|                                      |
|o1 = 2                                |
+--------------------------------------+
|i2 : R = ZZ[x];                       |
+--------------------------------------+
|i3 : f = ((x+1)^3*(x+4))/((x+4)*(x+1))|
|                                      |
|      2                               |
|o3 = x  + 2x + 1                      |
|                                      |
|o3 : frac R                           |
+--------------------------------------+
|i4 : lift(f,R)                        |
|                                      |
|      2                               |
|o4 = x  + 2x + 1                      |
|                                      |
|o4 : R                                |
+--------------------------------------+

Another use of lift is to take polynomials in a quotient ring and lift them to
the polynomial ring.

+-------------------------------------------+
|i5 : A = QQ[a..d];                         |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i6 : B = A/(a^2-b,c^2-d-a-3);              |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i7 : f = c^5                               |
|                                           |
|                 2                         |
|o7 = 2a*c*d + c*d  + 6a*c + b*c + 6c*d + 9c|
|                                           |
|o7 : B                                     |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i8 : lift(f,A)                             |
|                                           |
|                 2                         |
|o8 = 2a*c*d + c*d  + 6a*c + b*c + 6c*d + 9c|
|                                           |
|o8 : A                                     |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i9 : jf = jacobian ideal f                 |
|                                           |
|o9 = {1} | 2cd+6c           |              |
|     {1} | c                |              |
|     {1} | 2ad+d2+6a+b+6d+9 |              |
|     {1} | 2ac+2cd+6c       |              |
|                                           |
|             4      1                      |
|o9 : Matrix B  <-- B                       |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i10 : lift(jf,A)                           |
|                                           |
|o10 = {1} | 2cd+6c           |             |
|      {1} | c                |             |
|      {1} | 2ad+d2+6a+b+6d+9 |             |
|      {1} | 2ac+2cd+6c       |             |
|                                           |
|              4      1                     |
|o10 : Matrix A  <-- A                      |
+-------------------------------------------+

Elements may be lifted to any base ring, if such a lift exists.

+-----------------------------+
|i11 : use B;                 |
+-----------------------------+
|i12 : g = (a^2+2*a-3)-(a+1)^2|
|                             |
|o12 = -4                     |
|                             |
|o12 : B                      |
+-----------------------------+
|i13 : lift(g,A)              |
|                             |
|o13 = -4                     |
|                             |
|o13 : A                      |
+-----------------------------+
|i14 : lift(g,QQ)             |
|                             |
|o14 = -4                     |
|                             |
|o14 : QQ                     |
+-----------------------------+
|i15 : lift(lift(g,QQ),ZZ)    |
|                             |
|o15 = -4                     |
+-----------------------------+

The functions lift and *note substitute: substitute, are useful to move numbers
from one kind of coefficient ring to another.

+------------------+
|i16 : lift(3.0,ZZ)|
|                  |
|o16 = 3           |
+------------------+
|i17 : lift(3.0,QQ)|
|                  |
|o17 = 3           |
|                  |
|o17 : QQ          |
+------------------+

A continued fraction method is used to lift a real number to a rational number.

+--------------------------------+
|i18 : lift(123/2341.,QQ)        |
|                                |
|       123                      |
|o18 = ----                      |
|      2341                      |
|                                |
|o18 : QQ                        |
+--------------------------------+
|i19 : factor oo                 |
|                                |
|      3*41                      |
|o19 = ----                      |
|      2341                      |
|                                |
|o19 : Expression of class Divide|
+--------------------------------+

For numbers and ring elements, an alternate syntax with *note ^: ^, is
available, analogous to the use of *note _: _us, for *note promote: promote,.

+--------------+
|i20 : .0001^QQ|
|              |
|        1     |
|o20 = -----   |
|      10000   |
|              |
|o20 : QQ      |
+--------------+

See also
========

  * *note baseRings: baseRings, -- store the list of base rings of a ring
  * *note promote: promote, -- promote to another ring
