RingMap RingElement -- apply a ring map
***************************************

  * Operator: *note SPACE: SPACE,
  * Usage: 
        f X
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring map: RingMap,, a ring map from R to S.
      * X, a *note ring element: RingElement,, an *note ideal: Ideal,, a *note
        matrix: Matrix,, a *note vector: Vector,, or a *note module: Module,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring element: RingElement,, the image of X under the ring map
        f.  The result has the same type as X, except that its ring will be S.

Description
===========

If X is a module then it must be either free or a submodule of a free module.
+------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y];             |
+------------------------------+
|i2 : S = QQ[t];               |
+------------------------------+
|i3 : f = map(S,R,{t^2,t^3})   |
|                              |
|                  2   3       |
|o3 = map (S, R, {t , t })     |
|                              |
|o3 : RingMap S <-- R          |
+------------------------------+
|i4 : f (x+y^2)                |
|                              |
|      6    2                  |
|o4 = t  + t                   |
|                              |
|o4 : S                        |
+------------------------------+
|i5 : f image vars R           |
|                              |
|o5 = image | t2 t3 |          |
|                              |
|                             1|
|o5 : S-module, submodule of S |
+------------------------------+
|i6 : f ideal (x^2,y^2)        |
|                              |
|             4   6            |
|o6 = ideal (t , t )           |
|                              |
|o6 : Ideal of S               |
+------------------------------+

Caveat
======

If the rings R and S have different degree monoids, then the degrees of the
image might need to be changed, since Macaulay2 sometimes doesn't have enough
information to determine the image degrees of elements of a free module.

See also
========

  * *note RingElement Sequence: RingElement Sequence, -- evaluation of
    polynomials

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * "RingMap Ideal"
  * "RingMap Matrix"
  * "RingMap Module"
  * *note RingMap RingElement: RingMap RingElement, -- apply a ring map
  * "RingMap Vector"
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

