graphIdeal(RingMap) -- the ideal of the graph of the regular map corresponding to a ring map
********************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note graphIdeal: graphIdeal_lpRingMap_rp,
  * Usage: 
        graphIdeal f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring map: RingMap,
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * MonomialOrder => ..., default value {GRevLex, Position => Up}, a
        monomial ordering, see *note monomial orderings: monomial orderings,
      * MonomialSize => an *note integer: ZZ,, default value 32, the monomial
        size, see *note MonomialSize: MonomialSize,
      * VariableBaseName => a *note symbol: Symbol,, default value "p", the
        variable base name, see *note VariableBaseName: VariableBaseName,
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: Ideal,, the ideal of the graph of regular map
        corresponding to f

Description
===========

+----------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y]                              |
|                                              |
|o1 = R                                        |
|                                              |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                           |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S = QQ[s,t,u]                            |
|                                              |
|o2 = S                                        |
|                                              |
|o2 : PolynomialRing                           |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i3 : f = map(R,S,{x^2,x*y,y^2})               |
|                                              |
|                  2        2                  |
|o3 = map (R, S, {x , x*y, y })                |
|                                              |
|o3 : RingMap R <-- S                          |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i4 : graphIdeal f                             |
|                                              |
|               2                       2      |
|o4 = ideal (- p  + p , - p p  + p , - p  + p )|
|               0    2     0 1    3     1    4 |
|                                              |
|o4 : Ideal of QQ[p ..p ]                      |
|                  0   4                       |
+----------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note graphRing: graphRing_lpRingMap_rp, -- the coordinate ring of the
    graph of the regular map corresponding to a ring map

Ways to use this method:
========================

