File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: antipode, Prev: isSkewCommutative, Up: exterior algebras

antipode -- antipode for skew commuting polynomial rings
********************************************************

  * Usage: 
        antipode f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring element: RingElement,, An element in any ring
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring element: RingElement,, An element in the same ring, the
        antipode of f

Description
===========

If the ring does not have skew commuting variables, antipode is the identify
function.

If the ring does have skew commuting variables, then the antipode returns a new
polynomial with the same monomials, and the coefficients have been modified to
provide an isomorphism with the ring, and its opposite ring, i.e. this switches
left and right modules of the ring.

This is implemented in the following simple manner: each monomial which has
exactly $d$ skew commuting variables occurring (thus, with exponent 1 each), is
multiplied by $(-1)^{(d-1)(d-2)/2}$.

+---------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/32003[a..f, SkewCommutative => true]   |
|                                                   |
|o1 = R                                             |
|                                                   |
|o1 : PolynomialRing, 6 skew commutative variable(s)|
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : F = 1 + a + b + a*b + b*c*d + a*b*c*d*e       |
|                                                   |
|o2 = a*b*c*d*e + b*c*d + a*b + a + b + 1           |
|                                                   |
|o2 : R                                             |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : antipode F                                    |
|                                                   |
|o3 = a*b*c*d*e - b*c*d - a*b + a + b + 1           |
|                                                   |
|o3 : R                                             |
+---------------------------------------------------+

If the ring has some skew commuting variables, and some commutative ones, then
only the skew ones are considered.

+---------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : S = ZZ/32003[a..f, SkewCommutative => {0,1,2}]|
|                                                   |
|o4 = S                                             |
|                                                   |
|o4 : PolynomialRing, 3 skew commutative variable(s)|
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : F = 1 + a + b + a*b + b*c*d + a*b*c*d*e       |
|                                                   |
|o5 = a*b*c*d*e + b*c*d + a*b + a + b + 1           |
|                                                   |
|o5 : S                                             |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : antipode F                                    |
|                                                   |
|o6 = - a*b*c*d*e - b*c*d - a*b + a + b + 1         |
|                                                   |
|o6 : S                                             |
+---------------------------------------------------+

The transpose of a matrix applies this function to each entry.  This is
basically because the transpose of a left module gives a right module, and we
need to make that a left module again.  The key reason for this is so that the
transpose of a complex remains a complex.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : use R                                                                   |
|                                                                             |
|o7 = R                                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o7 : PolynomialRing, 6 skew commutative variable(s)                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : M = matrix{{a*b-1, a*c-d, e-a*b*c}}                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o8 = | ab-1 ac-d -abc+e |                                                    |
|                                                                             |
|             1      3                                                        |
|o8 : Matrix R  <-- R                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : N = syz M                                                               |
|                                                                             |
|o9 = {2} | -ad 0  cd   be ae -abd+ac-d -abc-bd -bcd 2abd-ac+d abc-abe-e      |
|     {2} | a   cd bd-c 0  0  1         b       bc   -ab-1     0              |
|     {3} | 0   0  0    b  a  0         0       0    0         -1             |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     bde+ce abce |                                                           |
|     be     0    |                                                           |
|     c      e    |                                                           |
|                                                                             |
|             3      12                                                       |
|o9 : Matrix R  <-- R                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : M*N                                                                    |
|                                                                             |
|o10 = 0                                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|              1      12                                                      |
|o10 : Matrix R  <-- R                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : (transpose N) * (transpose M)                                          |
|                                                                             |
|o11 = 0                                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|              12      1                                                      |
|o11 : Matrix R   <-- R                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

If we had not used the antipode, then this would be false!

+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : Mt = matrix transpose entries M -- this does not use antipode|
|                                                                   |
|o12 = | ab-1   |                                                   |
|      | ac-d   |                                                   |
|      | -abc+e |                                                   |
|                                                                   |
|              3      1                                             |
|o12 : Matrix R  <-- R                                              |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : Nt = matrix transpose entries N -- this does not use antipode|
|                                                                   |
|o13 = | -ad       a     0  |                                       |
|      | 0         cd    0  |                                       |
|      | cd        bd-c  0  |                                       |
|      | be        0     b  |                                       |
|      | ae        0     a  |                                       |
|      | -abd+ac-d 1     0  |                                       |
|      | -abc-bd   b     0  |                                       |
|      | -bcd      bc    0  |                                       |
|      | 2abd-ac+d -ab-1 0  |                                       |
|      | abc-abe-e 0     -1 |                                       |
|      | bde+ce    be    c  |                                       |
|      | abce      0     e  |                                       |
|                                                                   |
|              12      3                                            |
|o13 : Matrix R   <-- R                                             |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : Nt*Mt -- is not zero!                                        |
|                                                                   |
|o14 = | 2ad    |                                                   |
|      | 0      |                                                   |
|      | -2cd   |                                                   |
|      | -2be   |                                                   |
|      | -2ae   |                                                   |
|      | 0      |                                                   |
|      | 2bd    |                                                   |
|      | 0      |                                                   |
|      | 0      |                                                   |
|      | 0      |                                                   |
|      | -2ce   |                                                   |
|      | -2abce |                                                   |
|                                                                   |
|              12      1                                            |
|o14 : Matrix R   <-- R                                             |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

If the ring is a Weyl algebra, the antipode is the identity, which is probably
not what is wanted.  If the ring is a quotient of a skew commuting polynomial
ring $R/I$, then the antipode of an element should really be in the quotient
ring $R/(antipode(I))$, but this function returns an element in the same ring.

See also
========

  * *note transpose: transpose, -- transpose a matrix or table

Ways to use antipode:
=====================

  * "antipode(RingElement)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note antipode: antipode, is a *note method function:
MethodFunction,.

