File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: symmetric algebras, Next: tensor products of rings, Prev: exterior algebras, Up: rings

symmetric algebras
******************

Polynomial rings are symmetric algebras with explicit generators, and we have
already seen how to construct them.  But if you have a module, then its
symmetric algebra can be constructed with *note symmetricAlgebra:
symmetricAlgebra,.
+-------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..d];       |
+-------------------------+
|i2 : symmetricAlgebra R^3|
|                         |
|o2 = R[p ..p ]           |
|        0   2            |
|                         |
|o2 : PolynomialRing      |
+-------------------------+
Maps between symmetric algebras can be constructed functorially.
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : vars R                                                         |
|                                                                    |
|o3 = | a b c d |                                                    |
|                                                                    |
|             1      4                                               |
|o3 : Matrix R  <-- R                                                |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : symmetricAlgebra vars R                                        |
|                                                                    |
|o4 = map (R[p ], R[p ..p ], {a*p , b*p , c*p , d*p , a, b, c, d})   |
|             0      0   3       0     0     0     0                 |
|                                                                    |
|o4 : RingMap R[p ] <-- R[p ..p ]                                    |
|                0         0   3                                     |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : symmetricAlgebra transpose vars R                              |
|                                                                    |
|o5 = map (R[p ..p ], R[p ], {a*p  + b*p  + c*p  + d*p , a, b, c, d})|
|             0   3      0       0      1      2      3              |
|                                                                    |
|o5 : RingMap R[p ..p ] <-- R[p ]                                    |
|                0   3         0                                     |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
Until the ring is used with *note use: use, or assigned to a global variable,
its generators are not assigned to global variables.
+--------------------+
|i6 : a              |
|                    |
|o6 = a              |
|                    |
|o6 : R              |
+--------------------+
|i7 : p_0            |
|                    |
|o7 = p              |
|      0             |
|                    |
|o7 : IndexedVariable|
+--------------------+
|i8 : S = o2;        |
+--------------------+
|i9 : a              |
|                    |
|o9 = a              |
|                    |
|o9 : R              |
+--------------------+
|i10 : p_0           |
|                    |
|o10 = p             |
|       0            |
|                    |
|o10 : S             |
+--------------------+
To specify the names of the variables when creating the ring, use the *note
Variables: Variables, option or the *note VariableBaseName: VariableBaseName,
option.
+--------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : symmetricAlgebra(R^3, Variables => {t,u,v}) |
|                                                  |
|o11 = R[t..v]                                     |
|                                                  |
|o11 : PolynomialRing                              |
+--------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : symmetricAlgebra(R^3, VariableBaseName => t)|
|                                                  |
|o12 = R[t ..t ]                                   |
|         0   2                                    |
|                                                  |
|o12 : PolynomialRing                              |
+--------------------------------------------------+
We can construct the symmetric algebra of a module that isn't necessarily free.
+-----------------------------------+
|i13 : use R                        |
|                                   |
|o13 = R                            |
|                                   |
|o13 : PolynomialRing               |
+-----------------------------------+
|i14 : symmetricAlgebra(R^1/(a,b^3))|
|                                   |
|          R[p ]                    |
|             0                     |
|o14 = ------------                 |
|              3                    |
|      (a*p , b p )                 |
|          0     0                  |
|                                   |
|o14 : QuotientRing                 |
+-----------------------------------+
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

