sums, products, and powers of ideals
************************************

Arithmetic for ideals uses the standard symbols.  Below are examples of the
basic arithmetic functions for ideal.
+------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/101[a..d]/(b*c-a*d,c^2-b*d,b^2-a*c);|
+------------------------------------------------+
For more information about quotient rings see *note quotient rings: quotient
rings,.
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal (a*b-c,d^3);                                         |
|                                                                    |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                                     |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : J = ideal (a^3,b*c-d);                                         |
|                                                                    |
|o3 : Ideal of R                                                     |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : I+J                                                            |
|                                                                    |
|                      3   3                                         |
|o4 = ideal (a*b - c, d , a , a*d - d)                               |
|                                                                    |
|o4 : Ideal of R                                                     |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : I*J                                                            |
|                                                                    |
|             4     3    2                            3 3     4    4 |
|o5 = ideal (a b - a c, a b*d - a*b*d - a*c*d + c*d, a d , a*d  - d )|
|                                                                    |
|o5 : Ideal of R                                                     |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : I^2                                                            |
|                                                                    |
|             3      2              3      3   6                     |
|o6 = ideal (a c - 2a d + b*d, a*b*d  - c*d , d )                    |
|                                                                    |
|o6 : Ideal of R                                                     |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
For more information see *note Ideal + Ideal: Ideal + Ideal,, *note Ideal *
Ideal: Ideal _st Ideal,, and *note Ideal ^ ZZ: Ideal ^ ZZ,.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_ideals.m2:192:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: equality and containment, Next: extracting generators of an ideal, Prev: sums_cm products_cm and powers of ideals, Up: ideals

equality and containment
************************

Equality and containment between two ideals in a polynomial ring (or quotient
of a polynomial ring) is checked by comparing their respective Gröbner bases.

equal and not equal
===================

Use *note Ideal == Ideal: equality and containment, to test if two ideals in
the same ring are equal.
+---------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..d];                           |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal (a^2*b-c^2, a*b^2-d^3, c^5-d);|
|                                             |
|o2 : Ideal of R                              |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i3 : J = ideal (a^2,b^2,c^2,d^2);            |
|                                             |
|o3 : Ideal of R                              |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i4 : I == J                                  |
|                                             |
|o4 = false                                   |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i5 : I != J                                  |
|                                             |
|o5 = true                                    |
+---------------------------------------------+

normal form with respect to a Gröbner basis and membership
==========================================================

The function *note RingElement % Ideal: methods for normal forms and remainder,
reduces an element with respect to a Gröbner basis of the ideal.
+----------------------+
|i6 : (1+a+a^3+a^4) % J|
|                      |
|o6 = a + 1            |
|                      |
|o6 : R                |
+----------------------+
We can then test membership in the ideal by comparing the answer to 0 using
*note ==: ==,.
+---------------------------+
|i7 : (1+a+a^3+a^4) % J == 0|
|                           |
|o7 = false                 |
+---------------------------+
|i8 : a^4 % J == 0          |
|                           |
|o8 = true                  |
+---------------------------+

containment for two ideals
==========================

Containment for two ideals is tested using *note isSubset: isSubset,.
+---------------------+
|i9 : isSubset(I,J)   |
|                     |
|o9 = false           |
+---------------------+
|i10 : isSubset(I,I+J)|
|                     |
|o10 = true           |
+---------------------+
|i11 : isSubset(I+J,I)|
|                     |
|o11 = false          |
+---------------------+

ideal equal to 1 or 0
=====================

Use the expression I == 1 to see if the ideal is equal to the ring.  Use I == 0
to see if the ideal is identically zero in the given ring.
+------------------------------+
|i12 : I = ideal (a^2-1,a^3+3);|
|                              |
|o12 : Ideal of R              |
+------------------------------+
|i13 : I == 1                  |
|                              |
|o13 = true                    |
+------------------------------+
|i14 : S = R/I                 |
|                              |
|o14 = S                       |
|                              |
|o14 : QuotientRing            |
+------------------------------+
|i15 : S == 0                  |
|                              |
|o15 = true                    |
+------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note Ideal == Ideal: equality and containment,
  * *note Ideal == ZZ: equality and containment,
  * *note !=: !=, -- inequality
  * *note RingElement % Ideal: methods for normal forms and remainder, --
    normal form of ring elements and matrices
  * *note isSubset(Ideal,Ideal): isSubset_lpIdeal_cmIdeal_rp, -- whether one
    object is a subset of another
  * *note radicalContainment: (MinimalPrimes)radicalContainment, -- whether an
    element is contained in the radical of an ideal
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

