File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: extracting generators of an ideal, Next: dimension_cm codimension_cm and degree, Prev: equality and containment, Up: ideals

extracting generators of an ideal
*********************************

obtain a single generator as an element
=======================================

Once an ideal has been constructed it is possible to obtain individual elements
using *note _: _us,.   As always in Macaulay2, indexing starts at 0.
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ[w,x,y,z];                                |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal(z*w-2*x*y, 3*w^3-z^3,w*x^2-4*y*z^2,x);|
|                                                     |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : I_0                                             |
|                                                     |
|o3 = - 2x*y + w*z                                    |
|                                                     |
|o3 : R                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : I_3                                             |
|                                                     |
|o4 = x                                               |
|                                                     |
|o4 : R                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------+

the generators as a matrix or list of elements
==============================================

Use *note generators: generators, or its abbreviation gens to get the
generators of an ideal I as a matrix. Applying first entries to this matrix
converts it to a list.
+----------------------------------------------+
|i5 : gens I                                   |
|                                              |
|o5 = | -2xy+wz 3w3-z3 wx2-4yz2 x |            |
|                                              |
|             1      4                         |
|o5 : Matrix R  <-- R                          |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i6 : first entries gens I                     |
|                                              |
|                      3    3     2       2    |
|o6 = {- 2x*y + w*z, 3w  - z , w*x  - 4y*z , x}|
|                                              |
|o6 : List                                     |
+----------------------------------------------+

number of generators
====================

The command *note numgens: numgens, gives the number of generators of an ideal
I.
+--------------+
|i7 : numgens I|
|              |
|o7 = 4        |
+--------------+

minimal generating set
======================

To obtain a minimal generating set of a homogeneous ideal use *note mingens:
mingens, to get the minimal generators as a matrix and use *note trim: trim, to
get the minimal generators as an ideal.
+------------------------------------+
|i8 : mingens I                      |
|                                    |
|o8 = | x wz 4yz2 3w3-z3 |           |
|                                    |
|             1      4               |
|o8 : Matrix R  <-- R                |
+------------------------------------+
|i9 : trim I                         |
|                                    |
|                        2    3    3 |
|o9 = ideal (x, w*z, 4y*z , 3w  - z )|
|                                    |
|o9 : Ideal of R                     |
+------------------------------------+
The function mingens is only well-defined for a homogeneous ideal or in a local
ring.  However, one can still try to get as small a generating set as possible
and when it is implemented this function will be done by trim.

obtaining the input form of an ideal
====================================

If the ideal was defined using a function like monomialCurveIdeal and the
generators are desired in the usual format for input of an ideal, the function
*note toString: toString, is very useful. (Note:  We are changing rings because
*note monomialCurveIdeal: monomialCurveIdeal, is not implemented for rings over
*note ZZ: ZZ,.)
+----------------------------------------+
|i10 : R = QQ[a..d];                     |
+----------------------------------------+
|i11 : I = monomialCurveIdeal(R,{1,2,3});|
|                                        |
|o11 : Ideal of R                        |
+----------------------------------------+
|i12 : toString I                        |
|                                        |
|o12 = ideal(c^2-b*d,b*c-a*d,b^2-a*c)    |
+----------------------------------------+
* Menu:

* generators of rings, ideals, and modules: generators of rings_cm ideals_cm and modules.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_ideals.m2:318:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: generators of rings_cm ideals_cm and modules, Up: extracting generators of an ideal

generators of rings, ideals, and modules
****************************************

get a single generator of a ring, ideal, or module
==================================================

  * Usage: 
        L_i
  * Inputs:
      * L, a *note ring: Ring,, an *note ideal: Ideal,, a *note module:
        Module,, or a *note matrix: Matrix,
      * i, an *note integer: ZZ,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring element: RingElement, or a *note vector: Vector, the i-th
        generator or column of L

get the list of generators of a ring, ideal, or module
======================================================

  * Usage: 
        M_*
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note ring: Ring,, an *note ideal: Ideal,, or a *note module:
        Module,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: List,
As usual in Macaulay2, the first generator has index zero.


+---------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..d];                     |
+---------------------------------------+
|i2 : numgens R                         |
|                                       |
|o2 = 4                                 |
+---------------------------------------+
|i3 : R_2                               |
|                                       |
|o3 = c                                 |
|                                       |
|o3 : R                                 |
+---------------------------------------+
|i4 : R_*                               |
|                                       |
|o4 = {a, b, c, d}                      |
|                                       |
|o4 : List                              |
+---------------------------------------+
|i5 : I = ideal(a^3, b^3-c^3, a^4, a*c);|
|                                       |
|o5 : Ideal of R                        |
+---------------------------------------+
|i6 : numgens I                         |
|                                       |
|o6 = 4                                 |
+---------------------------------------+
|i7 : I_0, I_2                          |
|                                       |
|       3   4                           |
|o7 = (a , a )                          |
|                                       |
|o7 : Sequence                          |
+---------------------------------------+
|i8 : I_*                               |
|                                       |
|       3   3    3   4                  |
|o8 = {a , b  - c , a , a*c}            |
|                                       |
|o8 : List                              |
+---------------------------------------+


Notice that the generators are the ones provided.  Alternatively we can
minimalize the set of generators.
+-----------------------------+
|i9 : J = trim I              |
|                             |
|                  3    3   3 |
|o9 = ideal (a*c, b  - c , a )|
|                             |
|o9 : Ideal of R              |
+-----------------------------+
|i10 : J_0                    |
|                             |
|o10 = a*c                    |
|                             |
|o10 : R                      |
+-----------------------------+


Elements of modules are useful for producing submodules or quotients.
+--------------------------------------+
|i11 : M = cokernel matrix{{a,b},{c,d}}|
|                                      |
|o11 = cokernel | a b |                |
|               | c d |                |
|                                      |
|                             2        |
|o11 : R-module, quotient of R         |
+--------------------------------------+
|i12 : M_0                             |
|                                      |
|o12 = | 1 |                           |
|      | 0 |                           |
|                                      |
|o12 : M                               |
+--------------------------------------+
|i13 : M_*                             |
|                                      |
|o13 = {| 1 |, | 0 |}                  |
|       | 0 |  | 1 |                   |
|                                      |
|o13 : List                            |
+--------------------------------------+
|i14 : M/M_0                           |
|                                      |
|o14 = cokernel | 1 a b |              |
|               | 0 c d |              |
|                                      |
|                             2        |
|o14 : R-module, quotient of R         |
+--------------------------------------+
|i15 : N = M/(a*M + R*M_0)             |
|                                      |
|o15 = cokernel | a 0 1 a b |          |
|               | 0 a 0 c d |          |
|                                      |
|                             2        |
|o15 : R-module, quotient of R         |
+--------------------------------------+
|i16 : N_0 == 0_N                      |
|                                      |
|o16 = true                            |
+--------------------------------------+
Columns of matrices may also be used as vectors in the target module.
+------------------------------------------------+
|i17 : M = matrix{{a,b,c},{c,d,a},{a-1,b-3,c-13}}|
|                                                |
|o17 = | a   b   c    |                          |
|      | c   d   a    |                          |
|      | a-1 b-3 c-13 |                          |
|                                                |
|              3      3                          |
|o17 : Matrix R  <-- R                           |
+------------------------------------------------+
|i18 : M_0                                       |
|                                                |
|o18 = |  a  |                                   |
|      |  c  |                                   |
|      | a-1 |                                   |
|                                                |
|       3                                        |
|o18 : R                                         |
+------------------------------------------------+
|i19 : prune((image M_{1,2})/(R*M_1))            |
|                                                |
|       1                                        |
|o19 = R                                         |
|                                                |
|o19 : R-module, free                            |
+------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Fewer methods exist for manipulating vectors than other types, such as modules
and matrices
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

