dimension, codimension, and degree
**********************************

Use *note dim: dim,, *note codim: codim,, and *note degree: degree, to compute
the dimension, codimension and degree, respectively, of an ideal.  The
functions *note dim: dim, and *note degree: degree, compute the dimension and
degree of the ring R/I.
+--------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/101[x,y,z];               |
+--------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal(x^3-y*z^2,x*y-z^2,x*z);|
|                                      |
|o2 : Ideal of R                       |
+--------------------------------------+
|i3 : dim I                            |
|                                      |
|o3 = 1                                |
+--------------------------------------+
|i4 : codim I                          |
|                                      |
|o4 = 2                                |
+--------------------------------------+
|i5 : degree I                         |
|                                      |
|o5 = 2                                |
+--------------------------------------+
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_ideals.m2:333:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: intersection of ideals, Prev: dimension_cm codimension_cm and degree, Up: ideals

intersection of ideals
**********************

Use *note intersect: intersect, to compute the intersection of two or more
ideals.
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..d];                                      |
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : intersect(ideal(a,b),ideal(c*d,a*b),ideal(b*d,a*c))|
|                                                        |
|o2 = ideal (b*c*d, a*c*d, a*b*d, a*b*c)                 |
|                                                        |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                         |
+--------------------------------------------------------+
The command *note intersect: intersect, will only work with proper ideals. To
intersect an ideal with a ring, use *note selectInSubring: selectInSubring,
along with the elimination ordering, see *note Eliminate: Eliminate,.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_ideals.m2:346:0.

