transpose -- transpose a matrix or table
****************************************

  * Usage: 
        transpose f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note matrix: Matrix, or a *note list: List, which is a table
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: Matrix,, the transpose of f

Description
===========

Here is an example.
+--------------------------------------------+
|i1 : S = ZZ/10007[x,y,z];                   |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i2 : f = matrix{{x^3,x*y^2,x},{y*x^2,y^3,y}}|
|                                            |
|o2 = | x3  xy2 x |                          |
|     | x2y y3  y |                          |
|                                            |
|             2      3                       |
|o2 : Matrix S  <-- S                        |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i3 : entries f                              |
|                                            |
|        3     2        2    3               |
|o3 = {{x , x*y , x}, {x y, y , y}}          |
|                                            |
|o3 : List                                   |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i4 : g = transpose f                        |
|                                            |
|o4 = {-3} | x3  x2y |                       |
|     {-3} | xy2 y3  |                       |
|     {-1} | x   y   |                       |
|                                            |
|             3      2                       |
|o4 : Matrix S  <-- S                        |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i5 : transpose entries f                    |
|                                            |
|        3   2        2   3                  |
|o5 = {{x , x y}, {x*y , y }, {x, y}}        |
|                                            |
|o5 : List                                   |
+--------------------------------------------+
The output of transpose is a map between the duals of the original source and
target free modules. See:
+-------------------------------------+
|i6 : degrees f                       |
|                                     |
|o6 = {{{0}, {0}}, {{3}, {3}, {1}}}   |
|                                     |
|o6 : List                            |
+-------------------------------------+
|i7 : degrees g                       |
|                                     |
|o7 = {{{-3}, {-3}, {-1}}, {{0}, {0}}}|
|                                     |
|o7 : List                            |
+-------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

transpose works only for maps between free modules. Use dual for more general
maps.

See also
========

  * *note dual: dual, -- dual module or map

Ways to use transpose:
======================

  * "transpose(List)"
  * "transpose(Matrix)"
  * "transpose(MutableMatrix)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note transpose: transpose, is a *note method function with a single
argument: MethodFunctionSingle,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/functions/transpose-doc.m2:40:0.

