support -- list of variables occurring in a polynomial or matrix
****************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        support f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring element: RingElement,, or a *note matrix: Matrix, over
        a polynomial ring
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: List,, of *note ring elements: RingElement,, the
        variables occurring in f

Description
===========

+---------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..g]                |
|                                 |
|o1 = R                           |
|                                 |
|o1 : PolynomialRing              |
+---------------------------------+
|i2 : f = a^3+b^2*c+3*f^10*d-1+e-e|
|                                 |
|         10    3    2            |
|o2 = 3d*f   + a  + b c - 1       |
|                                 |
|o2 : R                           |
+---------------------------------+
|i3 : support f                   |
|                                 |
|o3 = {a, b, c, d, f}             |
|                                 |
|o3 : List                        |
+---------------------------------+
+--------------------------------+
|i4 : M = matrix"a+b2,c+g2;c,a-1"|
|                                |
|o4 = | b2+a g2+c |              |
|     | c    a-1  |              |
|                                |
|             2      2           |
|o4 : Matrix R  <-- R            |
+--------------------------------+
|i5 : support M                  |
|                                |
|o5 = {a, b, c, g}               |
|                                |
|o5 : List                       |
+--------------------------------+
If the ring is a polynomial ring over another polynomial ring, then the support
contains all of the variables, even the ones in the coefficient ring. The  ring
of each of these is the ring of f.
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : A = ZZ[a,b]; B = A[r,s,t]; C = B[x,y,z,w];            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : f = (a+r+z+1)^2+y                                     |
|                                                           |
|      2                         2                2         |
|o9 = z  + y + (2r + 2a + 2)z + r  + (2a + 2)r + a  + 2a + 1|
|                                                           |
|o9 : C                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : S = support f                                        |
|                                                           |
|o10 = {y, z, r, a}                                         |
|                                                           |
|o10 : List                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : ring S_2 === ring f                                  |
|                                                           |
|o11 = true                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
Here is one way to select only the top level variables.
+-----------------------------------------+
|i12 : select(S, x -> index x < numgens C)|
|                                         |
|o12 = {y, z}                             |
|                                         |
|o12 : List                               |
+-----------------------------------------+
To obtain a list of the integer indices of the variables one can use either
*note indices(RingElement): indices_lpRingElement_rp, or apply *note index:
index, to each variable.
+-----------------------------+
|i13 : indices f              |
|                             |
|o13 = {1, 2, 4, 7}           |
|                             |
|o13 : List                   |
+-----------------------------+
|i14 : apply(support f, index)|
|                             |
|o14 = {1, 2, 4, 7}           |
|                             |
|o14 : List                   |
+-----------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note index: index, -- numeric index of a ring variable
  * *note indices(RingElement): indices_lpRingElement_rp, -- indices of
    variables occurring in a polynomial
  * *note indices(Matrix): indices_lpRingElement_rp, -- indices of variables
    occurring in a polynomial
  * *note Ring _ ZZ: get a ring variable by index, -- get a ring variable by
    index

Ways to use support:
====================

  * "support(Matrix)"
  * "support(RingElement)"
  * *note support(Ideal): support_lpIdeal_rp, -- list of variables occurring in
    the generators of an ideal

For the programmer
==================

The object *note support: support, is a *note method function: MethodFunction,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

