Matrix * Matrix -- matrix multiplication
****************************************

  * Operator: *note *: _st,
  * Usage: 
        f * g
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note matrix: Matrix,
      * g, a *note matrix: Matrix,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: Matrix,

Description
===========

Multiplication of matrices corresponds to composition of maps, and when the
target Q of g equals the source P of f, the product f*g is defined, its source
is the source of g, and its target is the target of f.
+----------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a,b,c,x,y,z];   |
+----------------------------+
|i2 : f = matrix{{x},{y},{z}}|
|                            |
|o2 = | x |                  |
|     | y |                  |
|     | z |                  |
|                            |
|             3      1       |
|o2 : Matrix R  <-- R        |
+----------------------------+
|i3 : g = matrix{{a,b,c}}    |
|                            |
|o3 = | a b c |              |
|                            |
|             1      3       |
|o3 : Matrix R  <-- R        |
+----------------------------+
|i4 : f*g                    |
|                            |
|o4 = | ax bx cx |           |
|     | ay by cy |           |
|     | az bz cz |           |
|                            |
|             3      3       |
|o4 : Matrix R  <-- R        |
+----------------------------+


The degree of f*g is the sum of the degrees of f and of g.


The product is also defined when P != Q, provided only that P and Q are free
modules of the same rank.  If the degrees of P differ from the corresponding
degrees of Q by the same degree d, then the degree of f*g is adjusted by d so
it will have a good chance to be homogeneous, and the target and source of f*g
are as before.
+-----------------------------+
|i5 : target (f*g) == target f|
|                             |
|o5 = true                    |
+-----------------------------+
|i6 : source (f*g) == source g|
|                             |
|o6 = false                   |
+-----------------------------+
|i7 : isHomogeneous (f*g)     |
|                             |
|o7 = true                    |
+-----------------------------+
|i8 : degree(f*g)             |
|                             |
|o8 = {0}                     |
|                             |
|o8 : List                    |
+-----------------------------+
Sometimes, it is useful to make this a map of degree zero.  Use *note
map(Matrix): map_lpMatrix_rp, for this purpose.
+---------------------------+
|i9 : h = map(f*g,Degree=>0)|
|                           |
|o9 = | ax bx cx |          |
|     | ay by cy |          |
|     | az bz cz |          |
|                           |
|             3      3      |
|o9 : Matrix R  <-- R       |
+---------------------------+
|i10 : degree h             |
|                           |
|o10 = {0}                  |
|                           |
|o10 : List                 |
+---------------------------+
|i11 : degrees source h     |
|                           |
|o11 = {{2}, {2}, {2}}      |
|                           |
|o11 : List                 |
+---------------------------+

See also
========

  * degree(Matrix) (missing documentation)
  * *note degrees: degrees, -- degrees of generators

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note Matrix * Matrix: Matrix _st Matrix, -- matrix multiplication
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

