File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: matrix_lpList_rp, Next: matrix_lpRing_cmList_rp, Up: matrix

matrix(List) -- create a matrix from a doubly-nested list of ring elements or matrices
**************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note matrix: matrix,
  * Usage: 
        matrix v
  * Inputs:
      * v, a *note list: List,, a list of lists of either ring elements or
        matrices
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Degree => ..., default value null, an integer or a list of integers, to
        serve as the degree of the matrix
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: Matrix,, A matrix where the first list of v gives the
        first row (or set of rows, if the elements are matrices), the second
        list is the second row, etc.

Description
===========

An attempt is made to coerce the ring elements and matrices to a common ring.
If the entries are ring elements, they are used as the entries of the matrix,
and if the entries are matrices, then they are used to provide blocks of
entries in the resulting matrix.


An attempt is made to set up the degrees of the generators of the free module
serving as source so that the map will be homogeneous and of degree zero.


+-------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/101[x,y,z]   |
|                         |
|o1 = R                   |
|                         |
|o1 : PolynomialRing      |
+-------------------------+
|i2 : p = matrix {{x,y,z}}|
|                         |
|o2 = | x y z |           |
|                         |
|             1      3    |
|o2 : Matrix R  <-- R     |
+-------------------------+
|i3 : degrees source p    |
|                         |
|o3 = {{1}, {1}, {1}}     |
|                         |
|o3 : List                |
+-------------------------+
|i4 : isHomogeneous p     |
|                         |
|o4 = true                |
+-------------------------+
Notice that the degrees were set up so that p is homogeneous, because the
source module is not explicitly specified by the user.  The next example
involves block matrices.
+--------------------------+
|i5 : q = vars R           |
|                          |
|o5 = | x y z |            |
|                          |
|             1      3     |
|o5 : Matrix R  <-- R      |
+--------------------------+
|i6 : matrix {{q,q,q}}     |
|                          |
|o6 = | x y z x y z x y z ||
|                          |
|             1      9     |
|o6 : Matrix R  <-- R      |
+--------------------------+
|i7 : matrix {{q},{q},{q}} |
|                          |
|o7 = | x y z |            |
|     | x y z |            |
|     | x y z |            |
|                          |
|             3      3     |
|o7 : Matrix R  <-- R      |
+--------------------------+
Here we construct a matrix from column vectors.
+---------------------------------------------+
|i8 : F = R^3                                 |
|                                             |
|      3                                      |
|o8 = R                                       |
|                                             |
|o8 : R-module, free                          |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i9 : matrix {F_2, F_1, x*F_0 + y*F_1 + z*F_2}|
|                                             |
|o9 = | 0 0 x |                               |
|     | 0 1 y |                               |
|     | 1 0 z |                               |
|                                             |
|             3      3                        |
|o9 : Matrix R  <-- R                         |
+---------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note map: map, -- make a map

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note matrix(List): matrix_lpList_rp, -- create a matrix from a
    doubly-nested list of ring elements or matrices
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/functions/matrix-doc.m2:102:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: matrix_lpRing_cmList_rp, Next: matrix_lpMatrix_rp, Prev: matrix_lpList_rp, Up: matrix

matrix(Ring,List) -- create a matrix from a doubly nested list of ring elements or matrices
*******************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note matrix: matrix,
  * Usage: 
        matrix(R, v)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: Ring,
      * v, a *note list: List,, a list of vectors; or a doubly nested list of
        ring elements and/or matrices
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degree: matrix_lpList_rp, => ..., default value null, create a
        matrix from a doubly-nested list of ring elements or matrices
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: Matrix,, A matrix over R, whose source and target are
        both free, formed by the  elements of v.

Description
===========

All of the vectors, ring elements, or matrices must be defined over the ring R,
or a base ring of R.


If a doubly nested list of matrices is given, then ring elements can be used
for 1 by 1 blocks, and 0 represents a zero block.


This is essentially the same as *note matrix(List): matrix_lpList_rp, together
with the specification of the ring.


+----------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..d];          |
+----------------------------+
|i2 : f = matrix{{a,b},{c,0}}|
|                            |
|o2 = | a b |                |
|     | c 0 |                |
|                            |
|             2      2       |
|o2 : Matrix R  <-- R        |
+----------------------------+
|i3 : h = matrix{{f,f},{f,0}}|
|                            |
|o3 = | a b a b |            |
|     | c 0 c 0 |            |
|     | a b 0 0 |            |
|     | c 0 0 0 |            |
|                            |
|             4      4       |
|o3 : Matrix R  <-- R        |
+----------------------------+

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note matrix(Ring,List): matrix_lpRing_cmList_rp, -- create a matrix from a
    doubly nested list of ring elements or matrices
  * "matrix(RingFamily,List)"
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/functions/matrix-doc.m2:60:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: matrix_lpMatrix_rp, Next: matrix_lpMutableMatrix_rp, Prev: matrix_lpRing_cmList_rp, Up: matrix

matrix(Matrix) -- the matrix between generators
***********************************************

  * Function: *note matrix: matrix,
  * Usage: 
        matrix f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note matrix: Matrix,, a map of modules
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degree: matrix_lpList_rp, => ..., default value null, create a
        matrix from a doubly-nested list of ring elements or matrices
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: Matrix,, If the source and target of f are free, then
        the result is f itself.  Otherwise, the source and target will be
        replaced by the free modules whose basis elements correspond to the
        generators of the modules.

Description
===========

Each homomorphism of modules $f : M \rightarrow N$ in Macaulay2 is induced from
a matrix $f0 : \mathtt{cover} M \rightarrow \mathtt{cover} N$. This function
returns this matrix.
+------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..d];                  |
+------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal(a^2,b^2,c*d)         |
|                                    |
|             2   2                  |
|o2 = ideal (a , b , c*d)            |
|                                    |
|o2 : Ideal of R                     |
+------------------------------------+
|i3 : f = basis(3,I)                 |
|                                    |
|o3 = {2} | a b c d 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 ||
|     {2} | 0 0 0 0 a b c d 0 0 0 0 ||
|     {2} | 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 a b c d ||
|                                    |
|o3 : Matrix                         |
+------------------------------------+
|i4 : source f                       |
|                                    |
|      12                            |
|o4 = R                              |
|                                    |
|o4 : R-module, free, degrees {12:3} |
+------------------------------------+
|i5 : target f                       |
|                                    |
|o5 = image | a2 b2 cd |             |
|                                    |
|                             1      |
|o5 : R-module, submodule of R       |
+------------------------------------+
The map f is induced by the following 3 by 12 matrix from R^12 to the 3
generators of I.
+------------------------------------+
|i6 : matrix f                       |
|                                    |
|o6 = {2} | a b c d 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 ||
|     {2} | 0 0 0 0 a b c d 0 0 0 0 ||
|     {2} | 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 a b c d ||
|                                    |
|             3      12              |
|o6 : Matrix R  <-- R                |
+------------------------------------+
To obtain the map that is the composite of this with the inclusion of I onto R,
use *note super(Matrix): super,.
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : super f                                          |
|                                                      |
|o7 = | a3 a2b a2c a2d ab2 b3 b2c b2d acd bcd c2d cd2 ||
|                                                      |
|             1      12                                |
|o7 : Matrix R  <-- R                                  |
+------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note cover: cover, -- get the covering free module
  * *note super: super, -- get the ambient module
  * *note basis: basis, -- basis or generating set of all or part of a ring,
    ideal or module

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note matrix(Matrix): matrix_lpMatrix_rp, -- the matrix between generators
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/functions/matrix-doc.m2:156:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: matrix_lpMutableMatrix_rp, Next: matrix_lpRing_cmRingElement_rp, Prev: matrix_lpMatrix_rp, Up: matrix

matrix(MutableMatrix) -- make a matrix from a mutable one
*********************************************************

  * Function: *note matrix: matrix,
  * Usage: 
        matrix m
  * Inputs:
      * m, a *note mutable matrix: MutableMatrix,
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degree: matrix_lpList_rp, => ..., default value null, create a
        matrix from a doubly-nested list of ring elements or matrices
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: Matrix,, the same matrix as m, but not mutable.

Description
===========

There are many more operations available for matrices than for immutable
matrices, but mutable matrices are sometimes easier to construct.
+------------------------------+
|i1 : m = mutableMatrix(ZZ,3,5)|
|                              |
|o1 = 0                        |
|                              |
|o1 : MutableMatrix            |
+------------------------------+
|i2 : m_(1,2) = 3              |
|                              |
|o2 = 3                        |
+------------------------------+
|i3 : m_(2,4) = 54             |
|                              |
|o3 = 54                       |
+------------------------------+
|i4 : m_(0,0) = -12            |
|                              |
|o4 = -12                      |
+------------------------------+
|i5 : m                        |
|                              |
|o5 = | -12 0 0 0 0  |         |
|     | 0   0 3 0 0  |         |
|     | 0   0 0 0 54 |         |
|                              |
|o5 : MutableMatrix            |
+------------------------------+
|i6 : matrix m                 |
|                              |
|o6 = | -12 0 0 0 0  |         |
|     | 0   0 3 0 0  |         |
|     | 0   0 0 0 54 |         |
|                              |
|              3       5       |
|o6 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ        |
