reshape -- reshape a matrix
***************************

  * Usage: 
        reshape(F, G, f)
  * Inputs:
      * F, a *note module: Module,, a free module; 
      * G, a *note module: Module,, a free module; 
      * f, a *note matrix: Matrix,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: Matrix,, F <-- G obtained from f by taking elements
        from the first column of f, then the second, and so on, filling them
        into the result column by column.

Description
===========

Currently, it is assumed that f and the result both have the same number of
entries. The resulting map has the same degree that f has, but it is easy to
spoil homogeneity by giving incorrect free modules.

+-----------------------------------------------+
|i1 : f = matrix{{1,3,5,7,9,11},{2,4,6,8,10,12}}|
|                                               |
|o1 = | 1 3 5 7 9  11 |                         |
|     | 2 4 6 8 10 12 |                         |
|                                               |
|              2       6                        |
|o1 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                         |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i2 : reshape(ZZ^3,ZZ^4,f)                      |
|                                               |
|o2 = | 1 4 7 10 |                              |
|     | 2 5 8 11 |                              |
|     | 3 6 9 12 |                              |
|                                               |
|              3       4                        |
|o2 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                         |
+-----------------------------------------------+

Ways to use reshape:
