minors(...,Strategy=>...) -- choose between Bareiss, Cofactor and Dynamic algorithms
************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        minors(M, Strategy => s)
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note matrix: Matrix,
      * s, a *note symbol: Symbol,, either Bareiss, Cofactor, or Dynamic
  * Consequences:
      * If s is *note Bareiss: Bareiss,, then the Bareiss algorithm is used; if
        s is *note Cofactor: Cofactor,, then cofactor expansion is used; if s
        is *note Dynamic: Dynamic,, then a dynamic programming algorithm is
        used.

Description
===========

The *note ring: Ring, of M determines the default strategy.  If the ring is a
*note polynomial ring: PolynomialRing, or a field (as identified by *note
isField: isField,) then the *note Bareiss: Bareiss, algorithm is used.  If the
ring is a *note quotient ring: QuotientRing, (which has not been declared a
field by *note toField: toField_lpRing_rp,), then the *note Cofactor: Cofactor,
algorithm is used.The *note Dynamic: Dynamic, algorithm implements a variant of
cofactor expansion that caches intermediate results. This strategy introduces
some memory overhead, but can be faster than *note Cofactor: Cofactor,,
especially with sparse matrices of low degree.


Caveat
======

The *note Bareiss: Bareiss, algorithm returns a ring element that may differ
from the actual determinant by a zero divisor in the ring.  Thus, an incorrect
answer may be computed if the ring contains zero divisors.

See also
========

  * *note determinant: determinant, -- determinant of a matrix
  * *note exteriorPower: exteriorPower, -- exterior power

