pushForward(RingMap,Module) -- compute the pushforward of a module along a ring map
***********************************************************************************

  * Function: *note pushForward: pushForward_lpRingMap_cmModule_rp,
  * Usage: 
        pushForward(F,M)
  * Inputs:
      * F, a *note ring map: RingMap,, a ring map F: R --> S, graded
      * M, a *note module: Module,, over S, graded
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * MonomialOrder => ..., default value Eliminate, the type of monomial
        ordering to use in the computation, as keyword, either *note Eliminate:
        Eliminate,, *note ProductOrder: ProductOrder,, or *note Lex: Lex,
      * UseHilbertFunction => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value
        true, whether to use the Hilbert function as a hint for the Gröbner
        basis computation, if M and F are homogeneous
      * StopBeforeComputation => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value
        false, see *note gb(...,StopBeforeComputation=>...): gb,
      * DegreeLimit => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value {}, see
        *note gb(...,DegreeLimit=>...): gb,
      * PairLimit => a *note Boolean value: Boolean,, default value infinity,
        see *note gb(...,PairLimit=>...): gb,
      * Strategy => ..., default value null
  * Outputs:
      * a *note module: Module,, M, considered as an R-module

Description
===========

Currently, R and S must both be polynomial rings over the same base field.


This function first checks to see whether M will be a finitely generated
R-module via F.  If not, an error message describing the codimension of M/(vars
of S)M is given (this is equal to the dimension of R if and only if M is a
finitely generated R-module.


Assuming that it is, the push forward F_*(M) is computed.  This is done by
first finding a presentation for M in terms of a set of elements that generates
M as an S-module, and then applying the routine *note coimage: coimage, to a
map whose target is M and whose source is a free module over R.

Example: The Auslander-Buchsbaum formula
========================================

Let's illustrate the Auslander-Buchsbaum formula. First construct some rings
and make a module of projective dimension 2.
+--------------------------------------+
|i1 : R4 = ZZ/32003[a..d];             |
+--------------------------------------+
|i2 : R5 = ZZ/32003[a..e];             |
+--------------------------------------+
|i3 : R6 = ZZ/32003[a..f];             |
+--------------------------------------+
|i4 : M = coker genericMatrix(R6,a,2,3)|
|                                      |
|o4 = cokernel | a c e |               |
|              | b d f |               |
|                                      |
|                              2       |
|o4 : R6-module, quotient of R6        |
+--------------------------------------+
|i5 : pdim M                           |
|                                      |
|o5 = 2                                |
+--------------------------------------+
Create ring maps.
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : G = map(R6,R5,{a+b+c+d+e+f,b,c,d,e})                                    |
|                                                                             |
|o6 = map (R6, R5, {a + b + c + d + e + f, b, c, d, e})                       |
|                                                                             |
|o6 : RingMap R6 <-- R5                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : F = map(R5,R4,random(R5^1, R5^{4:-1}))                                  |
|                                                                             |
