free modules
************

We use *note Ring ^ ZZ: Ring ^ ZZ, to make a new free module.


+-----------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/101[x,y,z];|
+-----------------------+
|i2 : M = R^4           |
|                       |
|      4                |
|o2 = R                 |
|                       |
|o2 : R-module, free    |
+-----------------------+
Such modules are often made implicitly when constructing matrices.
+--------------------------------+
|i3 : m = matrix{{x,y,z},{y,z,0}}|
|                                |
|o3 = | x y z |                  |
|     | y z 0 |                  |
|                                |
|             2      3           |
|o3 : Matrix R  <-- R            |
+--------------------------------+
|i4 : target m == R^2            |
|                                |
|o4 = true                       |
+--------------------------------+


When a ring is graded, so are its free modules.  By default, the degrees of the
basis elements are taken to be 0.
+-------------------------+
|i5 : degrees M           |
|                         |
|o5 = {{0}, {0}, {0}, {0}}|
|                         |
|o5 : List                |
+-------------------------+
We can use *note Ring ^ List: Ring ^ List, to specify other degrees, or more
precisely, their additive inverses.
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : F = R^{1,4:2,3,3:4}                                   |
|                                                           |
|      9                                                    |
|o6 = R                                                     |
|                                                           |
|o6 : R-module, free, degrees {-1, 4:-2, -3, 3:-4}          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : degrees F                                             |
|                                                           |
|o7 = {{-1}, {-2}, {-2}, {-2}, {-2}, {-3}, {-4}, {-4}, {-4}}|
|                                                           |
|o7 : List                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
Notice the use of *note _colon_: _co, above to indicate repetition.


If the variables of the ring have multi-degrees represented by lists (vectors)
of integers, then the degrees of a free module must also be multi-degrees.
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : S = ZZ[a,b,c, Degrees=>{{1,2},{2,0},{3,3}}]      |
|                                                      |
|o8 = S                                                |
|                                                      |
|o8 : PolynomialRing                                   |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : N = S ^ {{-1,-1},{-4,4},{0,0}}                   |
|                                                      |
|      3                                               |
|o9 = S                                                |
|                                                      |
|o9 : S-module, free, degrees {{1, 1}, {4, -4}, {0, 0}}|
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : degree N_0                                      |
|                                                      |
|o10 = {1, 1}                                          |
|                                                      |
|o10 : List                                            |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : degree (a*b*N_1)                                |
|                                                      |
|o11 = {7, -2}                                         |
|                                                      |
|o11 : List                                            |
+------------------------------------------------------+
* Menu:

* Ring ^ ZZ::                   make a free module
* Ring ^ List::                 make a free module
* isFreeModule::                whether something is a free module
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

