matrices to and from modules -- including kernel, cokernel and image
********************************************************************

matrices to modules (kernel, image, cokernel)
=============================================

Given a matrix, we may compute the *note kernel: kernel,, *note image: image,,
and *note cokernel: cokernel,.
+------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..f];                  |
+------------------------------------+
|i2 : F = matrix{{a,b,d,e},{b,c,e,f}}|
|                                    |
|o2 = | a b d e |                    |
|     | b c e f |                    |
|                                    |
|             2      4               |
|o2 : Matrix R  <-- R                |
+------------------------------------+
+----------------------------------------------+
|i3 : M = ker F                                |
|                                              |
|o3 = image {1} | cd-be  0      e2-df  ce-bf  ||
|           {1} | -bd+ae e2-df  0      -be+af ||
|           {1} | b2-ac  -ce+bf -be+af 0      ||
|           {1} | 0      cd-be  bd-ae  b2-ac  ||
|                                              |
|                             4                |
|o3 : R-module, submodule of R                 |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i4 : coker F                                  |
|                                              |
|o4 = cokernel | a b d e |                     |
|              | b c e f |                     |
|                                              |
|                            2                 |
|o4 : R-module, quotient of R                  |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i5 : image F                                  |
|                                              |
|o5 = image | a b d e |                        |
|           | b c e f |                        |
|                                              |
|                             2                |
|o5 : R-module, submodule of R                 |
+----------------------------------------------+
Some routines in Macaulay2 have abbreviations, for example ker may be used for
kernel, and coker may be used for cokernel. The image function has no
abbreviated form.

modules to matrices
===================

Each module has, at least implicitly, two matrices associated to it: *note
generators: generators, (abbreviated form: gens), and *note relations:
relations,.  If a module is a submodule of a free module, then the relations
matrix is zero.  If a module is a quotient of a free module, then the generator
matrix is the identity matrix.  If a module is a *note subquotient:
subquotient,, then both may be more general.
+----------------------------------------+
|i6 : generators M                       |
|                                        |
|o6 = {1} | cd-be  0      e2-df  ce-bf  ||
|     {1} | -bd+ae e2-df  0      -be+af ||
|     {1} | b2-ac  -ce+bf -be+af 0      ||
|     {1} | 0      cd-be  bd-ae  b2-ac  ||
|                                        |
|             4      4                   |
|o6 : Matrix R  <-- R                    |
+----------------------------------------+
|i7 : relations M                        |
|                                        |
|o7 = 0                                  |
|                                        |
|             4                          |
|o7 : Matrix R  <-- 0                    |
+----------------------------------------+


Every finitely generated module has a presentation matrix. In Macaulay2, if the
module is not a quotient of a free module, then a syzygy computation is
performed to find a presentation matrix.
+---------------------+
|i8 : presentation M  |
|                     |
|o8 = {3} | -f -e |   |
|     {3} | b  a  |   |
|     {3} | -c -b |   |
|     {3} | e  d  |   |
|                     |
|             4      2|
|o8 : Matrix R  <-- R |
+---------------------+

See also
========

  * *note generators(Module): generators_lpModule_rp, -- the generator matrix
    of a module
  * *note relations(Module): relations, -- the defining relations
  * *note presentation(Module): presentation_lpModule_rp, -- presentation of a
    module
* Menu:

* kernel(Matrix): kernel_lpMatrix_rp.  kernel of a matrix
* cokernel(Matrix): cokernel_lpMatrix_rp.  cokernel of a map of modules
* image(Matrix): image_lpMatrix_rp.  image of a map
* generators(Module): generators_lpModule_rp.  the generator matrix of a module
* relations(Module): relations.  the defining relations
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/ov_modules.m2:232:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: kernel_lpMatrix_rp, Next: cokernel_lpMatrix_rp, Up: matrices to and from modules

kernel(Matrix) -- kernel of a matrix
************************************

  * Function: *note kernel: kernel,
  * Usage: 
        kernel f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note matrix: Matrix,, a map of modules M --> N
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * DegreeLimit (missing documentation) => ..., default value {}, 
      * Strategy (missing documentation) => ..., default value {}, 
      * *note SubringLimit: kernel_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmSubringLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value infinity, stop after finding enough elements of a
        subring
  * Outputs:
      * a *note module: Module,, the kernel of f, a submodule of M

Description
===========

If f is a *note ring element: RingElement,, then it will be interpreted as a
one by one matrix.


The kernel is the submodule of M of all elements mapping to zero under f. Over
polynomial rings, this is computed using a Gröbner basis computation.
+---------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/32003[vars(0..10)]   |
|                                 |
|o1 = R                           |
|                                 |
|o1 : PolynomialRing              |
+---------------------------------+
|i2 : M = genericSkewMatrix(R,a,5)|
|                                 |
|o2 = | 0  a  b  c  d |           |
|     | -a 0  e  f  g |           |
|     | -b -e 0  h  i |           |
|     | -c -f -h 0  j |           |
|     | -d -g -i -j 0 |           |
|                                 |
|             5      5            |
|o2 : Matrix R  <-- R             |
+---------------------------------+
|i3 : ker M                       |
|                                 |
|o3 = image {1} | gh-fi+ej  |     |
|           {1} | -dh+ci-bj |     |
|           {1} | df-cg+aj  |     |
|           {1} | -de+bg-ai |     |
|           {1} | ce-bf+ah  |     |
|                                 |
|                             5   |
|o3 : R-module, submodule of R    |
+---------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note syz: syz, -- the syzygy matrix
  * *note genericSkewMatrix: genericSkewMatrix, -- make a generic skew
    symmetric matrix of variables

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note kernel(Matrix): kernel_lpMatrix_rp, -- kernel of a matrix
  * "kernel(RingElement)"
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/functions/kernel-doc.m2:72:0.

