map(Module,Module,List) -- create a matrix by giving a sparse or dense list of entries
**************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note map: map,
  * Usage: 
        map(M,N,v)
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note module: Module,
      * N, a *note module: Module,
      * v, a *note list: List,
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degree: map_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegree=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ..., default
        value null, specify the degree of a map
      * *note DegreeLift: map_lpRing_cmRing_cmMatrix_rp, => ..., default value
        null, make a ring map
      * *note DegreeMap: map_lpRing_cmRing_cmMatrix_rp, => ..., default value
        null, make a ring map
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: Matrix,, A matrix M <-- N whose entries are obtained
        from v

Description
===========

The list v is either a doubly nested list of ring elements, or a list of
elements (i,j) => f.  The first version provides all of the elements of the
output matrix, row by row.  The second form provides only the non-zero elements
of the output matrix h: h_(i,j) = f, for every (i,j) => f in the list v.


In each case, the modules M and N should have the same base ring R, and the
ring elements appearing in v should be over R, or over a base ring of R.


In the first form, each list in v gives a row of the matrix. The length of the
list v should be the number of generators of M, and the length of each element
of v (which is itself a list of ring elements) should be the number of
generators of the source module N.
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/101[x,y,z];                            |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : p = map(R^2,R^{-2,-2,0},{{x^2,0,3},{0,y^2,5}})|
|                                                   |
|o2 = | x2 0  3 |                                   |
|     | 0  y2 5 |                                   |
|                                                   |
|             2      3                              |
|o2 : Matrix R  <-- R                               |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : isHomogeneous p                               |
|                                                   |
|o3 = true                                          |
+---------------------------------------------------+
In the second form, if an index (i,j) occurs more than once, only the last is
taken.
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : p = map(R^2,R^3,{(0,0) => x+y, (1,1) => x^2, (0,2) => x-1, (0,0) =>     |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = | x-y 0  x-1 |                                                          |
|     | 0   x2 0   |                                                          |
|                                                                             |
|             2      3                                                        |
|o4 : Matrix R  <-- R                                                         |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|x-y})                                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note matrix: matrix, -- make a matrix
  * *note map(Module,Nothing,List): map_lpModule_cmNothing_cmList_rp, -- create
    a matrix by giving a doubly nested list of ring elements
  * *note inputting a matrix: inputting a matrix,

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note map(Module,Module,List): map_lpModule_cmModule_cmList_rp, -- create a
    matrix by giving a sparse or dense list of entries
