map(Matrix) -- make a matrix with a different degree
****************************************************

  * Function: *note map: map,
  * Usage: 
        map(f, Degree => d)
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note matrix: Matrix,
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degree: map_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegree=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ..., default
        value null, specify the degree of a map
      * *note DegreeLift: map_lpRing_cmRing_cmMatrix_rp, => ..., default value
        null, make a ring map
      * *note DegreeMap: map_lpRing_cmRing_cmMatrix_rp, => ..., default value
        null, make a ring map
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: Matrix,, a map identical to f, except that it has
        degree d, and the source module has been tensored by a graded free
        module of rank 1 of the appropriate degree.

Description
===========

The input d should be an *note integer: ZZ,, or a list of integers.


This routine is often used to take a matrix that has a non-zero degree, and
make the degree zero.


For example, multiplication of a matrix by a scalar increases the degree,
leaving the source and target fixed:
+--------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a,b];         |
+--------------------------+
|i2 : f1 = matrix{{a,b}}   |
|                          |
|o2 = | a b |              |
|                          |
|             1      2     |
|o2 : Matrix R  <-- R      |
+--------------------------+
|i3 : f = a * f1           |
|                          |
|o3 = | a2 ab |            |
|                          |
|             1      2     |
|o3 : Matrix R  <-- R      |
+--------------------------+
|i4 : degree f             |
|                          |
|o4 = {1}                  |
|                          |
|o4 : List                 |
+--------------------------+
|i5 : source f == source f1|
|                          |
|o5 = true                 |
+--------------------------+
One solution is to change the degree:
+-------------------------------------+
|i6 : g = map(f, Degree => 0)         |
|                                     |
|o6 = | a2 ab |                       |
|                                     |
|             1      2                |
|o6 : Matrix R  <-- R                 |
+-------------------------------------+
|i7 : degree g                        |
|                                     |
|o7 = {0}                             |
|                                     |
|o7 : List                            |
+-------------------------------------+
|i8 : source g == (source f) ** R^{-1}|
|                                     |
|o8 = true                            |
+-------------------------------------+
An alternate solution would be to use tensor product with the scalar.
+----------------------------+
|i9 : g2 = a ** matrix{{a,b}}|
|                            |
|o9 = | a2 ab |              |
|                            |
|             1      2       |
|o9 : Matrix R  <-- R        |
+----------------------------+
|i10 : degree g2             |
|                            |
|o10 = {0}                   |
|                            |
|o10 : List                  |
+----------------------------+
|i11 : isHomogeneous g2      |
|                            |
|o11 = true                  |
+----------------------------+
* Menu:

* map(...,Degree=>...): map_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegree=>_pd_pd_pd_rp.  specify the degree of a map

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note map(Matrix): map_lpMatrix_rp, -- make a matrix with a different
    degree
