map(Ring,Ring,List) -- make a ring map
**************************************

  * Function: *note map: map,
  * Usage: 
        map(R,S,m)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: Ring,, the target ring
      * S, a *note ring: Ring,, the source ring
      * m, a *note list: List,, of n elements of R, where n is the number of
        variables in the polynomial ring S, or a list of pairs x => r, where x
        is a generator of S and r is an element of R, specifying that x is to
        be sent to r.
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degree: map_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegree=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ..., default
        value null, specify the degree of a map
      * *note DegreeLift: map_lpRing_cmRing_cmMatrix_rp, => ..., default value
        null, make a ring map
      * *note DegreeMap: map_lpRing_cmRing_cmMatrix_rp, => ..., default value
        null, make a ring map
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring map: RingMap,, the ring homomorphism from S to R which
        sends the i-th variable of S to the i-th entry in m, or in the second
        case, performs the indicated substitutions.

Description
===========

+----------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ[x,y];                       |
+----------------------------------------+
|i2 : S = ZZ[a,b,c];                     |
+----------------------------------------+
|i3 : f = map(R,S,{x^2,x*y,y^2})         |
|                                        |
|                  2        2            |
|o3 = map (R, S, {x , x*y, y })          |
|                                        |
|o3 : RingMap R <-- S                    |
+----------------------------------------+
|i4 : f(a+b+c^2)                         |
|                                        |
|      4    2                            |
|o4 = y  + x  + x*y                      |
|                                        |
|o4 : R                                  |
+----------------------------------------+
|i5 : g = map(R,S,{a=>x^2,b=>x*y,c=>y^2})|
|                                        |
|                  2        2            |
|o5 = map (R, S, {x , x*y, y })          |
|                                        |
|o5 : RingMap R <-- S                    |
+----------------------------------------+
|i6 : g(a+b+c^2)                         |
|                                        |
|      4    2                            |
|o6 = y  + x  + x*y                      |
|                                        |
|o6 : R                                  |
+----------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note substitution and maps between rings: substitution and maps between
    rings,
  * *note map(Ring,Ring,Matrix): map_lpRing_cmRing_cmMatrix_rp, -- make a ring
    map

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note map(Ring,Ring,List): map_lpRing_cmRing_cmList_rp, -- make a ring map
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/functions/map-doc.m2:467:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: map_lpRing_cmRing_cmMatrix_rp, Next: Hom, Prev: map_lpRing_cmRing_cmList_rp, Up: map

map(Ring,Ring,Matrix) -- make a ring map
****************************************

  * Function: *note map: map,
  * Usage: 
        map(R,S,m)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: Ring,, the target ring
      * S, a *note ring: Ring,, the source ring
      * m, a *note matrix: Matrix,, a 1 by n matrix over R, where n is the
        number of variables in the polynomial ring S, or a matrix over the
        common coefficient ring of the two rings.  If a ring map is used here,
        (just) its matrix will be used instead.
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * DegreeMap => a *note function: Function,, default value null, the
        degree map: a (linear) function from the multidegrees of S to the
        multidegrees of R, to be used in determining homogeneity and in
        determining degrees in tensor products. If the two rings have the same
        degree length, then the default degree map is the identity function.
      * DegreeLift => a *note function: Function,, default value null, the
        degree lift function: a (partial) inverse of the degree map, giving an
        error when lifting is not possible.  If the degree map is the identity,
        then by default the identity map will be provided.  If the degree
        length of R is 0, then by default a suitable degree lift function will
        be provided.
      * *note Degree: map_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegree=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ..., default
        value null, specify the degree of a map
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring map: RingMap,, the ring homomorphism from S to R which, in
        case m is a matrix over R, sends the i-th variable of S to the i-th
        entry in m, or, in case m is a matrix over the common coefficient ring,
        is the linear change of coordinates corresponding to m

Description
===========

+-------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ[x,y];                          |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S = ZZ[a,b,c];                        |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i3 : f = map(R,S,matrix {{x^2,x*y,y^2}})   |
|                                           |
|                  2        2               |
|o3 = map (R, S, {x , x*y, y })             |
|                                           |
|o3 : RingMap R <-- S                       |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i4 : f(a+b+c^2)                            |
|                                           |
|      4    2                               |
|o4 = y  + x  + x*y                         |
|                                           |
|o4 : R                                     |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i5 : g = map(R,S,matrix {{1,2,3},{4,5,6}}) |
|                                           |
|o5 = map (R, S, {x + 4y, 2x + 5y, 3x + 6y})|
|                                           |
|o5 : RingMap R <-- S                       |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i6 : g(a+b)                                |
|                                           |
|o6 = 3x + 9y                               |
|                                           |
|o6 : R                                     |
+-------------------------------------------+
If the coefficient ring of S is itself a polynomial ring, then one may
optionally include values to which its variables should be sent: they should
appear last in the matrix m.
+------------------------------------+
|i7 : S = ZZ[a][b,c];                |
+------------------------------------+
|i8 : h = map(S,S,matrix {{b,c,2*a}})|
|                                    |
|o8 = map (S, S, {b, c, 2a})         |
|                                    |
|o8 : RingMap S <-- S                |
+------------------------------------+
|i9 : h(a^7 + b^3 + c)               |
|                                    |
|      3           7                 |
|o9 = b  + c + 128a                  |
|                                    |
|o9 : S                              |
+------------------------------------+
|i10 : k = map(S,S,matrix {{c,b}})   |
|                                    |
|o10 = map (S, S, {c, b, a})         |
|                                    |
|o10 : RingMap S <-- S               |
+------------------------------------+
|i11 : k(a^7 + b^3 + c)              |
|                                    |
|       3        7                   |
|o11 = c  + b + a                    |
|                                    |
|o11 : S                             |
+------------------------------------+
Specifying a degree map is a useful way to preserve homogeneity, which can
speed computation.
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : R = QQ[x,y,z];                                  |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : S = QQ[t,u];                                    |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : f = map(S,R,{t^2,t*u,u^2},DegreeMap => i -> 2*i)|
|                                                      |
|                   2        2                         |
|o14 = map (S, R, {t , t*u, u })                       |
|                                                      |
|o14 : RingMap S <-- R                                 |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i15 : isHomogeneous f                                 |
|                                                      |
|o15 = true                                            |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i16 : M = R^{1,2}                                     |
|                                                      |
|       2                                              |
|o16 = R                                               |
|                                                      |
|o16 : R-module, free, degrees {-1, -2}                |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i17 : f M                                             |
|                                                      |
|       2                                              |
|o17 = S                                               |
|                                                      |
|o17 : S-module, free, degrees {-2, -4}                |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i18 : f ** M                                          |
|                                                      |
|       2                                              |
|o18 = S                                               |
|                                                      |
|o18 : S-module, free, degrees {-2, -4}                |
+------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note substitution and maps between rings: substitution and maps between
    rings,

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note map(Ring,Ring,Matrix): map_lpRing_cmRing_cmMatrix_rp, -- make a ring
    map
  * "map(Ring,Ring,RingMap)"
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

