Singular Book 1.8.9 -- radical membership
*****************************************

Recall that an element $f$ is in an ideal $I$ if $1 \in (I, tf-1) \subset
R[t]$.
+---------------------------------+
|i1 : A = QQ[x,y,z];              |
+---------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal"x5,xy3,y7,z3+xyz";|
|                                 |
|o2 : Ideal of A                  |
+---------------------------------+
|i3 : f = x+y+z;                  |
+---------------------------------+
+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : B = A[t];                                         |
+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : J = substitute(I,B) + ideal(f*t-1)                |
|                                                       |
|             5     3   7           3                   |
|o5 = ideal (x , x*y , y , x*y*z + z , (x + y + z)t - 1)|
|                                                       |
|o5 : Ideal of B                                        |
+-------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : 1 % J                                             |
|                                                       |
|o6 = 0                                                 |
|                                                       |
|o6 : B                                                 |
+-------------------------------------------------------+
The polynomial f is in the radical. Let's compute the radical to make sure.
+--------------------+
|i7 : radical I      |
|                    |
|o7 = ideal (z, y, x)|
|                    |
|o7 : Ideal of A     |
+--------------------+
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/M2-Singular-Book.m2:603:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd11, Next: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd13, Prev: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd9, Up: M2SingularBook

Singular Book 1.8.11 -- intersection of ideals
**********************************************

Intersecting ideals using the Macaulay2 *note intersect: intersect, function.
+-------------------------+
|i1 : A = QQ[x,y,z];      |
+-------------------------+
|i2 : I1 = ideal(x,y);    |
|                         |
|o2 : Ideal of A          |
+-------------------------+
|i3 : I2 = ideal(y^2,z);  |
|                         |
|o3 : Ideal of A          |
+-------------------------+
|i4 : intersect(I1,I2)    |
|                         |
|                       2 |
|o4 = ideal (y*z, x*z, y )|
|                         |
|o4 : Ideal of A          |
+-------------------------+
Now we use the method described in the Singular book in section 1.8.7.
+---------------------------------------------+
|i5 : B = QQ[t,x,y,z];                        |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i6 : I1 = substitute(I1,B);                  |
|                                             |
|o6 : Ideal of B                              |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i7 : I2 = substitute(I2,B);                  |
|                                             |
|o7 : Ideal of B                              |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i8 : J = t*I1 + (1-t)*I2                     |
|                                             |
|                           2    2            |
|o8 = ideal (t*x, t*y, - t*y  + y , - t*z + z)|
|                                             |
|o8 : Ideal of B                              |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i9 : eliminate(J,t)                          |
|                                             |
|                       2                     |
|o9 = ideal (y*z, x*z, y )                    |
|                                             |
|o9 : Ideal of B                              |
+---------------------------------------------+
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/M2-Singular-Book.m2:623:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd13, Next: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd15, Prev: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd11, Up: M2SingularBook

Singular Book 1.8.13 -- quotient of ideals
******************************************

+-----------------------+
|i1 : A = QQ[x,y,z];    |
+-----------------------+
|i2 : I1 = ideal(x,y);  |
|                       |
|o2 : Ideal of A        |
+-----------------------+
|i3 : I2 = ideal(y^2,z);|
|                       |
|o3 : Ideal of A        |
+-----------------------+
|i4 : I1 : I2           |
|                       |
|o4 = ideal (y, x)      |
|                       |
|o4 : Ideal of A        |
+-----------------------+
The quotient function is the same as the colon operator, except that optional
arguments may be given.
+--------------------+
|i5 : quotient(I1,I2)|
|                    |
|o5 = ideal (y, x)   |
|                    |
|o5 : Ideal of A     |
+--------------------+
Now we use the method described in Lemma 1.8.12 in the Singular book.
+-----------------------------------+
|i6 : J1 = intersect(I1,ideal(I2_0))|
|                                   |
|            2                      |
|o6 = ideal y                       |
|                                   |
|o6 : Ideal of A                    |
+-----------------------------------+
|i7 : J2 = intersect(I1,ideal(I2_1))|
|                                   |
|o7 = ideal (y*z, x*z)              |
|                                   |
|o7 : Ideal of A                    |
+-----------------------------------+
Now divide each generator of J1 by x, and each generator of J2 by y.  Notice
that division uses two slashes.  Using only one slash gives the quotient in the
fraction ring.
+---------------------------------------+
|i8 : K1 = ideal(J1_0//I2_0)            |
|                                       |
|o8 = ideal 1                           |
|                                       |
|o8 : Ideal of A                        |
+---------------------------------------+
|i9 : K2 = ideal(J2_0//I2_1, J2_1//I2_1)|
|                                       |
|o9 = ideal (y, x)                      |
|                                       |
|o9 : Ideal of A                        |
+---------------------------------------+
|i10 : intersect(K1,K2)                 |
|                                       |
|o10 = ideal (y, x)                     |
|                                       |
|o10 : Ideal of A                       |
+---------------------------------------+
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/M2-Singular-Book.m2:651:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd15, Next: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd18, Prev: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd13, Up: M2SingularBook

Singular Book 1.8.15 -- saturation
**********************************

+---------------------------------------+
|i1 : A = QQ[x,y,z];                    |
+---------------------------------------+
|i2 : I1 = ideal(x^5*z^3, x*y*z, y*z^4);|
|                                       |
|o2 : Ideal of A                        |
+---------------------------------------+
|i3 : saturate(I1,z)                    |
|                                       |
|                5                      |
|o3 = ideal (y, x )                     |
|                                       |
|o3 : Ideal of A                        |
+---------------------------------------+
Now we compute the saturation using a loop.
+----------------------------------+
|i4 : J = I1:z                     |
|                                  |
|                    3   5 2       |
|o4 = ideal (x*y, y*z , x z )      |
|                                  |
|o4 : Ideal of A                   |
+----------------------------------+
|i5 : k = 0;                       |
+----------------------------------+
|i6 : while not isSubset(J,I1) do (|
|        k = k+1;                  |
|        I1 = J;                   |
|        J = I1 : z;               |
|        );                        |
+----------------------------------+
|i7 : J                            |
|                                  |
|                5                 |
|o7 = ideal (y, x )                |
|                                  |
|o7 : Ideal of A                   |
+----------------------------------+
|i8 : k                            |
|                                  |
|o8 = 4                            |
+----------------------------------+
We needed to use quotient four times.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in Macaulay2Doc/M2-Singular-Book.m2:674:0.

File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd18, Next: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd19, Prev: Singular Book 1_pd8_pd15, Up: M2SingularBook

Singular Book 1.8.18 -- kernel of a ring map
********************************************

First, we use Macaulay2's *note kernel(RingMap): kernel_lpRingMap_rp, function.
+---------------------------------+
|i1 : A = QQ[x,y,z];              |
+---------------------------------+
|i2 : B = QQ[a,b];                |
+---------------------------------+
|i3 : phi = map(B,A,{a^2,a*b,b^2})|
|                                 |
|                  2        2     |
|o3 = map (B, A, {a , a*b, b })   |
|                                 |
|o3 : RingMap B <-- A             |
+---------------------------------+
|i4 : kernel phi                  |
|                                 |
|            2                    |
|o4 = ideal(y  - x*z)             |
|                                 |
|o4 : Ideal of A                  |
+---------------------------------+
Now use the elimination of variables method.
+------------------------------------+
|i5 : C = QQ[x,y,z,a,b]              |
|                                    |
|o5 = C                              |
|                                    |
|o5 : PolynomialRing                 |
+------------------------------------+
|i6 : H = ideal(x-a^2, y-a*b, z-b^2);|
|                                    |
|o6 : Ideal of C                     |
+------------------------------------+
|i7 : eliminate(H, {a,b})            |
|                                    |
|            2                       |
|o7 = ideal(y  - x*z)                |
|                                    |
|o7 : Ideal of C                     |
+------------------------------------+
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

