Singular Book 2.1.26 -- computation of Hom
******************************************

+------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : A = QQ[x,y,z];                                   |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : M = cokernel matrix(A, {{1,2,3},{4,5,6},{7,8,9}})|
|                                                      |
|o2 = cokernel | 1 2 3 |                               |
|              | 4 5 6 |                               |
|              | 7 8 9 |                               |
|                                                      |
|                            3                         |
|o2 : A-module, quotient of A                          |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : N = cokernel matrix{{x,y},{z,0}}                 |
|                                                      |
|o3 = cokernel | x y |                                 |
|              | z 0 |                                 |
|                                                      |
|                            2                         |
|o3 : A-module, quotient of A                          |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : H = Hom(M,N)                                     |
|                                                      |
|o4 = subquotient (| 1  0  |, | y x 0 0 0 0 |)         |
|                  | 0  1  |  | 0 z 0 0 0 0 |          |
|                  | -2 0  |  | 0 0 y x 0 0 |          |
|                  | 0  -2 |  | 0 0 0 z 0 0 |          |
|                  | 1  0  |  | 0 0 0 0 y x |          |
|                  | 0  1  |  | 0 0 0 0 0 z |          |
|                                                      |
|                               6                      |
|o4 : A-module, subquotient of A                       |
+------------------------------------------------------+
H is a subquotient module.  In Macaulay2, the most general form of a module is
as a subquotient: a submodule of a cokernel module.  For more about subquotient
modules, see *note modules: modules,.
+---------------------------+
|i5 : f = homomorphism H_{0}|
|                           |
|o5 = | 1 -2 1 |            |
|     | 0 0  0 |            |
|                           |
|o5 : Matrix N <-- M        |
+---------------------------+
|i6 : target f === N        |
|                           |
|o6 = true                  |
+---------------------------+
|i7 : source f === M        |
|                           |
|o7 = true                  |
+---------------------------+
|i8 : matrix f              |
|                           |
|o8 = | 1 -2 1 |            |
|     | 0 0  0 |            |
|                           |
|             2      3      |
|o8 : Matrix A  <-- A       |
+---------------------------+
Macaulay2 has a modulo command (it was initially introduced in the original
Macaulay, in the late 1980's), but it is not needed very often.  It is used
internally in Macaulay2 to implement kernels of module homomorphisms.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

