The answer is 4.  In fact, the ideal $I$ represents the union of two conics.

C. Surfaces of degree $4$ in $\PP^5$

We now turn to the three surfaces of degree $4$ in $\PP^5$, and make their
ideals:


The ring of $\PP^5$

+--------------------+
|i33 : KK = ZZ/31991 |
|                    |
|o33 = KK            |
|                    |
|o33 : QuotientRing  |
+--------------------+
|i34 : P5 = KK[a..f] |
|                    |
|o34 = P5            |
|                    |
|o34 : PolynomialRing|
+--------------------+

It happens that the ideals of all three surfaces are generated by minors of
suitable matrices:


The Veronese embedding of $\PP^2$:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i35 : MVero = genericSymmetricMatrix(P5,a,3)                                 |
|                                                                             |
|o35 = | a b c |                                                              |
|      | b d e |                                                              |
|      | c e f |                                                              |
|                                                                             |
|               3       3                                                     |
|o35 : Matrix P5  <-- P5                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i36 : Vero = minors(2,MVero)                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|                2                                                  2         |
|o36 = ideal (- b  + a*d, - b*c + a*e, - c*d + b*e, - b*c + a*e, - c  + a*f, -|
|      -----------------------------------------------------------------------|
|                                              2                              |
|      c*e + b*f, - c*d + b*e, - c*e + b*f, - e  + d*f)                       |
|                                                                             |
|o36 : Ideal of P5                                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The other scrolls are defined by the minors of matrices that are made from
``catalecticant'' blocks, that is, from matrices such as $$\begin{pmatrix}b & c
& d & e \\ c & d & e & f \end{pmatrix}$$ which are manufactured by

