random(ZZ,Ideal) -- get a random homogeneous element from a graded ideal
************************************************************************

  * Function: *note random: random,
  * Usage: 
        random(d, I)
        random(L, I)
  * Inputs:
      * d, an *note integer: ZZ, or a *note list: List,, the degree,
        multi-degree, or a list of degrees;
      * I, an *note ideal: Ideal,, in a graded ring; 
  * *note Optional inputs: using functions with optional inputs,:
      * CoefficientRing (missing documentation) => ..., default value null, 
      * *note Density: random_lpModule_cmModule_rp, => ..., default value 1,
        get a random map of module
      * *note Height: random_lpZZ_cmRing_rp, => ..., default value 10, get a
        random homogeneous element from a graded ring
      * *note MaximalRank: random_lpModule_cmModule_rp, => ..., default value
        false, get a random map of module
      * *note UpperTriangular: random_lpModule_cmModule_rp, => ..., default
        value false, get a random map of module
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring element: RingElement, or a *note list: List,, homogeneous
        element(s) with prescribed degrees;

Description
===========

This function produces one or more homogeneous elements of an ideal.

+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : S = ZZ/101[x, y]                                      |
|                                                           |
|o1 = S                                                     |
|                                                           |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal"x2, y2"                                     |
|                                                           |
|             2   2                                         |
|o2 = ideal (x , y )                                        |
|                                                           |
|o2 : Ideal of S                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : random(2, I)                                          |
|                                                           |
|        2      2                                           |
|o3 = 24x  - 36y                                            |
|                                                           |
|o3 : S                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : random({2}, I)                                        |
|                                                           |
|          2      2                                         |
|o4 = - 30x  - 29y                                          |
|                                                           |
|o4 : S                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : random({2, 3}, I)                                     |
|                                                           |
|         2      2       3     2         2      3           |
|o5 = {19x  + 19y , - 10x  - 8x y - 29x*y  - 22y }          |
|                                                           |
|o5 : List                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : random({{2}, {3}}, I)                                 |
|                                                           |
|           2      2       3      2         2      3        |
|o6 = {- 29x  - 24y , - 38x  + 39x y - 16x*y  + 21y }       |
|                                                           |
|o6 : List                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : R = ZZ/101[x, y, z, Degrees => {{1,0}, {-1,1}, {0,1}}]|
|                                                           |
|o7 = R                                                     |
|                                                           |
|o7 : PolynomialRing                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : J = ideal"x2, y2, z2"                                 |
|                                                           |
|             2   2   2                                     |
|o8 = ideal (x , y , z )                                    |
|                                                           |
|o8 : Ideal of R                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : random({2, 2}, J)                                     |
|                                                           |
|         4 2      3         2 2                            |
|o9 = - 5x y  + 19x y*z + 36x z                             |
|                                                           |
|o9 : R                                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : random({{2, 2}}, J)                                  |
|                                                           |
|            4 2      3         2 2                         |
|o10 = {- 41x y  - 43x y*z + 39x z }                        |
|                                                           |
|o10 : List                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : random(toList(3:{1, 1}), J)                          |
|                                                           |
|          2     2      2                                   |
|o11 = {38x y, 2x y, 16x y}                                 |
|                                                           |
|o11 : List                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Note that in the single graded case, the input {d} is treated as a multidegree
of length one rather than a list of length one, hence the output is one
polynomial, while {{d}} is treated as a list containing a single multidegree of
length one and therefore the output is a list containing a single polynomial.

