File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: randomSubset, Prev: setRandomSeed, Up: random

randomSubset
************

  * Usage: 
        randomSubset(x, n)
        randomSubset x
  * Inputs:
      * x, a *note visible list: VisibleList,, a *note set: Set,, or an *note
        integer: ZZ,,
      * n, an *note integer: ZZ,, 

Description
===========

When n is given, then a random subset x of cardinality n is returned.

+--------------------------------------+
|i1 : randomSubset({2, 3, 5, 7, 11}, 2)|
|                                      |
|o1 = {3, 5}                           |
|                                      |
|o1 : List                             |
+--------------------------------------+

Otherwise, a random subset of arbitrary cardinality is returned.

+----------------------------------+
|i2 : randomSubset {2, 3, 5, 7, 11}|
|                                  |
|o2 = {7}                          |
|                                  |
|o2 : List                         |
+----------------------------------+
|i3 : randomSubset {2, 3, 5, 7, 11}|
|                                  |
|o3 = {2, 5, 7, 11}                |
|                                  |
|o3 : List                         |
+----------------------------------+
|i4 : randomSubset {2, 3, 5, 7, 11}|
|                                  |
|o4 = {2, 3, 5, 7}                 |
|                                  |
|o4 : List                         |
+----------------------------------+

If x is an integer, then a subset of $\{0,\ldots,x - 1\}$ is returned.

+-----------------------+
|i5 : randomSubset(4, 2)|
|                       |
|o5 = {0, 1}            |
|                       |
|o5 : List              |
+-----------------------+
|i6 : randomSubset 5    |
|                       |
|o6 = {1}               |
|                       |
|o6 : List              |
+-----------------------+

The order of elements in x is preserved.  For an arbitrary order, use *note
shuffle(List,ZZ): shuffle, instead.

References
==========

Knuth, Donald E. The Art of Computer Programming: Seminumerical Algorithms,
Volume 2. Addison-Wesley Professional, 2014. (Algorithm S, Section 3.4.2)

See also
========

  * *note random: random, -- get a random object
  * *note shuffle: shuffle, -- shuffle a list randomly

Ways to use randomSubset:
=========================

  * "randomSubset(Set)"
  * "randomSubset(Set,ZZ)"
  * "randomSubset(VisibleList)"
  * "randomSubset(VisibleList,ZZ)"
  * "randomSubset(ZZ)"
  * "randomSubset(ZZ,ZZ)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note randomSubset: randomSubset, is a *note method function:
MethodFunction,.

