File: Macaulay2Doc.info, Node: Set, Next: rays, Prev: shuffle, Up: combinatorics

Set -- the class of all sets
****************************

Description
===========

Elements of sets may be any immutable object, such as integers, ring elements
and lists of such.  Ideals may also be elements of sets.
+-------------------------+
|i1 : A = set {1,2};      |
+-------------------------+
|i2 : R = QQ[a..d];       |
+-------------------------+
|i3 : B = set{a^2-b*c,b*d}|
|                         |
|                2        |
|o3 = set {b*d, a  - b*c} |
|                         |
|o3 : Set                 |
+-------------------------+
Set operations, such as *note membership: isMember,, *note union:
union_lpSet_cmSet_rp,, *note intersection: intersect_lpSet_cmSet_rp,, *note
difference: Set - Set,, *note Cartesian product: Set _st_st Set,, *note
Cartesian power: VirtualTally ^_st_st ZZ,, and *note subset:
isSubset_lpSet_cmSet_rp, are available. For example,
+-----------------------------------------+
|i4 : toList B                            |
|                                         |
|            2                            |
|o4 = {b*d, a  - b*c}                     |
|                                         |
|o4 : List                                |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i5 : member(1,A)                         |
|                                         |
|o5 = true                                |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i6 : member(-b*c+a^2,B)                  |
|                                         |
|o6 = true                                |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i7 : A ** A                              |
|                                         |
|o7 = set {(1, 1), (1, 2), (2, 1), (2, 2)}|
|                                         |
|o7 : Set                                 |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i8 : A^**2                               |
|                                         |
|o8 = set {(1, 1), (1, 2), (2, 1), (2, 2)}|
|                                         |
|o8 : Set                                 |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i9 : set{1,3,2} - set{1}                 |
|                                         |
|o9 = set {2, 3}                          |
|                                         |
|o9 : Set                                 |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i10 : set{4,5} + set{5,6}                |
|                                         |
|o10 = set {4, 5, 6}                      |
|                                         |
|o10 : Set                                |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i11 : set{4,5} * set{5,6}                |
|                                         |
|o11 = set {5}                            |
|                                         |
|o11 : Set                                |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i12 : set{1,3,2} === set{1,2,3}          |
|                                         |
|o12 = true                               |
+-----------------------------------------+


*note Ideals: Ideal, in Macaulay2 come equipped with a specific sequence of
generators, so the following two ideals are not considered strictly equal, and
thus the set containing them will appear to have two elements.
+--------------------------------------+
|i13 : I = ideal(a,b); J = ideal(b,a); |
|                                      |
|o13 : Ideal of R                      |
|                                      |
|o14 : Ideal of R                      |
+--------------------------------------+
|i15 : I == J                          |
|                                      |
|o15 = true                            |
+--------------------------------------+
|i16 : I === J                         |
|                                      |
|o16 = false                           |
+--------------------------------------+
|i17 : C = set(ideal(a,b),ideal(b,a))  |
|                                      |
|o17 = set {ideal (b, a), ideal (a, b)}|
|                                      |
|o17 : Set                             |
+--------------------------------------+
However, if you *note trim: trim, the ideals, then the generating sets will be
the same, and so the set containing them will have one element.
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i18 : C1 = set(trim ideal(a,b),trim ideal(b,a))|
|                                               |
|o18 = set {ideal (b, a)}                       |
|                                               |
|o18 : Set                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------+


A set is implemented as a *note HashTable: HashTable,, whose keys are the
elements of the set, and whose values are all 1.  In particular, this means
that two objects are considered the same exactly when they are strictly equal,
according to *note ===: ===,.
* Menu:

* set::                         make a set
* elements::                    list of elements
* union::                       compute the union
* intersect::                   compute an intersection
* Set #? Thing::                test set membership
* Set - Set::                   set difference
* union(Set,Set): union_lpSet_cmSet_rp.  set union
* intersect(Set,Set): intersect_lpSet_cmSet_rp.  set intersection
* Set ** Set: Set _st_st Set.   Cartesian product
* sum(Set): sum_lpSet_rp.       sum of elements
* product(Set): product_lpSet_rp.  product of elements
* select(Set,Function): select_lpSet_cmFunction_rp.  select a part of a set
* isSubset(Set,Set): isSubset_lpSet_cmSet_rp.  whether one object is a subset of another

Functions and methods returning a set:
======================================

  * intersect(Set) (missing documentation)
  * *note intersect(Set,Set): intersect_lpSet_cmSet_rp, -- set intersection
  * "Set * Set" -- see *note intersect(Set,Set): intersect_lpSet_cmSet_rp, --
    set intersection
  * *note select(Set,Function): select_lpSet_cmFunction_rp, -- select a part of
    a set
  * "select(ZZ,Set,Function)" -- see *note select(Set,Function):
    select_lpSet_cmFunction_rp, -- select a part of a set
  * *note set: set, -- make a set
  * "set(HashTable)" -- see *note set: set, -- make a set
  * "set(VisibleList)" -- see *note set: set, -- make a set
  * *note Set ** Set: Set _st_st Set, -- Cartesian product
  * *note Set - Set: Set - Set, -- set difference
  * "Set - VisibleList" -- see *note Set - Set: Set - Set, -- set difference
  * "Set + Set" -- see *note union(Set,Set): union_lpSet_cmSet_rp, -- set union
  * *note union(Set,Set): union_lpSet_cmSet_rp, -- set union
  * /// Command \ Set /// -- see *note VisibleList / Function: VisibleList /
    Function, -- apply a function to elements of a list
  * /// Function \ Set /// -- see *note VisibleList / Function: VisibleList /
    Function, -- apply a function to elements of a list
  * "Set / Command" -- see *note VisibleList / Function: VisibleList /
    Function, -- apply a function to elements of a list
  * "Set / Function" -- see *note VisibleList / Function: VisibleList /
    Function, -- apply a function to elements of a list

Methods that use a set:
=======================

  * "# Set" -- see *note # List: # List, -- length or cardinality of a list,
    hash table, dictionary, set, or string
  * "commonest(Set)" -- see *note commonest: commonest, -- the most common
    elements of a list or tally
  * "elements(Set)" -- see *note elements: elements, -- list of elements
  * "isMember(Thing,Set)" -- see *note isMember: isMember, -- test membership
    in a list or set
  * *note isSubset(Set,Set): isSubset_lpSet_cmSet_rp, -- whether one object is
    a subset of another
  * "isSubset(Set,VisibleList)" -- see *note isSubset(Set,Set):
    isSubset_lpSet_cmSet_rp, -- whether one object is a subset of another
  * "isSubset(VisibleList,Set)" -- see *note isSubset(Set,Set):
    isSubset_lpSet_cmSet_rp, -- whether one object is a subset of another
  * partitions(Set,BasicList) (missing documentation)
  * *note product(Set): product_lpSet_rp, -- product of elements
  * "randomSubset(Set)" -- see *note randomSubset: randomSubset,
  * "randomSubset(Set,ZZ)" -- see *note randomSubset: randomSubset,
  * "set(Set)" -- see *note set: set, -- make a set
  * *note Set #? Thing: Set #? Thing, -- test set membership
  * "VisibleList - Set" -- see *note Set - Set: Set - Set, -- set difference
  * "subsets(Set)" -- see *note subsets: subsets, -- produce the subsets of a
    set or list
  * "subsets(Set,ZZ)" -- see *note subsets: subsets, -- produce the subsets of
    a set or list
  * *note sum(Set): sum_lpSet_rp, -- sum of elements
  * "toList(Set)" -- see *note toList: toList, -- create a list
  * union(Set) (missing documentation)

For the programmer
==================

The object *note Set: Set, is a *note type: Type,, with ancestor classes *note
