GC garbage collector
********************

Macaulay2 uses the excellent garbage collector GC, version 8.2.6, written by
Hans-J. Boehm and Alan J. Demers and generously licensed to the public.  It is
available at https://www.hboehm.info/gc/.

Some environment variables can be set by the user to tune garbage collector
performance:

  * GC_INITIAL_HEAP_SIZE -- initial heap size in bytes, or number of gigabytes
    followed by 'G', similarly for 'M', 'K'
  * GC_MAXIMUM_HEAP_SIZE -- optional maximum collected heap size
  * GC_FREE_SPACE_DIVISOR -- if set to a number D, then we try to make sure
    that we allocate at least N/D bytes between collections, where N is twice
    the number of traced bytes, plus the number of untraced bytes, plus a rough
    estimate of the root set size.  Increasing its value will use less space
    but more collection time.  Decreasing it will appreciably decrease
    collection time at the expense of space.
  * GC_PRINT_STATS -- whether to turn on logging
  * GC_PRINT_VERBOSE_STATS -- whether to turn on even more logging
  * GC_DUMP_REGULARLY -- whether to generate a debugging dump on startup and
    during every collection; very verbose
  * GC_NPROCS -- the number of processors to use (for Linux only)
  * GC_MARKERS -- the number of marker threads.  This is normally set to the
    number of processors.  It is safer to adjust GC_MARKERS than GC_NPROCS,
    since GC_MARKERS has no impact on the lock implementation

The full list is found in the source code for gc in the file
doc/README.environment.

Here are some error messages you may see from it when it aborts the program,
due to lack of memory or related problems.

  * Insufficient space for initial table allocation
  * No space for lwp data structures
  * Out of memory
  * Too many exclusions
  * Too many heap sections
  * Too many heap sections: Increase MAXHINCR or MAX_HEAP_SECTS
  * Too many root sets

You may simply be out of memory, and then the only recourse is to increase the
memory available to the program. However, if you don't have enough heap
sections, sometimes one can just start Macaulay2 by setting the
GC_INITIAL_HEAP_SIZE environment variable to some larger value with an
environment setting prefix on the M2 command line, e.g.,
GC_INITIAL_HEAP_SIZE=20G M2.

See also
========

  * *note collectGarbage: collectGarbage, -- collect the garbage in memory
  * *note GCstats: GCstats, -- information about the status of the garbage
    collector
